{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01M1Dx5tPvzV1WjMGGyAmdKj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've gathered enough information about CoRL reviewing. Let me now compile what I know:\n\nFrom the CoRL 2023 guidelines, the six evaluation dimensions are:\n1. Originality\n2. Quality\n3. Clarity\n4. Significance\n5. Relevance (to robot learning)\n6. Limitations\n\nFrom the OpenReview default form and CoRL practices, the typical scale is 1-10:\n- 10: Top 5% of accepted papers, seminal paper\n\n\n- 9: Top 15% of accepted papers, strong accept\n- 8: Top 50% of accepted papers, clear accept\n- 7: Good paper, accept\n- 6: Marginally above acceptance threshold\n- 5: Marginally below acceptance threshold\n- 4: Ok but not good enough, reject\n- 3: Clear reject\n- 2: Strong reject\n- 1: Trivial or wrong\n\nCoRL 2024 had a 6.6% oral acceptance rate with an overall acceptance rate around 30-35%. Now I'm ready to proceed with the full dream-review and dispatch the reviewers.\n\nI'm setting up the review structure with one reviewer focusing on methods and experiments while the other examines novelty and related work, then running them through a four-step roundtable process: independent evaluations first, followed by cross-sharing perspectives, converging on key points, and finally synthesizing the findings. Let me load the submission tool and get both reviewers running in parallel.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":726,"cache_read_input_tokens":83597,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":726},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"d514350e-5297-43bc-a18a-2dff997b584d"}
