{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012Z2BneBNe8eR95rDPKPyrN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TM3u5QfQkhWB8okD6Pp3QB","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce an independent, detailed review of the paper \"Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Token-Level Analysis\" submitted to CoRL 2026.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates papers on six dimensions using a 1-10 scale:\n- **Originality**: Novel problems, methods, or combinations\n- **Quality**: Technical soundness and support for claims\n- **Clarity**: Writing quality and organization\n- **Significance**: Importance and advancement of the field\n- **Relevance**: Connection to robot learning and CoRL audience\n- **Limitations**: Honest acknowledgment of limitations\n\nScale: 10=Top 5% seminal, 9=Top 15% strong accept, 8=Top 50% clear accept, 7=Good accept, 6=Marginally above threshold, 5=Marginally below, 4=Not good enough reject, 3=Clear reject, 2=Strong reject, 1=Trivial/wrong.\nCoRL acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n\n## Your Focus: Methods and Experiments\nYou must deeply evaluate:\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the methods (RSA, CKA, probes, attention profiling, counterfactual perturbation, cross-instruction classification) well-chosen and correctly applied?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are confidence intervals, bootstrap tests, effect sizes, and controls appropriate?\n3. **Cross-architecture validation**: Is the 3×4 matrix (OpenVLA/TracVLA/NORA × 4 LIBERO suites) sufficient? Are the 10 filled cells (not full 12) adequately justified?\n4. **Controls**: NLP finetuning control (ΔR²=0.000), label permutation control, instruction ablation — are these sufficient?\n5. **Threshold choices**: The Δ>0.10 threshold for exit collapse — is this principled or arbitrary?\n6. **Metric issues**: RSA-CKA ordering reversal, counterfactual metric incomparability (5/8 cells) — how well are these handled?\n7. **Reproducibility**: Are methods described well enough to reproduce?\n8. **Missing experiments**: What's notably absent? (e.g., closed-loop evaluation, more architectures)\n\n## Experimental Design Reference\nThe paper reports these experiments:\n- **Vision Encoder Geometry** (Sec 4): RSA source localization, DINOv2/CLIP baselines, branch-split analysis, per-layer RSA curves, CKA verification, 6-method consistency, 2×2 factorial (training × instruction), instruction content ablation\n- **Instruction Routing** (Sec 5): Cross-instruction classification (3 strategies), L1 attention 3-condition gradient, 32-layer dense attention profile, full-layer R² scan, counterfactual action analysis (head ablation), training trajectory (4 conditions)\n- **Cross-Architecture** (Sec 6): R² exit collapse matrix (3×4, 10 cells), NLP finetuning control (3 models), cross-architecture counterfactual sensitivity, three-tier routing gradient, L1 heads training vs inheritance, cross-benchmark consistency, TracVLA mixed heads\n\nThe paper also has an experimental design document listing 20 imagined experiments (plans 001-013, plus cross_arch_r2_collapse, cross_arch_cf, three_tier_gradient, nlp_control, cross_arch_l1_heads, cross_inst_probe, l1_attention_three_cond, dense_attention_profile, cf_trajectory_analysis, training_trajectory, tracvla_mixed_heads). Check whether the paper covers these adequately.\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper presents a systematic framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs. Three main findings:\n1. VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from mid-layer (pretrained LLM pattern) to entry-exit bookend pattern (immediate routing at L1: 99.6% vs 80.2%).\n2. R² exit collapse emerges in VLM→VLA transitions (6/6 cells, Δ=0.138-0.691) but not VLA→VLA (0/4, Δ<0.086). NLP control ΔR²=0.000.\n3. Vision encoder geometry (RSA) follows inverted-U, invariant to training and instruction (Δρ<0.017). DINOv2 highest under RSA but CKA reverses ordering.\n\nThree architectures: OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B), TracVLA (Phi-3-mini), NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B). Four LIBERO suites.\n\nKey tables: Table 1 (weight change), Tables 2-11 (vision encoder geometry), Tables 12-17 (instruction routing), Tables 18-25 (cross-architecture).\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in the following structure:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences summarizing the paper.\n\n### Strengths (Methods & Experiments)\nNumbered list of specific strengths.\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods & Experiments)\nNumbered list of specific weaknesses, with severity (Minor/Moderate/Major/Critical).\n\n### Questions for Authors\nNumbered list of clarifying questions.\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\nWhat would strengthen the paper.\n\n### Scores\n- Quality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\n### Verdict\nOne paragraph summary of your assessment."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":84917,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"e295c9c6-9be2-4f42-b242-d3321c0bfd4f"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aa29a1454b7ab0581","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TM3u5QfQkhWB8okD6Pp3QB","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce an independent, detailed review of the paper \"Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Token-Level Analysis\" submitted to CoRL 2026.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates papers on six dimensions using a 1-10 scale:\n- **Originality**: Novel problems, methods, or combinations\n- **Quality**: Technical soundness and support for claims\n- **Clarity**: Writing quality and organization\n- **Significance**: Importance and advancement of the field\n- **Relevance**: Connection to robot learning and CoRL audience\n- **Limitations**: Honest acknowledgment of limitations\n\nScale: 10=Top 5% seminal, 9=Top 15% strong accept, 8=Top 50% clear accept, 7=Good accept, 6=Marginally above threshold, 5=Marginally below, 4=Not good enough reject, 3=Clear reject, 2=Strong reject, 1=Trivial/wrong.\nCoRL acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n\n## Your Focus: Methods and Experiments\nYou must deeply evaluate:\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the methods (RSA, CKA, probes, attention profiling, counterfactual perturbation, cross-instruction classification) well-chosen and correctly applied?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are confidence intervals, bootstrap tests, effect sizes, and controls appropriate?\n3. **Cross-architecture validation**: Is the 3×4 matrix (OpenVLA/TracVLA/NORA × 4 LIBERO suites) sufficient? Are the 10 filled cells (not full 12) adequately justified?\n4. **Controls**: NLP finetuning control (ΔR²=0.000), label permutation control, instruction ablation — are these sufficient?\n5. **Threshold choices**: The Δ>0.10 threshold for exit collapse — is this principled or arbitrary?\n6. **Metric issues**: RSA-CKA ordering reversal, counterfactual metric incomparability (5/8 cells) — how well are these handled?\n7. **Reproducibility**: Are methods described well enough to reproduce?\n8. **Missing experiments**: What's notably absent? (e.g., closed-loop evaluation, more architectures)\n\n## Experimental Design Reference\nThe paper reports these experiments:\n- **Vision Encoder Geometry** (Sec 4): RSA source localization, DINOv2/CLIP baselines, branch-split analysis, per-layer RSA curves, CKA verification, 6-method consistency, 2×2 factorial (training × instruction), instruction content ablation\n- **Instruction Routing** (Sec 5): Cross-instruction classification (3 strategies), L1 attention 3-condition gradient, 32-layer dense attention profile, full-layer R² scan, counterfactual action analysis (head ablation), training trajectory (4 conditions)\n- **Cross-Architecture** (Sec 6): R² exit collapse matrix (3×4, 10 cells), NLP finetuning control (3 models), cross-architecture counterfactual sensitivity, three-tier routing gradient, L1 heads training vs inheritance, cross-benchmark consistency, TracVLA mixed heads\n\nThe paper also has an experimental design document listing 20 imagined experiments (plans 001-013, plus cross_arch_r2_collapse, cross_arch_cf, three_tier_gradient, nlp_control, cross_arch_l1_heads, cross_inst_probe, l1_attention_three_cond, dense_attention_profile, cf_trajectory_analysis, training_trajectory, tracvla_mixed_heads). Check whether the paper covers these adequately.\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper presents a systematic framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs. Three main findings:\n1. VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from mid-layer (pretrained LLM pattern) to entry-exit bookend pattern (immediate routing at L1: 99.6% vs 80.2%).\n2. R² exit collapse emerges in VLM→VLA transitions (6/6 cells, Δ=0.138-0.691) but not VLA→VLA (0/4, Δ<0.086). NLP control ΔR²=0.000.\n3. Vision encoder geometry (RSA) follows inverted-U, invariant to training and instruction (Δρ<0.017). DINOv2 highest under RSA but CKA reverses ordering.\n\nThree architectures: OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B), TracVLA (Phi-3-mini), NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B). Four LIBERO suites.\n\nKey tables: Table 1 (weight change), Tables 2-11 (vision encoder geometry), Tables 12-17 (instruction routing), Tables 18-25 (cross-architecture).\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in the following structure:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences summarizing the paper.\n\n### Strengths (Methods & Experiments)\nNumbered list of specific strengths.\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods & Experiments)\nNumbered list of specific weaknesses, with severity (Minor/Moderate/Major/Critical).\n\n### Questions for Authors\nNumbered list of clarifying questions.\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\nWhat would strengthen the paper.\n\n### Scores\n- Quality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\n### Verdict\nOne paragraph summary of your assessment.","uuid":"c4cdd452-0688-43d3-976a-d7e6e4b16421","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce an independent, detailed review of the paper \"Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Token-Level Analysis\" submitted to CoRL 2026.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates papers on six dimensions using a 1-10 scale:\n- **Originality**: Novel problems, methods, or combinations\n- **Quality**: Technical soundness and support for claims\n- **Clarity**: Writing quality and organization\n- **Significance**: Importance and advancement of the field\n- **Relevance**: Connection to robot learning and CoRL audience\n- **Limitations**: Honest acknowledgment of limitations\n\nScale: 10=Top 5% seminal, 9=Top 15% strong accept, 8=Top 50% clear accept, 7=Good accept, 6=Marginally above threshold, 5=Marginally below, 4=Not good enough reject, 3=Clear reject, 2=Strong reject, 1=Trivial/wrong.\nCoRL acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n\n## Your Focus: Methods and Experiments\nYou must deeply evaluate:\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the methods (RSA, CKA, probes, attention profiling, counterfactual perturbation, cross-instruction classification) well-chosen and correctly applied?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are confidence intervals, bootstrap tests, effect sizes, and controls appropriate?\n3. **Cross-architecture validation**: Is the 3×4 matrix (OpenVLA/TracVLA/NORA × 4 LIBERO suites) sufficient? Are the 10 filled cells (not full 12) adequately justified?\n4. **Controls**: NLP finetuning control (ΔR²=0.000), label permutation control, instruction ablation — are these sufficient?\n5. **Threshold choices**: The Δ>0.10 threshold for exit collapse — is this principled or arbitrary?\n6. **Metric issues**: RSA-CKA ordering reversal, counterfactual metric incomparability (5/8 cells) — how well are these handled?\n7. **Reproducibility**: Are methods described well enough to reproduce?\n8. **Missing experiments**: What's notably absent? (e.g., closed-loop evaluation, more architectures)\n\n## Experimental Design Reference\nThe paper reports these experiments:\n- **Vision Encoder Geometry** (Sec 4): RSA source localization, DINOv2/CLIP baselines, branch-split analysis, per-layer RSA curves, CKA verification, 6-method consistency, 2×2 factorial (training × instruction), instruction content ablation\n- **Instruction Routing** (Sec 5): Cross-instruction classification (3 strategies), L1 attention 3-condition gradient, 32-layer dense attention profile, full-layer R² scan, counterfactual action analysis (head ablation), training trajectory (4 conditions)\n- **Cross-Architecture** (Sec 6): R² exit collapse matrix (3×4, 10 cells), NLP finetuning control (3 models), cross-architecture counterfactual sensitivity, three-tier routing gradient, L1 heads training vs inheritance, cross-benchmark consistency, TracVLA mixed heads\n\nThe paper also has an experimental design document listing 20 imagined experiments (plans 001-013, plus cross_arch_r2_collapse, cross_arch_cf, three_tier_gradient, nlp_control, cross_arch_l1_heads, cross_inst_probe, l1_attention_three_cond, dense_attention_profile, cf_trajectory_analysis, training_trajectory, tracvla_mixed_heads). Check whether the paper covers these adequately.\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper presents a systematic framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs. Three main findings:\n1. VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from mid-layer (pretrained LLM pattern) to entry-exit bookend pattern (immediate routing at L1: 99.6% vs 80.2%).\n2. R² exit collapse emerges in VLM→VLA transitions (6/6 cells, Δ=0.138-0.691) but not VLA→VLA (0/4, Δ<0.086). NLP control ΔR²=0.000.\n3. Vision encoder geometry (RSA) follows inverted-U, invariant to training and instruction (Δρ<0.017). DINOv2 highest under RSA but CKA reverses ordering.\n\nThree architectures: OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B), TracVLA (Phi-3-mini), NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B). Four LIBERO suites.\n\nKey tables: Table 1 (weight change), Tables 2-11 (vision encoder geometry), Tables 12-17 (instruction routing), Tables 18-25 (cross-architecture).\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in the following structure:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences summarizing the paper.\n\n### Strengths (Methods & Experiments)\nNumbered list of specific strengths.\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods & Experiments)\nNumbered list of specific weaknesses, with severity (Minor/Moderate/Major/Critical).\n\n### Questions for Authors\nNumbered list of clarifying questions.\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\nWhat would strengthen the paper.\n\n### Scores\n- Quality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\n### Verdict\nOne paragraph summary of your assessment."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TM3u5QfQkhWB8okD6Pp3QB","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"f4c71534-8670-4b22-b1e4-d016261e231a","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:18:26.540Z"}
