{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012Z2BneBNe8eR95rDPKPyrN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y4Hb9JbcXyLY73TkTTPeEh","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus area is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce an independent, detailed review of the paper \"Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Token-Level Analysis\" submitted to CoRL 2026.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates papers on six dimensions using a 1-10 scale:\n- **Originality**: Novel problems, methods, or combinations\n- **Quality**: Technical soundness and support for claims\n- **Clarity**: Writing quality and organization\n- **Significance**: Importance and advancement of the field\n- **Relevance**: Connection to robot learning and CoRL audience\n- **Limitations**: Honest acknowledgment of limitations\n\nScale: 10=Top 5% seminal, 9=Top 15% strong accept, 8=Top 50% clear accept, 7=Good accept, 6=Marginally above threshold, 5=Marginally below, 4=Not good enough reject, 3=Clear reject, 2=Strong reject, 1=Trivial/wrong.\nCoRL acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n\n## Your Focus: Novelty and Related Work\nYou must deeply evaluate:\n1. **Novelty of the problem**: Is \"how VLA training reshapes instruction routing at the token level\" a genuinely new and important question?\n2. **Novelty of findings**: Are the three main findings (bookend routing, R² exit collapse, vision encoder invariance) genuinely new insights or largely expected from existing LLM interpretability work?\n3. **Related work coverage**: The paper cites RT-2 (Brohan 2023), OpenVLA (Kim 2024), Grant et al. 2026, Haon et al. 2025, probing (Alain & Bengio 2017, Hewitt & Liang 2019), attention analysis (Clark et al. 2019), RSA (Kornblith 2019, Kriegeskorte 2008), LIBERO (Liu 2024), OXE (2023). Is this sufficient? What's missing?\n4. **Positioning relative to LLM interpretability**: There is a massive body of work on mechanistic interpretability of LLMs (circuits, induction heads, residual stream analysis, etc.). Does the paper adequately position itself?\n5. **Positioning relative to VLA behavioral work**: Grant et al. 2026 and Haon et al. 2025 are cited as behavioral analyses. Is the distinction clearly drawn?\n6. **Novelty of the framework**: Six complementary methods (RSA, CKA, probes, classification, attention, counterfactual) — is this combination novel or standard?\n7. **Incremental vs. fundamental**: Does the paper offer fundamental mechanistic understanding or incremental observations?\n8. **Practical impact**: Does the analysis lead to actionable insights for VLA design? Or is it purely observational?\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper presents a systematic framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs validated across 3 architectures and 4 task suites. Three main findings:\n\n**Finding 1 (C2): Immediate dedicated routing.** VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from mid-layer inverted-U (pretrained LLM, peak L9-16) to entry-exit bookend (peaks L0-1, L31). Three routing strategies: trained VLA (99.6% at L1), pretrained LLM (80.2% at L1, saturates L5), random init (100% at L1, decays to 82% at L31).\n\n**Finding 2: R² exit collapse.** Action-decodability drops at exit layers during VLM→VLA transition (6/6 cells, Δ=0.138-0.691) but not VLA→VLA (0/4, Δ<0.086). NLP control ΔR²=0.000.\n\n**Finding 3 (C3): Vision encoder geometry.** Frozen encoder provides RSA ρ=0.615 (highest), invariant to training/instruction (Δρ<0.017). DINOv2 tops RSA ranking (0.692) but CKA reverses ordering.\n\nUnified by three-tier routing gradient: VLM base → general VLA → task-specific VLA.\n\n## Key Claims to Evaluate for Novelty\n- \"how training reshapes instruction routing and information flow at the token level remains uncharacterized\" — Is this true?\n- \"whether such effects generalize across architectures is unknown\" — Is cross-architecture validation truly new?\n- The \"three-tier routing gradient\" — Is this a novel conceptual contribution or obvious given the training pipeline?\n- \"instruction routing is a staged emergent property\" — Novel insight or expected?\n- RSA-CKA dissociation as \"metric dependence\" finding — Novel or well-known?\n\n## Related Work You Should Check Against\n- **Mechanistic interpretability of LLMs**: Elhage et al. (2021), Olsson et al. (2022) on induction heads, Conmy et al. (2023) on circuits, Geva et al. (2023) on MLP knowledge neurons. Does the paper engage with this literature?\n- **VLM interpretability**: Any work on how vision-language models process multi-modal information internally?\n- **Probing in NLP**: Beyond Alain & Bengio (2017) and Hewitt & Liang (2019), there's Tenney et al. (2019), Jawahar et al. (2019), etc.\n- **Action prediction from representations**: Any prior work on probing robot policies?\n- **Attention analysis**: Beyond Clark et al. (2019), there's Voita et al. (2019) on attention head roles, Michel et al. (2019) on head pruning, etc.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in the following structure:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences summarizing the paper.\n\n### Strengths (Novelty & Related Work)\nNumbered list of specific strengths.\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty & Related Work)\nNumbered list of specific weaknesses, with severity (Minor/Moderate/Major/Critical).\n\n### Questions for Authors\nNumbered list of clarifying questions.\n\n### Missing Related Work\nSpecific papers or research areas that should be discussed.\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\n### Verdict\nOne paragraph summary of your assessment."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":713,"cache_read_input_tokens":84917,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":713},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"2d3c40cd-beb6-4953-b534-17f9de0e3455"}
