sive body of work on mechanistic interpretability of LLMs (circuits, induction heads, residual stream analysis, etc.). Does the paper adequately position itself?\n5. **Positioning relative to VLA behavioral work**: Grant et al. 2026 and Haon et al. 2025 are cited as behavioral analyses. Is the distinction clearly drawn?\n6. **Novelty of the framework**: Six complementary methods (RSA, CKA, probes, classification, attention, counterfactual) — is this combination novel or standard?\n7. **Incremental vs. fundamental**: Does the paper offer fundamental mechanistic understanding or incremental observations?\n8. **Practical impact**: Does the analysis lead to actionable insights for VLA design? Or is it purely observational?\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper presents a systematic framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs validated across 3 architectures and 4 task suites. Three main findings:\n\n**Finding 1 (C2): Immediate dedicated routing.** VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from mid-layer inverted-U (pretrained LLM, peak L9-16) to entry-exit bookend (peaks L0-1, L31). Three routing strategies: trained VLA (99.6% at L1), pretrained LLM (80.2% at L1, saturates L5), random init (100% at L1, decays to 82% at L31).\n\n**Finding 2: R² exit collapse.** Action-decodability drops at exit layers during VLM→VLA transition (6/6 cells, Δ=0.138-0.691) but not VLA→VLA (0/4, Δ<0.086). NLP control ΔR²=0.000.\n\n**Finding 3 (C3): Vision encoder geometry.** Frozen encoder provides RSA ρ=0.615 (highest), invariant to training/instruction (Δρ<0.017). DINOv2 tops RSA ranking (0.692) but CKA reverses ordering.\n\nUnified by three-tier routing gradient: VLM base → general VLA → task-specific VLA.\n\n## Key Claims to Evaluate for Novelty\n- \"how training reshapes instruction routing and information flow at the token level remains uncharacterized\" — Is this true?\n- \"whether such effects generalize across architectures is unknown\" — Is cross-architecture validation truly new?\n- The \"three-tier routing gradient\" — Is this a novel conceptual contribution or obvious given the training pipeline?\n- \"instruction routing is a staged emergent property\" — Novel insight or expected?\n- RSA-CKA dissociation as \"metric dependence\" finding — Novel or well-known?\n\n## Related Work You Should Check Against\n- **Mechanistic interpretability of LLMs**: Elhage et al. (2021), Olsson et al. (2022) on induction heads, Conmy et al. (2023) on circuits, Geva et al. (2023) on MLP knowledge neurons. Does the paper engage with this literature?\n- **VLM interpretability**: Any work on how vision-language models process multi-modal information internally?\n- **Probing in NLP**: Beyond Alain & Bengio (2017) and Hewitt & Liang (2019), there's Tenney et al. (2019), Jawahar et al. (2019), etc.\n- **Action prediction from representations**: Any prior work on probing robot policies?\n- **Attention analysis**: Beyond Clark et al. (2019), there's Voita et al. (2019) on attention head roles, Michel et al. (2019) on head pruning, etc.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in the following structure:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences summarizing the paper.\n\n### Strengths (Novelty & Related Work)\nNumbered list of specific strengths.\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty & Related Work)\nNumbered list of specific weaknesses, with severity (Minor/Moderate/Major/Critical).\n\n### Questions for Authors\nNumbered list of clarifying questions.\n\n### Missing Related Work\nSpecific papers or research areas that should be discussed.\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\n### Verdict\nOne paragraph summary of your assessment.","uuid":"3f2a2ecb-c3d4-4386-8568-51441c1c6aca","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus area is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce an independent, detailed review of the paper \"Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Token-Level Analysis\" submitted to CoRL 2026.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates papers on six dimensions using a 1-10 scale:\n- **Originality**: Novel problems, methods, or combinations\n- **Quality**: Technical soundness and support for claims\n- **Clarity**: Writing quality and organization\n- **Significance**: Importance and advancement of the field\n- **Relevance**: Connection to robot learning and CoRL audience\n- **Limitations**: Honest acknowledgment of limitations\n\nScale: 10=Top 5% seminal, 9=Top 15% strong accept, 8=Top 50% clear accept, 7=Good accept, 6=Marginally above threshold, 5=Marginally below, 4=Not good enough reject, 3=Clear reject, 2=Strong reject, 1=Trivial/wrong.\nCoRL acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n\n## Your Focus: Novelty and Related Work\nYou must deeply evaluate:\n1. **Novelty of the problem**: Is \"how VLA training reshapes instruction routing at the token level\" a genuinely new and important question?\n2. **Novelty of findings**: Are the three main findings (bookend routing, R² exit collapse, vision encoder invariance) genuinely new insights or largely expected from existing LLM interpretability work?\n3. **Related work coverage**: The paper cites RT-2 (Brohan 2023), OpenVLA (Kim 2024), Grant et al. 2026, Haon et al. 2025, probing (Alain & Bengio 2017, Hewitt & Liang 2019), attention analysis (Clark et al. 2019), RSA (Kornblith 2019, Kriegeskorte 2008), LIBERO (Liu 2024), OXE (2023). Is this sufficient? What's missing?\n4. **Positioning relative to LLM interpretability**: There is a massive body of work on mechanistic interpretability of LLMs (circuits, induction heads, residual stream analysis, etc.). Does the paper adequately position itself?\n5. **Positioning relative to VLA behavioral work**: Grant et al. 2026 and Haon et al. 2025 are cited as behavioral analyses. Is the distinction clearly drawn?\n6. **Novelty of the framework**: Six complementary methods (RSA, CKA, probes, classification, attention, counterfactual) — is this combination novel or standard?\n7. **Incremental vs. fundamental**: Does the paper offer fundamental mechanistic understanding or incremental observations?\n8. **Practical impact**: Does the analysis lead to actionable insights for VLA design? Or is it purely observational?\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper presents a systematic framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs validated across 3 architectures and 4 task suites. Three main findings:\n\n**Finding 1 (C2): Immediate dedicated routing.** VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from mid-layer inverted-U (pretrained LLM, peak L9-16) to entry-exit bookend (peaks L0-1, L31). Three routing strategies: trained VLA (99.6% at L1), pretrained LLM (80.2% at L1, saturates L5), random init (100% at L1, decays to 82% at L31).\n\n**Finding 2: R² exit collapse.** Action-decodability drops at exit layers during VLM→VLA transition (6/6 cells, Δ=0.138-0.691) but not VLA→VLA (0/4, Δ<0.086). NLP control ΔR²=0.000.\n\n**Finding 3 (C3): Vision encoder geometry.** Frozen encoder provides RSA ρ=0.615 (highest), invariant to training/instruction (Δρ<0.017). DINOv2 tops RSA ranking (0.692) but CKA reverses ordering.\n\nUnified by three-tier routing gradient: VLM base → general VLA → task-specific VLA.\n\n## Key Claims to Evaluate for Novelty\n- \"how training reshapes instruction routing and information flow at the token level remains uncharacterized\" — Is this true?\n- \"whether such effects generalize across architectures is unknown\" — Is cross-architecture validation truly new?\n- The \"three-tier routing gradient\" — Is this a novel conceptual contribution or obvious given the training pipeline?\n- \"instruction routing is a staged emergent property\" — Novel insight or expected?\n- RSA-CKA dissociation as \"metric dependence\" finding — Novel or well-known?\n\n## Related Work You Should Check Against\n- **Mechanistic interpretability of LLMs**: Elhage et al. (2021), Olsson et al. (2022) on induction heads, Conmy et al. (2023) on circuits, Geva et al. (2023) on MLP knowledge neurons. Does the paper engage with this literature?\n- **VLM interpretability**: Any work on how vision-language models process multi-modal information internally?\n- **Probing in NLP**: Beyond Alain & Bengio (2017) and Hewitt & Liang (2019), there's Tenney et al. (2019), Jawahar et al. (2019), etc.\n- **Action prediction from representations**: Any prior work on probing robot policies?\n- **Attention analysis**: Beyond Clark et al. (2019), there's Voita et al. (2019) on attention head roles, Michel et al. (2019) on head pruning, etc.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in the following structure:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences summarizing the paper.\n\n### Strengths (Novelty & Related Work)\nNumbered list of specific strengths.\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty & Related Work)\nNumbered list of specific weaknesses, with severity (Minor/Moderate/Major/Critical).\n\n### Questions for Authors\nNumbered list of clarifying questions.\n\n### Missing Related Work\nSpecific papers or research areas that should be discussed.\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\n### Verdict\nOne paragraph summary of your assessment."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4Hb9JbcXyLY73TkTTPeEh","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"7a53b555-cba2-472d-be7f-8f6673d0ddfc","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:18:54.424Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FxMKpRzo44G5Sxff7bn4QH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\n5\t\\label{sec:setup}\n6\t\n7\tWe apply all three analyses (\\S\\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}) to the $3 \\times 4$ architecture-suite matrix described in \\S\\ref{sec:arch_data} (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).\n8\tOf the 12 cells, 10 are filled; NORA Object and Long-Horizon lack available checkpoints.\n9\tFor OpenVLA LIBERO-Goal, $N = 63{,}228$ observations; other cells are at comparable scale.\n10\tFor trained models that produce action outputs, counterfactual sensitivity is measured via Cohen's $d$ on action MSE.\n11\tFor reference VLMs (Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2.5-VL) that do not produce actions, sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations; these metrics operate on different scales and are not directly comparable (\\S\\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}).\n12\tOpenVLA's OXE-pretrained checkpoint produces actions, permitting same-metric comparison (Figure~\\ref{fig:result_matrix}; Bonferroni correction in Appendix~\\ref{sec:bonferroni_correction}).\n13\t\n14\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n15\t\\centering\n16\t\\includegraphics[width=0.82\\textwidth]{figures/fig_matrix_heatmap.pdf}\n17\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n18\t\\caption{\\textbf{Cross-architecture evaluation matrix.} Each cell reports $\\Delta R^2$ (peak$-$exit). OpenVLA: VLA-to-VLA; TracVLA and NORA: VLM-to-VLA. Gray cells: checkpoints not released by original authors. Red borders indicate $\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$.}\n19\t\\label{fig:result_matrix}\n20\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n21\t\\end{figure}\n22\t\n23\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n24\t\\subsection{OpenVLA: Motivating Single-Architecture Analysis}\n25\t\\label{sec:openvla_routing}\n26\t\n27\tBefore testing cross-architectural generality, we establish three core phenomena on OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024_openvla} (full analysis in Appendix~\\ref{sec:openvla_deepdive_appendix}).\n28\tAt L1, three conditions form a gradient: random-weight initialization allocates 0/32 instruction-dominant heads, Llama-2 base~\\cite{touvron2023_llama2} allocates 9/32, and VLA-trained allocates 24/32, indicating that VLA fine-tuning is associated with instruction routing beyond language-model priors.\n29\tCounterfactual perturbation produces graded action divergence ordered by semantic distance ($d = 0.81$--$1.18$), and dose-response ablation of instruction-dominant heads yields monotonic trajectory divergence ($d = 0.48$--$0.64$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:action_trajectory}).\n30\t$R^2$ probing localizes training effects at the readout token ($+0.069$ vs.\\ $+0.015$ at image\\_mean), and readout instruction classification remains at $100\\%$ through L31 in the trained model (reference: $82\\%$ decay).\n31\tThese phenomena motivate the cross-architecture tests that follow.\n32\t\n33\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n34\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n35\t\\subsection{Exit Collapse: Layer-Wise Action Probing}\n36\t\\label{sec:r2_collapse}\n37\t\n38\tWe define $R^2$ exit collapse as $\\Delta R^2 = R^2_{\\text{peak}} - R^2_{\\text{exit}}$, the drop in action-probe decodability from peak layer to exit layer (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).\n39\tVLM-to-VLA fine-tuning is associated with substantial exit collapse across all tested conditions: TracVLA~\\cite{zheng2024tracevla} $\\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.362$ across all four suites, and NORA~\\cite{hung2025nora} $\\Delta R^2 = 0.308$ (Spatial) and $0.455$ (Goal) (Figure~\\ref{fig:r2_collapse}), all exceeding the $\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ threshold for substantive collapse (\\S\\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}).\n40\tUnder VLA-to-VLA conditions, OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024_openvla} shows only small fluctuations across all four suites ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.033$--$0.086$, all below $0.10$); reference baselines similarly show $\\Delta R^2 \\leq 0.097$.\n41\tThe gap between the largest non-collapse value ($0.097$) and smallest collapse value ($0.156$) spans $0.059$; any threshold in this interval yields identical grouping, and a permutation test rejects random label assignment ($p = 0.008$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:delta_r2_threshold}).\n42\tEven excluding NORA-Goal (whose bootstrap CI is unreliable; Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}, footnote $b$), the VLM-to-VLA range remains $\\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.362$ (TracVLA only), consistently exceeding the $0.10$ threshold.\n43\tMonte Carlo cross-validation supports this separation: VLM-to-VLA trained CIs lie entirely above zero (lower bounds $\\geq 0.09$), while VLA-to-VLA trained CIs span or nearly span zero (Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix}).\n44\t\n45\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n46\t\\centering\n47\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_r2_collapse.pdf}\n48\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n49\t\\caption{\\textbf{Layer-wise action-probe $R^2$ (LIBERO-Goal, readout token).} Solid: trained; dashed: reference. VLM-to-VLA transitions show pronounced peak-to-exit drops ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.16$--$0.46$); VLA-to-VLA remains flat ($< 0.09$). Quantitative $\\Delta R^2$ values use demo-level splitting (\\S\\ref{sec:cross_arch_analyses}); see Table~\\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix} for all cells.}\n50\t\\label{fig:r2_collapse}\n51\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n52\t\\end{figure}\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{NLP finetuning control.}\n55\tTo test whether exit collapse is specific to action-oriented training, we probe three chat-finetuned models that share backbones with our VLAs but lack action heads or robot data: Llama-2-7b-chat (OpenVLA), Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct (TracVLA), and Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct (NORA).\\footnote{Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct shares the Qwen2.5 language backbone with NORA's Qwen2.5-VL-3B but lacks the vision encoder and cross-attention modules; see Appendix~\\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}.}\n56\tAll three produce flat $R^2$ profiles ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.000$; $R^2 = 0.155 \\pm 0.001$, $0.155 \\pm 0.000$, and $0.155 \\pm 0.000$ respectively; Appendix~\\ref{sec:nlp_control_appendix}, Figure~\\ref{fig:nlp_control_r2}), contrasting with VLM-to-VLA collapse ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.455$).\n57\tThe absence of exit collapse in any NLP control is inconsistent with exit collapse being a generic consequence of language-model fine-tuning.\n58\t\\textbf{In sum, exit collapse is consistently associated with VLM-to-VLA transitions and absent in both VLA-to-VLA and NLP-only fine-tuning.}\n59\t\n60\t\\begin{table}[t]\n61\t\\centering\n62\t\\caption{\\textbf{Peak-to-exit action-probe drop across the architecture-suite matrix.} $\\Delta R^2$ is computed as the difference between the maximum layer-wise action-probe $R^2$ and the exit-layer $R^2$. Values above 0.10 are treated as substantial descriptive drops. OpenVLA is a VLA-to-VLA comparison, whereas TracVLA and NORA are VLM-to-VLA comparisons.}\n63\t\\label{tab:cross_arch_matrix}\n64\t\\small\n65\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n66\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll cccc@{}}\n67\t\\toprule\n68\t& & & \\multicolumn{4}{c}{$\\Delta R^2$ (peak $-$ exit)} \\\\\n69\t\\cmidrule(l){4-7}\n70\tArchitecture & Suite & Transition & Trained & 95\\% CI & Reference & 95\\% CI \\\\\n71\t\\midrule\n72\tOpenVLA & Goal    & VLA$\\to$VLA & 0.086 & [$-$0.060, 0.110] & 0.056 & [0.010, 0.080] \\\\\n73\tOpenVLA & Spatial & VLA$\\to$VLA & 0.037 & [0.010, 0.100]    & 0.044 & [0.030, 0.110] \\\\\n74\tOpenVLA & Object  & VLA$\\to$VLA & 0.037 & [0.000, 0.090]    & 0.051 & [0.000, 0.090] \\\\\n75\tOpenVLA & Long    & VLA$\\to$VLA & 0.033 & [0.000, 0.100]    & 0.035 & [0.030, 0.100] \\\\\n76\t\\midrule\n77\tTracVLA & Goal    & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.333 & [0.210, 0.450]    & 0.065 & [$-$0.110, 0.050] \\\\\n78\tTracVLA & Spatial & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.156 & [0.090, 0.220]    & 0.048 & ---$^{a}$ \\\\\n79\tTracVLA & Object  & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.362 & [0.280, 0.500]    & 0.009 & [$-$0.070, 0.020] \\\\\n80\tTracVLA & Long    & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.358 & [0.170, 0.530]    & 0.097 & [$-$0.020, 0.110] \\\\\n81\t\\midrule\n82\tNORA    & Goal    & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.455$^{b}$ & ---$^{b}$  & 0.081 & [$-$0.010, 0.110] \\\\\n83\tNORA    & Spatial & VLM$\\to$VLA & 0.308 & [0.300, 0.510]    & 0.089 & [$-$0.010, 0.090] \\\\\n84\t\\bottomrule\n85\t\\multicolumn{7}{l}{\\footnotesize NORA Object and Long-Horizon checkpoints are unavailable.}\\\\\n86\t\\multicolumn{7}{l}{\\footnotesize $^{a}$CI artifact from flat $R^2$ profile. $^{b}$Bootstrap CI unreliable (exit-layer $R^2 \\approx -0.006$).}\\\\\n87\t\\end{tabular}\n88\t\\end{table}\n89\t\n90\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n91\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n92\t\\subsection{Instruction Sensitivity: Counterfactual Perturbation}\n93\t\\label{sec:cf_sensitivity}\n94\t\n95\t\\subsubsection{Instruction Sensitivity in Action-Producing VLAs}\n96\tWe first evaluate counterfactual sensitivity in action-producing models using a shared action-space metric.\n97\tAcross OpenVLA and TracVLA, instruction perturbations produce medium-to-large action divergence in all eight architecture-suite cells (Figure~\\ref{fig:counterfactual_bar}).\n98\t\n99\t\\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.46\\columnwidth}\n100\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n101\t\\centering\n102\t\\includegraphics[width=0.44\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_counterfactual_bar.pdf}\n103\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n104\t\\caption{Counterfactual sensitivity across the architecture-suite matrix. (a)~Cohen's $d$ for four perturbation conditions (E=empty, W=wrong, S=shuffled, P=paraphrased); asterisks mark the top condition per cell; dashed line: large-effect threshold ($d = 0.8$). (b)~Cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale).}\n105\t\\label{fig:counterfactual_bar}\n106\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n107\t\\end{wrapfigure}\n108\tEvery perturbation condition yields $d = 0.45$--$1.52$ (Table~\\ref{tab:cf_summary}; full per-condition rankings in Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:counterfactual}).\n109\tThe ``empty'' condition ranks first in 6 of 8 cells ($d = 1.17$--$1.52$); ``paraphrased'' ranks last in 7 of 8, indicating that VLAs encode instruction content beyond token-level matching.\n110\tTracVLA produces systematically higher effect sizes ($d = 0.97$--$1.52$) than OpenVLA ($0.45$--$1.21$).\n111\tRemoving the instruction entirely often produces larger divergence than replacing it with a wrong task, consistent with instruction removal producing larger disruption than instruction substitution.\n112\t\n113\tTwo OpenVLA cells permit same-metric trained/reference comparison: on Spatial, training increases sensitivity (trained$-$reference difference: $+0.05$ to $+0.24$ across conditions); on Long-Horizon, the OXE-pretrained model exceeds the LIBERO-trained model ($d = 1.05$ vs.\\ $0.57$), consistent with the general-purpose model maintaining broader instruction responsiveness.\n114\t\\textbf{Instruction perturbation produces graded action divergence across all tested conditions, with effect sizes reflecting semantic distance.}\n115\t\n116\t\\begin{table}[t]\n117\t\\centering\n118\t\\caption{\\textbf{Counterfactual sensitivity summary.} Cohen's $d$ range and most sensitive perturbation for action-producing models (OpenVLA, TracVLA). NORA uses cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale; see \\S\\ref{sec:cf_repr}).}\n119\t\\label{tab:cf_summary}\n120\t\\small\n121\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcl@{}}\n122\t\\toprule\n123\tArchitecture & $d$ range & Most sensitive & Metric \\\\\n124\t\\midrule\n125\tOpenVLA & 0.45--1.21 & empty (2/4 suites) & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n126\tTracVLA & 0.97--1.52 & empty (4/4 suites) & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n127\t\\midrule\n128\tNORA    & 0.28--sat. & --- & cosine dist.$^{a}$ \\\\\n129\t\\bottomrule\n130\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\footnotesize $^{a}$Representation-space only; not comparable to Cohen's $d$ (\\S\\ref{sec:cf_repr}).}\\\\\n131\t\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\footnotesize Empty ranks first in 6/8 suites total across both architectures.}\\\\\n132\t\\end{tabular}\n133\t\\end{table}\n134\t\n135\t\\subsubsection{Representation-Space Sensitivity in NORA and Reference VLMs}\n136\t\\label{sec:cf_repr}\n137\t\n138\tNORA and reference VLMs lack action outputs; their sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations (different scale from Cohen's $d$).\n139\tNORA Goal produces saturated cosine distances ($1.0$); NORA Spatial peaks at $0.275$; reference VLMs show weak sensitivity ($\\leq 0.19$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:cf_details_appendix}).\n140\tThe trained-reference contrast is consistent with action-space findings, though the different metric prevents direct magnitude comparison.\n141\t\n142\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n143\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n144\t\\subsection{Inherited Routing: L1 Attention Profiling}\n145\t\\label{sec:l1_priors}\n146\t\n147\tL1 instruction-dominant head counts across architectures reveal a pattern that contrasts with the training-associated effects documented in \\S\\ref{sec:r2_collapse} and \\S\\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}.\n148\tThe TracVLA reference (Phi-3-Vision) allocates 32/32 heads to instruction-dominant attention across all four suites, saturating the capacity of its 32-head L1 before any VLA training.\n149\tTraining slightly reduces this count to 27--30/32, indicating that VLA fine-tuning does not increase L1 instruction-dominant attention in this architecture.\n150\tThe NORA reference (Qwen2.5-VL) allocates 13--14 of its 16 L1 heads to instruction dominance (81--88\\%), and training leaves this essentially unchanged at 12--13/16.\n151\tOpenVLA shows 24--26/32 instruction-dominant heads in the OXE-pretrained baseline; trained values are similar (14--24/32), with the largest change in Long-Horizon where training reduces the count from 25 to 14 heads (Figure~\\ref{fig:cross_arch_l1}).\n152\t\n153\tIf VLA training drove L1 instruction routing, reference models should show low counts that increase after training, as in OpenVLA's random-weight condition (0/32; \\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}); instead, both reference VLMs already saturate or near-saturate, and training leaves counts unchanged or slightly reduced.\n154\t\\textbf{L1 instruction routing is an inherited backbone property, not a consequence of VLA training.}\n155\t\n156\t\\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.46\\columnwidth}\n157\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n158\t\\centering\n159\t\\includegraphics[width=0.44\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_l1_attention.pdf}\n160\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n161\t\\caption{L1 instruction-dominant head counts (trained vs.\\ reference; heads with $>50\\%$ attention on instruction tokens). Reference VLMs already saturate or near-saturate; training does not increase counts. Medians across available LIBERO suites per architecture.}\n162\t\\label{fig:cross_arch_l1}\n163\t\\vspace{-4pt}\n164\t\\end{wrapfigure}\n165\t\n166\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n167\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n168\t\\subsection{Synthesis: Training-Associated vs.\\ Inherited Routing}\n169\t\\label{sec:routing_synthesis}\n170\t\n171\tThe three analyses separate two empirical patterns: exit collapse and counterfactual sensitivity are training-associated (pronounced in VLM-to-VLA, small in VLA-to-VLA), while L1 instruction attention is inherited from reference backbones.\n172\tDespite qualitatively different reference starting points (OpenVLA: peak at L0 with monotonic decline; Phi-3-Vision: gradual decline from L6), trained OpenVLA and TracVLA converge to nearly identical mid-layer peaks ($R^2 = 0.637$ at L9 vs.\\ $0.634$ at L11, $\\Delta = 0.003$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:cross_arch_r2}, Table~\\ref{tab:arch_overview}), suggesting that action fine-tuning is associated with convergence toward a shared mid-layer structure regardless of starting point.\n173\tThese probes measure decodability, not causal reliance (label-permutation controls confirm genuine structure; Appendix~\\ref{sec:label_permutation}).\n174\tMatched-size ablation reveals that instruction sensitivity is distributed across heads in proportion to their instruction attention fraction, consistent with instruction information being woven into the general residual stream rather than concentrated in a dedicated routing circuit ($d = 0.65$ vs.\\ $0.60$, 8\\% gap; Appendix Table~\\ref{tab:specificity_control}).\n175\tThis distributed pattern suggests that uniform pruning may be preferable to attention-score-based head selection for VLA compression.\n176\tThe consistent three-way pattern provides a first cross-architecture decomposition of VLA instruction routing.\n177\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TM3u5QfQkhWB8okD6Pp3QB","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"ce56b51b-dc50-41cd-a9c7-474cdf2b579a","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:18:54.070Z"}
