{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PHP2QccWDNXNqHSnfupQGK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\paragraph{Use of Generative AI.}\n2\tGenerative AI tools (Claude, GPT-4) assisted with code implementation and manuscript editing; all scientific content was produced by the authors.\n3\t\n4\t\\appendix\n5\t\n6\t\\section{Supplementary Material}\n7\t\\label{sec:appendix}\n8\t\n9\tUnless noted otherwise, all analyses use OpenVLA-7B (\\texttt{openvla/openvla-7b} on HuggingFace) evaluated on LIBERO-Goal (10 tasks $\\times$ 50 demonstrations, $N = 63{,}228$ observations after excluding terminal frames).\n10\tModel conditions, representation extraction, metric definitions, and probe architectures follow \\S\\ref{sec:method}.\n11\tCross-suite experiments (\\S\\ref{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}) use LIBERO-Spatial with the same protocol (10 tasks $\\times$ 50 demonstrations).\n12\tAll experiments ran on 2$\\times$ NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPUs (96\\,GB each); random seed is 42 unless stated otherwise.\n13\t\n14\t\\subsection{Architecture Summary}\n15\t\\begin{table}[h]\n16\t\\centering\n17\t\\caption{Architectures studied. The reference baselines differ in kind: the OpenVLA reference is an OXE-pretrained VLA that produces actions; TracVLA and NORA references are pure VLMs with no action training.}\n18\t\\label{tab:arch_overview}\n19\t\\footnotesize\n20\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n21\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccll@{}}\n22\t\\toprule\n23\tArch. & Backbone & Layers & Attention & Vision Enc. & Reference Baseline \\\\\n24\t\\midrule\n25\tOpenVLA & Llama-2-7B & 32 & 32 MHA & DinoSigLIP $\\to$ MLP proj. & OXE-pret.\\ VLA \\\\\n26\tTracVLA & Phi-3-mini & 32 & 32 MHA & SigLIP + spatial trace & Phi-3-Vision (VLM) \\\\\n27\tNORA & Qwen2.5-VL-3B & 36 & 16q/2kv GQA & Qwen2.5-VL native & Qwen2.5-VL (VLM) \\\\\n28\t\\bottomrule\n29\t\\end{tabular}\n30\t\\end{table}\n31\t\n32\t\\subsection{OpenVLA-Specific Analysis Methods}\n33\t\\label{sec:openvla_methods_appendix}\n34\t\n35\tThree additional analyses are applied to OpenVLA to characterize instruction encoding and representational geometry.\n36\t\n37\t\\paragraph{Cross-instruction probes.}\n38\tEach visual frame is paired with all 10 LIBERO-Goal task instructions, producing 10 representation vectors per frame per layer.\n39\tA 10-way linear classifier identifies which instruction was present, applied separately at image\\_mean and readout, with leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) over 10 folds.\n40\t\n41\t\\paragraph{Representational similarity analysis (RSA).}\n42\tRSA~\\cite{kriegeskorte2008} quantifies geometric alignment between representations and the action space.\n43\tFor each task, we compute pairwise representational dissimilarity matrices (RDMs) using cosine distances for representations and Euclidean distances for 7-D action vectors, from within-task observation pairs across 50 demonstrations.\n44\tAlignment is measured via Spearman $\\rho$ between upper-triangular RDM entries; we report mean $\\rho$ across 10 tasks with 95\\% confidence intervals from 10{,}000 bootstrap resamples at the task level~\\cite{nili2014toolbox}.\n45\tWe prefer RSA over CKA~\\cite{kornblith2019}: RSA captures relative distance structure via scale-invariant rank correlation, suited to comparing high- and low-dimensional spaces (4{,}096-D vs.\\ 7-D), while CKA measures global subspace overlap that can remain high when geometric structure differs.\n46\tBoth metrics are reported in \\S\\ref{sec:cka_appendix}; their divergence (\\S\\ref{sec:routing_synthesis}) confirms that neither alone fully characterizes representational structure.\n47\t\n48\t\\paragraph{External baselines.}\n49\tDINOv2 ViT-L/14~\\cite{oquab2024_dinov2} (\\texttt{cls\\_token}/\\texttt{patch\\_mean}) and CLIP ViT-L/14~\\cite{radford2021_clip} (\\texttt{patch\\_mean}) provide self-supervised and vision-language pretrained reference points for probing and RSA.\n50\t\n51\t\\subsection{OpenVLA Single-Architecture Deep Dive}\n52\t\\label{sec:openvla_deepdive_appendix}\n53\t\n54\tThis section provides the full single-architecture analysis summarized in \\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}.\n55\t\n56\t\\paragraph{Cross-instruction probes.}\n57\tCross-instruction probes validate the analysis framework on OpenVLA~\\cite{kim2024_openvla}: as predicted by the causal mask, image\\_mean instruction classification stays at chance ($10\\%$) across all 32 layers in all conditions (full profile in \\S\\ref{sec:dense_routing}).\n58\tTrained readout achieves $100\\%$ 10-way classification from L1 onward and maintains this through L31 (LOOCV-confirmed; \\S\\ref{sec:loocv}).\n59\tReference readout also reaches $100\\%$ at L1 but decays to $82\\%$ by L31; the readout token is thus the primary multimodal integration site, and instruction encoding remains stable across depth in the trained model.\n60\tAll patterns reproduce with a linear probe (\\S\\ref{sec:linear_probe}).\n61\t\n62\t\\paragraph{L1 attention gradient.}\n63\tAt L1, three conditions form a gradient of instruction attention: randomly initialized networks allocate 0/32 instruction-dominant heads ($11.7\\%$ text attention, $1.5\\times$ text-to-image ratio), Llama-2 base~\\cite{touvron2023_llama2} allocates 9/32 ($36.5\\%$), and VLA-trained allocates 24/32 ($57.7\\%$, $38.2\\times$ text-to-image ratio; \\S\\ref{sec:attention_decomp}).\n64\tThe 32-layer attention profile reveals qualitatively distinct organizations: Llama-2 base concentrates instruction attention at mid-depth (L9--L16: 15--22 heads), while VLA fine-tuning produces an entry-exit bookend pattern with L0--L1 at 21--24 heads, L31 at 18, and intermediate layers at 0--11 (\\S\\ref{sec:threshold_sensitivity}).\n65\tHead counts are computed at a $50\\%$ threshold; sensitivity analysis ($30\\%$--$70\\%$) confirms ordering robustness.\n66\tA fourth condition, OpenVLA fine-tuned on LIBERO, preserves L1 entry concentration (22/32 heads) but loses L31 exit re-engagement entirely (0 vs.\\ 18 heads for the OXE-pretrained model), consistent with exit-layer attention serving multi-task flexibility rather than task-specific routing.\n67\tThe redistribution beyond Llama-2 base priors is consistent with VLA fine-tuning being associated with instruction routing beyond what is inherited from language-model attention.\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{Counterfactual perturbation.}\n70\tFour perturbation types across 10 LIBERO-Goal tasks produce large action divergence: wrong task ($d = 1.182$), empty instruction ($1.170$), shuffled tokens ($1.096$), paraphrased ($0.996$).\n71\tAll four conditions yield $d \\geq 1.0$, with ``wrong'' slightly exceeding ``empty.''\n72\tThe effect replicates across LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks): empty $1.173$, shuffled $0.979$, wrong $0.885$, paraphrased $0.807$ (Table~\\ref{tab:counterfactual_spatial}).\n73\tAcross both suites (20 tasks), the gradient spans $d = 0.81$--$1.18$.\n74\tSpatial instructions differ only in prepositions, yet wrong-task perturbation still produces $d = 0.885$; instruction-dominant heads route fine-grained spatial information, not merely task identity.\n75\t\n76\t\\paragraph{Dose-response ablation.}\n77\tDose-response ablation of L1 instruction-dominant heads produces monotonic trajectory divergence: $d$ rises from $0.482$ to $0.639$ as 4--32 heads are progressively ablated, with steep increase through 12 heads and plateau beyond 20 (\\S\\ref{sec:action_trajectory}).\n78\tAblating all 24 instruction-dominant heads alters $72.3\\%$ of action tokens (token-level $d = 0.65$, trajectory-level $d = 2.943$); control ablation of 8 random heads yields $d = 0.41$ ($29.3\\%$ tokens affected), concentrated on the gripper dimension.\n79\tThe routing effect is task-dependent: instruction-discriminative tasks show $2.5\\times$ larger $|\\Delta R^2|$ under L1 ablation than visually-sufficient tasks ($p = 0.033$; \\S\\ref{sec:task_complexity}).\n80\t\n81\t\\paragraph{$R^2$ localization.}\n82\t$R^2$ action probing localizes training effects: trained readout peaks at $R^2 = 0.678$ (L13), reference readout peaks at $0.609$ (L32), and the training effect concentrates at the readout ($+0.069$) rather than at image\\_mean ($+0.015$; \\S\\ref{sec:action_r2_threecond}).\n83\tThese effects survive Bonferroni correction across all 63 tests (\\S\\ref{sec:bonferroni_correction}).\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Counterfactual Sensitivity Details}\n86\t\\label{sec:cf_details_appendix}\n87\t\n88\tTable~\\ref{tab:counterfactual} reports per-condition counterfactual rankings for all eight cells where Cohen's $d$ is available.\n89\t\n90\t\\begin{table}[h]\n91\t\\centering\n92\t\\caption{Counterfactual condition ranking for trained models (Cohen's $d$; higher $=$ greater action divergence under instruction perturbation). Conditions: E $=$ empty instruction, W $=$ wrong task, S $=$ shuffled tokens, P $=$ paraphrased. ``Empty'' ranks first in 6 of 8 cells; OpenVLA Goal (``wrong'' first) and Long (``shuffled'' first) are the exceptions. NORA cells use cosine distance and are reported in \\S\\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}.}\n93\t\\label{tab:counterfactual}\n94\t\\small\n95\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n96\t\\toprule\n97\tArch. & Suite & 1st & 2nd & 3rd & 4th \\\\\n98\t\\midrule\n99\tOpenVLA & Goal    & W\\,1.18 & E\\,1.17 & S\\,1.10 & P\\,1.00 \\\\\n100\tOpenVLA & Spatial & E\\,1.17 & S\\,0.98 & W\\,0.89 & P\\,0.81 \\\\\n101\tOpenVLA & Object  & E\\,1.21 & W\\,1.19 & S\\,1.12 & P\\,0.90 \\\\\n102\tOpenVLA & Long    & S\\,0.57 & W\\,0.55 & E\\,0.51 & P\\,0.45 \\\\\n103\t\\midrule\n104\tTracVLA & Goal    & E\\,1.52 & W\\,1.40 & S\\,1.34 & P\\,1.21 \\\\\n105\tTracVLA & Spatial & E\\,1.33 & S\\,1.30 & P\\,1.18 & W\\,1.17 \\\\\n106\tTracVLA & Object  & E\\,1.21 & S\\,1.16 & W\\,1.06 & P\\,0.97 \\\\\n107\tTracVLA & Long    & E\\,1.47 & W\\,1.42 & S\\,1.39 & P\\,1.16 \\\\\n108\t\\bottomrule\n109\t\\end{tabular}\n110\t\\end{table}\n111\t\n112\t\\paragraph{Metric constraints on trained--reference comparison.}\n113\tTrained models produce action outputs, enabling Cohen's $d$ on action MSE (OpenVLA, TracVLA) or cosine distance on action tokens (NORA).\n114\tReference VLMs (Phi-3-Vision, Qwen2.5-VL) do not generate actions; their sensitivity is measured via cosine distance on hidden representations---a metric with different scale, units, and distributional properties.\n115\tRatios between Cohen's $d$ and cosine distance are therefore methodologically invalid, and we do not report cross-metric amplification factors.\n116\tQualitatively, reference VLMs show weak sensitivity: the TracVLA reference (Phi-3-Vision) produces peak cosine distances of $0.15$--$0.19$; the NORA reference (Qwen2.5-VL) yields $0.081$ on Goal.\n117\t\n118\t\\paragraph{NORA details.}\n119\tNORA Goal produces saturated exit-layer cosine distances of $1.0$ for all conditions, with differentiation visible only at earlier layers (L24: wrong $= 0.123$, empty $= 0.098$).\n120\tNORA Spatial produces a peak cosine distance of $0.275$ for the ``wrong'' condition.\n121\t\n122\t\\subsection{CKA Analysis Method}\n123\t\\label{sec:cka_appendix}\n124\t\n125\tWe employ Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA)~\\cite{kornblith2019} alongside RSA as a complementary perspective.\n126\tWhile RSA captures local geometric alignment via rank-order preservation, CKA measures global representational similarity through kernel-based comparisons.\n127\t\n128\tWe compute both \\emph{linear CKA}, which uses a linear kernel and is sensitive to second-order statistics, and \\emph{RDM kernel CKA}, which operates on the same distance matrices as RSA but aggregates them differently.\n129\tUsing both variants tests whether conclusions about representation structure are robust to the choice of similarity metric.\n130\t\n131\tLinear CKA between high-dimensional representations (4{,}096-D for LLM layers) and the 7-D action space is subject to a known dimension mismatch: the linear kernel in the low-dimensional space may not capture the same structure as in the high-dimensional space.\n132\tRDM kernel CKA avoids this issue by operating on distance matrices of equal size regardless of the original feature dimensionality.\n133\t\n134\t\\subsection{Metric Sensitivity: RSA versus CKA}\n135\tLinear CKA~\\cite{kornblith2019} reverses the RSA ordering: reference LLM ($\\text{CKA} = 0.089$) $>$ trained LLM ($0.083$) $>$ DinoSigLIP+Projector ($0.081$) $>$ DINOv2 ($0.066$), partially inverting the self-supervised advantage (Figure~\\ref{fig:metric_comparison}).\n136\tRDM kernel CKA partially resolves this discrepancy: trained ($0.260$) $\\approx$ Projector ($0.257$) $>$ reference ($0.246$) $>$ DINOv2 ($0.241$).\n137\tThis divergence reflects the known sensitivity of representation similarity metrics to different geometric properties.\n138\t\n139\tAt the per-layer level, linear CKA between image\\_mean and the action space rises monotonically from L0 ($0.081$) to L32 ($0.098$), with no inverted-U pattern.\n140\tRSA captures rank-order geometric alignment; CKA captures global linear structure.\n141\tThe dissociation indicates that the geometric refinement in early layers operates on the relative arrangement of representations (affecting RSA) without altering the overall covariance structure (captured by CKA).\n142\t\n143\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n144\t\\centering\n145\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig6.pdf}\n146\t\\caption{Three similarity metrics across representation sources. \\textbf{(a)}~RSA ranks DINOv2 cls ($0.692$) $>$ Fused ($0.634$) $>$ Projector ($0.615$). \\textbf{(b)}~Linear CKA reverses the ordering: Reference ($0.089$) $>$ Trained ($0.083$) $>$ Projector ($0.081$) $>$ DINOv2 ($0.066$). \\textbf{(c)}~RDM kernel CKA: Trained ($0.260$) $\\approx$ Projector ($0.257$) $>$ Reference ($0.246$) $>$ DINOv2 ($0.241$). The metric divergence underscores that RSA and CKA capture different aspects of representational structure.}\n147\t\\label{fig:metric_comparison}\n148\t\\end{figure}\n149\t\n150\t\\subsection{Branch-Split Detailed Analysis}\n151\tTo disentangle the contributions of the two encoder streams within DinoSigLIP, we extract representations from the DINOv2 branch (1{,}024-D) and SigLIP branch (1{,}152-D) separately before fusion.\n152\tThe two branches yield near-identical RSA: DINOv2 branch $\\rho = 0.619$, SigLIP branch $\\rho = 0.621$.\n153\tFusion produces a representation ($\\rho = 0.634$) that exceeds either branch alone; concatenation captures complementary structure.\n154\tThe MLP projector then compresses this signal: DinoSigLIP+Projector achieves $\\rho = 0.615$, a drop of $\\Delta = 0.019$ from the fused 2{,}176-D input.\n155\t\n156\t\\subsection{RSA Across Representation Sources}\n157\tFigure~\\ref{fig:rsa_bar} shows RSA with the action space across all representation sources; the ordering matches the RSA results reported in \\S\\ref{sec:routing_synthesis}.\n158\t\n159\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n160\t\\centering\n161\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig2.pdf}\n162\t\\caption{RSA with the action space ($\\rho$) across representation sources. Self-supervised DINOv2 \\texttt{cls\\_token} ($\\rho{=}0.692$) achieves the highest geometric alignment. CLIP ($0.557$) yields the weakest alignment among pretrained encoders. Error bars: 95\\% bootstrap CIs.}\n163\t\\label{fig:rsa_bar}\n164\t\\end{figure}\n165\t\n166\t\\subsection{Per-Layer RSA Profiles}\n167\tFigure~\\ref{fig:rsa_perlayer_curve} extends Figure~\\ref{fig:r2_collapse} (main text) by adding readout and reference profiles.\n168\t\n169\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n170\t\\centering\n171\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_rsa_perlayer_curve.pdf}\n172\t\\caption{Per-layer RSA ($\\rho$) for trained and reference models at image\\_mean and readout positions. The trained model shows an inverted-U peaking at L12 for both positions; the reference model shows monotonic decline. After L12, readout and image\\_mean diverge: readout $\\rho$ declines gradually ($0.628 \\to 0.584$) while image\\_mean $\\rho$ drops sharply ($0.628 \\to 0.422$), consistent with task-specific conditioning at the readout position.}\n173\t\\label{fig:rsa_perlayer_curve}\n174\t\\end{figure}\n175\t\n176\t\\subsection{Per-Layer Action Decoding Across Training Stages}\n177\t\\label{sec:action_r2_threecond}\n178\t\n179\tFigure~\\ref{fig:action_r2_threecond} compares per-layer action probe $R^2$ across three training stages (randomly initialized, Llama-2 base, VLA-trained) using 50 demonstrations per task, averaged over 3 seeds (mean $\\pm$ std).\n180\tAll three conditions share L0 representations ($R^2 = 0.645 \\pm 0.008$); subsequent differences thus arise from Transformer processing rather than input variation.\n181\tThe trained model peaks at L9 ($R^2 = 0.643 \\pm 0.014$), consistent with progressive specialization for action-relevant feature integration.\n182\tLlama-2 base shows a steady decline (L0: $0.645 \\to$ L32: $0.551$, std $0.003$--$0.035$), consistent with stable but unspecialized processing.\n183\tThe randomly initialized model exhibits high cross-seed variance and deep-layer collapse, with negative $R^2$ and bimodal behavior across seeds at late layers; this instability indicates that randomly initialized Transformer layers produce destructive interference on action-relevant structure.\n184\tRandom weights at intermediate layers produce anti-correlated representations ($R^2 < 0$, L10--L11), consistent with randomly initialized networks lacking structured computation.\n185\tThe three-condition gradient (trained $>$ Llama-2 base $>$ randomly initialized) holds from L1 through L15, with a reversal at L32 where trained $R^2$ drops below the pretrained baseline.\n186\tThe qualitative pattern is consistent across all 3 seeds.\n187\tThree conditions show substantial agreement in layer-wise $R^2$ ordering (Kendall's $W = 0.72$, $\\chi^2 = 68.99$, $p < 0.001$).\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_action_r2_threecond.pdf}\n192\t\\caption{Per-layer action probe $R^2$ (image\\_mean) across three training conditions: randomly initialized VLA (random weights), Llama-2 base (pretrained, no VLA fine-tuning), and VLA-trained. All conditions share L0 ($0.645 \\pm 0.008$). Trained model peaks at L9; Llama-2 base declines steadily; randomly initialized shows high cross-seed variance and deep-layer collapse. 50 demonstrations per task, 3 seeds (mean $\\pm$ std).}\n193\t\\label{fig:action_r2_threecond}\n194\t\\end{figure}\n195\t\n196\t\\subsection{Random-Subset Ablation Details}\n197\tTo evaluate whether the ablation effect in \\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing} is specific to instruction-dominant heads, we compare dominant-head ablation against 30 random subsets of 24/32 L1 heads (each subset drawn without replacement).\n198\tDominant-head ablation increases action $R^2$ by $+0.030$ (within-experiment baseline $0.524$), while random subsets yield a mean increase of $+0.071 \\pm 0.013$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:random_subset_ablation}).\n199\tThe dominant-head effect falls below all 30 random subsets ($z = -3.11$, percentile $= 0\\%$); heads with higher instruction-attention fraction carry more organized attention signals, consistent with graded rather than binary specialization.\n200\t\n201\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n202\t\\centering\n203\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_specificity_bootstrap_30seeds.pdf}\n204\t\\caption{$R^2$ increase from ablating 24/32 L1 heads. Gray bars: 30 random subsets; red line: dominant-24 heads ($+0.030$). Random subsets yield larger gains (mean $+0.071 \\pm 0.013$; $z = -3.11$, percentile $= 0\\%$), consistent with graded attention specialization across L1 heads.}\n205\t\\label{fig:random_subset_ablation}\n206\t\\end{figure}\n207\t\n208\t\\subsection{Action Trajectory Analysis Details}\n209\t\\label{sec:action_trajectory}\n210\t\n211\tTable~\\ref{tab:action_shift_perdim} reports per-dimension mean absolute shift for instruction-dominant (24 heads) and control (8 heads) L1 ablations.\n212\tBoth conditions produce negligible shifts on translational dimensions ($dx$, $dy$, $dz$ $< 0.01$) and small rotational shifts ($rx$, $ry$, $rz$ $< 0.04$).\n213\tThe gripper dimension absorbs the majority of the effect: instruction-head ablation shifts gripper predictions by $0.291$ (control: $0.141$), consistent with L1 attention influencing discrete open/close decisions.\n214\t\n215\tPer-task Cohen's $d$ values for instruction-head ablation are $0.43$, $0.69$, $0.70$, $0.70$, and $0.73$ (mean $0.65$), with all tasks significant ($p < 10^{-10}$).\n216\tToken-level KL divergence between original and ablated action distributions is $5.08$ for instruction heads and $1.69$ for control heads.\n217\t\n218\t\\paragraph{Trajectory-level aggregation.}\n219\tAggregating per-demo mean actions to the trajectory level reveals that token-level $d$ values are conservative: within-trajectory averaging reduces noise, yielding ${\\sim}4\\times$ larger effect sizes (mean token-to-trajectory ratio $0.245$).\n220\tInstruction-head ablation ($N = 50$: 5 tasks $\\times$ 10 demos) yields trajectory $d = 2.943$ (95\\% CI $[2.469, 3.707]$); bottom-24 non-instruction heads ($N = 30$) yield $d = 2.347$ (CI $[1.854, 3.221]$).\n221\tAll 14 conditions produce trajectory $d > 2.0$ with CI lower bounds $> 1.7$.\n222\tCounterfactual trajectory-level $d$ (10 LIBERO-Goal tasks, $N = 100$): wrong $3.798$ (CI $[3.194, 4.938]$), empty $3.438$ ($[2.921, 4.448]$), shuffled $2.595$ ($[2.028, 3.791]$), paraphrased $2.131$ ($[1.740, 2.819]$).\n223\tToken-level $d$ underestimates trajectory-level effects by ${\\times}3.1$--$4.5$, consistent with within-trajectory averaging reducing noise.\n224\tFine-grained ordering is not reliably distinguished at trajectory level ($N = 100$); token-level comparisons ($N {\\sim} 5{,}000$) provide higher resolution for ordering claims.\n225\t\n226\t\\paragraph{Ground-truth action error.}\n227\tAblation redirects model outputs ($d = 0.65$) but does not systematically increase error relative to expert demonstrations: original MSE $= 0.498$; top-24 instruction-head ablation MSE $= 0.497$ ($\\Delta = -0.001$, Wilcoxon $p = 0.52$, n.s.); bottom-24 non-instruction heads MSE $= 0.506$ ($\\Delta = +0.008$).\n228\tChanges concentrate on the gripper dimension; translation and rotation dimensions are negligibly affected.\n229\tPer-task breakdown shows all $|\\Delta\\text{MSE}| < 3\\%$ of baseline with all $p > 0.27$; the near-zero aggregate $\\Delta$ reflects uniformly small per-task effects rather than cancellation across tasks.\n230\tThis is consistent with the information-computation dissociation: instruction-dominant heads control instruction-specific direction selection rather than action precision.\n231\t\n232\tTable~\\ref{tab:perhead_bootstrap} reports per-head bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples).\n233\tIndividual control heads produce ${\\sim}1.9\\times$ stronger effects ($d = 0.051$) than instruction heads ($d = 0.027$, $p = 0.0018$), consistent across all 5 tasks.\n234\tThe 24 instruction heads nonetheless produce a larger aggregate perturbation ($d = 0.65$ vs.\\ $0.41$), consistent with collective coordination rather than individual strength.\n235\t\n236\t\\begin{table}[h]\n237\t\\centering\n238\t\\caption{Per-head bootstrap analysis of L1 ablation effects. Instruction heads are individually weaker but collectively stronger, consistent with coordinated attention redistribution.}\n239\t\\label{tab:perhead_bootstrap}\n240\t\\small\n241\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n242\t\\toprule\n243\tGroup & $N$ heads & Collective $d$ & Per-head $d$ [95\\% CI] \\\\\n244\t\\midrule\n245\tInstruction-dominant & 24 & 0.65 & 0.027 [0.026, 0.029] \\\\\n246\tControl & 8 & 0.41 & 0.051 [0.048, 0.055] \\\\\n247\t\\midrule\n248\tDifference & --- & --- & 0.024, $p = 0.0018$ \\\\\n249\t\\bottomrule\n250\t\\end{tabular}\n251\t\\end{table}\n252\t\n253\tInstruction-head ablation produces more uniform perturbation across action dimensions (CV${}=1.30$ vs.\\ control CV${}=2.74$, permutation $p = 0.016$), consistent with structured, language-conditioned computation rather than diffuse noise.\n254\t\n255\tTable~\\ref{tab:dose_response} and Figure~\\ref{fig:dose_response} report the dose-response gradient for instruction-head ablation at L1.\n256\tTrajectory divergence increases monotonically from $d = 0.482$ (4 heads) to $d = 0.639$ (32 heads), with steep rise through 12 heads (slope $\\Delta d / \\Delta 4 \\approx 0.026$), a plateau at 16--20 heads ($\\Delta d = 0.001$), and slow increase at 20--32.\n257\t\n258\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n259\t\\centering\n260\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}\n261\t\\caption{Dose-response gradient: trajectory divergence (Cohen's $d$) as a function of ablated instruction-dominant heads at L1. Error bars: 95\\% bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples). Dashed red: control ablation (8 non-instruction heads, $d = 0.41$). Steep rise through 12 heads, plateau at 16--20, diminishing returns beyond.}\n262\t\\label{fig:dose_response}\n263\t\\end{figure}\n264\t\n265\t\\begin{table}[h]\n266\t\\centering\n267\t\\caption{Dose-response data for L1 instruction-head ablation. Cohen's $d$ vs.\\ original action sequence; 95\\% bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples).}\n268\t\\label{tab:dose_response}\n269\t\\small\n270\t\\begin{tabular}{rcc}\n271\t\\toprule\n272\tHeads ablated & Cohen's $d$ & 95\\% CI \\\\\n273\t\\midrule\n274\t4 & 0.482 & [0.449, 0.511] \\\\\n275\t8 & 0.508 & [0.479, 0.541] \\\\\n276\t12 & 0.560 & [0.529, 0.593] \\\\\n277\t16 & 0.592 & [0.559, 0.625] \\\\\n278\t20 & 0.593 & [0.559, 0.627] \\\\\n279\t24 & 0.611 & [0.578, 0.645] \\\\\n280\t28 & 0.619 & [0.587, 0.654] \\\\\n281\t32 & 0.639 & [0.603, 0.671] \\\\\n282\t\\bottomrule\n283\t\\end{tabular}\n284\t\\end{table}\n285\t\n286\t\\begin{table}[h]\n287\t\\centering\n288\t\\caption{Counterfactual instruction analysis across all 10 LIBERO-Goal tasks. Jaccard: token overlap with original instruction; Cohen's $d$: mean across 10 tasks. ``Wrong'' slightly exceeds ``empty'' ($d = 1.182$ vs.\\ $1.170$); all four conditions produce large effects ($d \\geq 1.0$). L1 ablation included for comparison.}\n289\t\\small\n290\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n291\t\\toprule\n292\tCondition & Jaccard & Cohen's $d$ \\\\\n293\t\\midrule\n294\tEmpty (no instruction) & 0.000 & 1.170 \\\\\n295\tWrong (different task) & 0.174 & 1.182 \\\\\n296\tShuffled (random order) & 1.000 & 1.096 \\\\\n297\tParaphrased & 0.533 & 0.996 \\\\\n298\t\\midrule\n299\tL1 ablation (24 heads) & --- & 0.650 \\\\\n300\t\\bottomrule\n301\t\\end{tabular}\n302\t\\end{table}\n303\t\n304\t\\begin{table}[h]\n305\t\\centering\n306\t\\caption{Mean absolute action shift per dimension under L1 head ablation. Instruction: 24 dominant heads; Control: 8 remaining heads. Gripper shift dominates both conditions.}\n307\t\\label{tab:action_shift_perdim}\n308\t\\small\n309\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n310\t\\toprule\n311\tCondition & $dx$ & $dy$ & $dz$ & $rx$ & $ry$ & $rz$ & Gripper \\\\\n312\t\\midrule\n313\tInstruction (24) & 0.002 & 0.005 & 0.008 & 0.021 & 0.031 & 0.034 & 0.291 \\\\\n314\tControl (8) & 0.000 & 0.001 & 0.002 & 0.008 & 0.014 & 0.018 & 0.141 \\\\\n315\t\\bottomrule\n316\t\\end{tabular}\n317\t\\end{table}\n318\t\n319\t\\subsection{Pretrained LLM Representation Quality Under Visual Input}\n320\tA potential concern with the three-condition comparison (\\S\\ref{sec:arch_data}) is that the pretrained Llama-2 backbone never encountered projected visual tokens during pretraining, so its representations of such input could be degenerate.\n321\tWe evaluate three diagnostics to assess representation quality.\n322\tActivation magnitude grows from $16.5$ (L0) to $90.2$ (L31) for Llama-2 base, compared to $16.5 \\to 51.7$ for the VLA-trained model; the pretrained LLM produces larger but non-exploding activations with no evidence of collapse.\n323\tPer-layer cosine similarity between L0 and deeper layers decreases progressively from $1.0$ to $\\approx 0.28$ at L25, consistent with gradual differentiation rather than catastrophic out-of-distribution behavior.\n324\tTop-10 principal component variance explained remains stable across all layers ($0.59$--$0.74$), with no low-rank collapse.\n325\tThese diagnostics indicate that the pretrained LLM produces structured, full-rank representations of visual input despite never encountering projected visual tokens during pretraining (Figure~\\ref{fig:ood_diagnostics}). These results support the validity of the three-condition comparison.\n326\t\n327\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n328\t\\centering\n329\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_ood_diagnostics.pdf}\n330\t\\caption{Pretrained LLM representation diagnostics under visual input. Left: activation magnitude across layers (Llama-2 base vs.\\ VLA-trained). Center: cosine similarity between L0 and deeper layers. Right: top-10 PC variance explained. All three metrics indicate structured, non-degenerate representations.}\n331\t\\label{fig:ood_diagnostics}\n332\t\\end{figure}\n333\t\n334\t\\subsection{Probe Architecture Sensitivity}\n335\t\\label{sec:linear_probe}\n336\t\n337\tTo rule out that our findings depend on MLP probe capacity ($N/\\text{params} \\approx 0.06$, overparameterized), we replicate the per-layer analysis with a linear probe ($4{,}096 \\to 7$, $N/\\text{params} \\approx 2.2$).\n338\tThe inverted-U pattern reproduces: trained image\\_mean peaks at L14 ($R^2 = 0.565$), while the reference declines monotonically from L0 ($R^2 = 0.517$).\n339\tThe trained--reference gap is larger under the linear probe (L14: $\\Delta = +0.324$) than the MLP probe (L8: $\\Delta = +0.015$); trained representations are thus more probe-decodable for action information.\n340\tReference deep layers collapse entirely under the linear probe (L25: $R^2 = -0.298$); MLP nonlinearity partially rescues these degraded representations.\n341\tFor readout tokens, the linear probe also corroborates the inverted-U (peak L13, $R^2 = 0.596$), with the same peak layer as the MLP probe and a trained-vs-reference gap of $\\Delta = 0.103$ ($p < 0.001$).\n342\tThese results indicate that our qualitative findings reflect representational structure rather than probe capacity artifacts (Figure~\\ref{fig:linear_vs_mlp}).\n343\t\n344\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n345\t\\centering\n346\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_linear_vs_mlp_probe.pdf}\n347\t\\caption{Per-layer action probe $R^2$ for linear (dashed) and MLP (solid) probes across trained (blue) and reference (red) conditions. The inverted-U reproduces under both probe architectures; the trained--reference gap is larger for linear probes.}\n348\t\\label{fig:linear_vs_mlp}\n349\t\\end{figure}\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{Functional Probe Comparison}\n352\tFigure~\\ref{fig:probe_bars} compares functional probe $R^2$ across representation sources at each source's best layer.\n353\t\n354\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n355\t\\centering\n356\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig4.pdf}\n357\t\\caption{Functional probe $R^2$ for action prediction (best layer per source). Trained readout ($R^2{=}0.678$, L13) and trained image\\_mean ($0.655$, L8) exceed the projector baseline ($0.630$); the inverted-U extends to functional capacity.}\n358\t\\label{fig:probe_bars}\n359\t\\end{figure}\n360\t\n361\t\\subsection{Training Effect and Instruction Invariance}\n362\tFigure~\\ref{fig:factorial} shows trained vs.\\ reference RSA profiles and instruction invariance.\n363\tInstruction invariance holds across all conditions: maximum $|\\Delta| < 0.006$, so action geometry is visually grounded and not modulated by language instructions.\n364\t\n365\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n366\t\\centering\n367\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig5.pdf}\n368\t\\caption{\\textbf{(a)}~Trained vs.\\ reference image\\_mean RSA profiles. Training shifts the peak from L0 ($0.612$) to L12 ($0.628$), yielding a modest best-vs-best gain ($\\Delta{=}+0.017$, $3\\%$). \\textbf{(b)}~Instruction invariance: all conditions within $|\\Delta|{<}0.006$; action geometry is visually grounded.}\n369\t\\label{fig:factorial}\n370\t\\end{figure}\n371\t\n372\t\\subsection{Additional RSA Values}\n373\tDINOv2 \\texttt{patch\\_mean} achieves RSA $\\rho = 0.631$, between the \\texttt{cls\\_token} ($0.692$) and the fused DinoSigLIP ($0.634$).\n374\tVLA reference readout peaks at L1 ($\\rho = 0.626$).\n375\t\n376\t\\subsection{Cross-Suite Validation within LIBERO: Spatial}\n377\t\\label{sec:cross_benchmark_appendix}\n378\t\n379\tTo assess generalization beyond in-distribution tasks, we repeat the core RSA and probe analyses on LIBERO-Spatial (10 out-of-distribution tasks $\\times$ 50 demonstrations each, requiring spatial reasoning).\n380\tFigure~\\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} shows per-layer RSA and $R^2$ profiles for both LIBERO-Goal and LIBERO-Spatial side by side.\n381\t\n382\tThe inverted-U profile replicates on LIBERO-Spatial: trained image\\_mean RSA peaks at L14 ($\\rho = 0.691$) and $R^2$ at L13 ($0.696$), with peaks shifting ${\\sim}2$ layers deeper than LIBERO-Goal (RSA L12, $R^2$ L8--L10), consistent with increased task difficulty requiring deeper processing.\n383\tTraining produces a significant geometric gain (best-vs-best $\\Delta = +0.023$, 95\\% CI $[0.012, 0.036]$, $p < 0.001$), larger than the LIBERO-Goal effect ($\\Delta = +0.017$, CI includes zero).\n384\t\n385\tTwo notable differences are observed.\n386\tFirst, the absolute RSA values are higher on Spatial ($0.691$ vs.\\ $0.628$), possibly reflecting greater action diversity in spatial tasks that produces a more discriminative action RDM.\n387\tSecond, RSA--$R^2$ peak convergence does not replicate: readout RSA peaks at L15 while $R^2$ peaks at L21 (6-layer gap), contrasting with the near-alignment on Goal ($|\\Delta_\\text{layer}| = 1$).\n388\tThis suggests that peak convergence is benchmark-specific rather than a general consequence of training.\n389\t\n390\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n391\t\\centering\n392\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig7.pdf}\n393\t\\caption{Cross-suite validation: per-layer RSA and $R^2$ profiles on LIBERO-Goal (left) vs.\\ LIBERO-Spatial (right). The inverted-U replicates across suites. LIBERO-Spatial shows a significant training effect (RSA best-vs-best $\\Delta = +0.023$, $p < 0.001$) and deeper peak layers (RSA L14, $R^2$ L13), but RSA--$R^2$ peak convergence does not replicate (6-layer gap).}\n394\t\\label{fig:cross_benchmark}\n395\t\\end{figure}\n396\t\n397\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Suite Counterfactual Replication}\n398\tTable~\\ref{tab:counterfactual_spatial} reports counterfactual instruction analysis on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks).\n399\tThe ordering differs from LIBERO-Goal: empty $>$ shuffled $>$ wrong $>$ paraphrased (vs.\\ wrong $>$ empty $>$ shuffled $>$ paraphrased on Goal).\n400\tAll global effect sizes exceed $d = 0.8$ (per-task wrong minimum is $0.591$).\n401\tSpatial instructions differ only in prepositions (e.g., ``on the plate'' vs.\\ ``in the drawer''); the wrong-task $d = 0.885$ suggests that instruction-dominant heads encode fine-grained spatial relations, not merely task identity.\n402\t\n403\t\\begin{table}[h]\n404\t\\centering\n405\t\\caption{Counterfactual instruction analysis on LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks). Ordering: empty $>$ shuffled $>$ wrong $>$ paraphrased, differing from Goal. All global $d > 0.8$.}\n406\t\\label{tab:counterfactual_spatial}\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n409\t\\toprule\n410\tCondition & Jaccard & Cohen's $d$ & CV \\\\\n411\t\\midrule\n412\tEmpty (no instruction) & 0.000 & 1.173 & 0.087 \\\\\n413\tWrong (different task) & 0.174 & 0.885 & 0.272 \\\\\n414\tShuffled (random order) & 1.000 & 0.979 & 0.162 \\\\\n415\tParaphrased & 0.533 & 0.807 & 0.179 \\\\\n416\t\\bottomrule\n417\t\\end{tabular}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\t\\subsubsection{Cross-Instruction Probe on LIBERO-Spatial}\n421\tTable~\\ref{tab:spatial_cross_instruction} reports cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy on LIBERO-Spatial.\n422\tThe core pattern from LIBERO-Goal replicates: image\\_mean remains at chance (${\\sim}10\\%$) across all layers and conditions, while the trained readout token saturates to $100\\%$ by L4.\n423\tThe reference readout decays more steeply than on LIBERO-Goal ($75.4\\%$ at L31 vs.\\ $82\\%$), consistent with greater task difficulty.\n424\t\n425\t\\begin{table}[h]\n426\t\\centering\n427\t\\caption{Cross-instruction 10-way classification accuracy on LIBERO-Spatial. Trained readout achieves $100\\%$ from L4; image\\_mean remains at chance. Reference readout decays more steeply than on LIBERO-Goal (cf.\\ \\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}).}\n428\t\\label{tab:spatial_cross_instruction}\n429\t\\small\n430\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n431\t\\toprule\n432\tToken & Condition & L1 & L4 & L8 & L16 & L31 \\\\\n433\t\\midrule\n434\treadout & Trained & 98.9\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% & 100.0\\% \\\\\n435\treadout & Reference & 99.5\\% & 98.3\\% & 85.7\\% & 68.3\\% & 75.4\\% \\\\\n436\timage\\_mean & Trained & 10.2\\% & 10.0\\% & 9.9\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.1\\% \\\\\n437\timage\\_mean & Reference & 10.1\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.0\\% & 10.2\\% \\\\\n438\t\\bottomrule\n439\t\\end{tabular}\n440\t\\end{table}\n441\t\n442\t\\subsection{Robustness Analyses}\n443\t\\subsubsection{Distance Metric Invariance}\n444\tThe inverted-U profile is robust to distance metric choice: cosine action distance yields identical peak layers (L12) and Pearson correlation $r > 0.989$ with the Euclidean profile reported in the main text (Table~\\ref{tab:metric_invariance}).\n445\t\n446\t\\begin{table}[h]\n447\t\\centering\n448\t\\caption{Peak layer comparison between Euclidean and cosine action distances. Profile $r$ denotes Pearson correlation across all 32 layers.}\n449\t\\label{tab:metric_invariance}\n450\t\\small\n451\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n452\t\\toprule\n453\tCondition & Eucl.\\ Peak & Cos.\\ Peak & Profile $r$ \\\\\n454\t\\midrule\n455\tTrained image\\_mean & L12 & L12 & 0.990 \\\\\n456\tTrained readout & L12 & L13 & 0.990 \\\\\n457\tReference image\\_mean & L0 & L0 & 0.995 \\\\\n458\tReference readout & L1 & L1 & 0.992 \\\\\n459\t\\bottomrule\n460\t\\end{tabular}\n461\t\\end{table}\n462\t\n463\t\\subsubsection{Per-DOF Decomposition}\n464\tRSA decomposition by action dimension indicates the inverted-U is not driven by any single degree of freedom: xyz translation (peak L13, $\\rho = 0.193$), rotation (peak L12, $\\rho = 0.175$), and gripper (peak L13, $\\rho = 0.106$) all exhibit non-monotonic profiles.\n465\tPer-DOF RSA values are lower than the 7-D RSA because single-dimension distances carry less discriminative information than the full action space.\n466\t\n467\t\\subsubsection{Permutation Test}\n468\tPermutation testing (10{,}000 shuffles) indicates all observed RSA values are highly significant: observed $\\rho$ ($0.42$--$0.63$) exceeds the null distribution maximum ($0.08$--$0.10$) by $15$--$23$ standard deviations (all $p < 0.0001$).\n469\t\n470\t\\subsubsection{Probe Seed Sensitivity}\n471\tAction probe $R^2$ is highly stable across random seeds.\n472\tOver 5 seeds (trained image\\_mean, full-layer scan), per-layer $R^2$ standard deviation is uniformly below $0.01$ (mean $0.004$), and the peak $R^2$ varies by less than $0.4\\%$ ($0.655 \\pm 0.002$; Table~\\ref{tab:seed_sensitivity}).\n473\tPeak layer concentrates in the L8--L10 region ($4/5$ seeds); the inverted-U shape is not a seed artifact.\n474\tSeed 1 peaks at L16, but with $R^2$ difference ${<}0.002$. The inverted-U plateau (L8--L16) is sufficiently flat that argmax is sensitive to minor noise while the profile shape is invariant.\n475\tA 15-seed replication confirms stability: trained peak remains at L9 ($R^2 = 0.637 \\pm 0.004$), with all trained/Llama-2-base layers showing CI width ${<}0.02$; the randomly initialized condition exhibits higher variance (std $0.185$ at peak), consistent with random initialization instability.\n476\tFixed-layer analysis at L9 yields $R^2 = 0.637 \\pm 0.008$ (trained) and $0.635 \\pm 0.011$ (Llama-2 base), confirming the inverted-U pattern.\n477\tThe per-seed peak method reports higher absolute values ($0.655$) because each seed selects its optimal layer independently; both methods agree on the qualitative pattern and the trained $>$ randomly initialized ordering.\n478\t\n479\t\\begin{table}[h]\n480\t\\centering\n481\t\\caption{Probe $R^2$ peak across 5 random seeds (trained image\\_mean).}\n482\t\\label{tab:seed_sensitivity}\n483\t\\small\n484\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n485\t\\toprule\n486\tSeed & Peak Layer & Peak $R^2$ \\\\\n487\t\\midrule\n488\t0 & L9 & 0.654 \\\\\n489\t1 & L16 & 0.656 \\\\\n490\t2 & L9 & 0.653 \\\\\n491\t3 & L10 & 0.655 \\\\\n492\t42 & L9 & 0.655 \\\\\n493\t\\midrule\n494\tMean$\\pm$std & $10.6\\pm2.7$ & $0.655\\pm0.002$ \\\\\n495\t\\bottomrule\n496\t\\end{tabular}\n497\t\\end{table}\n498\t\n499\t\\paragraph{Cross-architecture $\\Delta R^2$ seed sensitivity.}\n500\tTo verify that $\\Delta R^2$ values are not sensitive to probe initialization, we retrain MLP probes with 4 different random seeds (0, 1, 2, 42) on three representative cells."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TM3u5QfQkhWB8okD6Pp3QB","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"8ea63706-e8d8-47ba-8a97-0be8d9629641","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:19:03.985Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X6z8S8HdPpdTevvoErGKt8","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/report.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Handover — 2026-05-26\\\\n\\\\n> 由 Agent 生成，每次状态变更后自动更新。硬限 150 行。\\\\n\\\\n## 运行中\\\\n（无）\\\\n\\\\n## 待处理\\\\n- tracvla_spatial_full_v2: TraceVLA (Phi-3-mini) on LIBERO-Spatial shows the convergent entry-exit routing pattern when fine-tuned, and the untrained base model (Phi-3-Vision) lacks this pattern. Cross-suite validation for second architecture.（已分配 ? GPU:?，等待提交）\\\\n- l1_head_ablation_control8（准备中）\\\\n- fulllayer_action_probe（准备中）\\\\n- behavioral_pilot: Ablating L1 instruction-dominant heads during VLA inference will measurably change task success rate, providing behavioral evidence that these heads serve a functional role beyond representational reorganization measured by probes.（准备中）\\\\n- behavioral_pilot_v2: Ablating L1 instruction-dominant heads (24/32) during VLA inference on LIBERO-Goal fine-tuned OpenVLA reduces task success rate, providing behavioral evidence that attention head specialization has functional consequences for task execution.（准备中）\\\\n- behavioral_smoketest_diag（准备中）\\\\n- libero_finetuned_probe（准备中）\\\\n- tracvla_spatial_trained（准备中）\\\\n- bootstrap_v3_cuda1（准备中）\\\\n- bootstrap_v3_cuda0（准备中）\\\\n- …另有 1 项\\\\n\\\\n## 近期结论\\\\n- **action_head_decoding_openvla_goal_untrained** (success): Untrained OpenVLA (OXE-pretrained, no LIBERO fine-tuning) should NOT show exit-peaking action-head R² because the LM head has not been specialized for LIBERO action decoding. If trained model shows exit-peak and untrained does not, this dissociation confirms format specialization is a training-induced effect, not an architectural artifact.\\\\n  [openvla_goal_untrained_50demos_action_head_r2](/api/workspace-files/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/action_head_decoding_openvla_goal_untrained/data/openvla_goal_untrained_50demos_action_head_r2.json) | [untrained_v2_action_head_r2](/api/workspace-files/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/action_head_decoding_openvla_goal_untrained/data/untrained_v2_action_head_r2.txt)\\\\n- **action_head_decoding_openvla_goal** (negative): Exit-layer ΔR² drop in VLM→VLA models reflects format specialization (exit representations are optimized for the action head's specific decoding format) rather than genuine information loss. If true, applying the frozen action head to each layer's hidden state should show monotonically increasing or exit-peaking R², while independent probes show mid-layer peak + exit drop — the dissociation between the two curves is direct evidence of format specialization.\\\\n  [action_head_v2_trained_r2](/api/workspace-files/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/action_head_decoding_openvla_goal/data/trained_action_head_r2.json)\\\\n- **qwen_nlp_control** (success): NLP instruction-tuning of Qwen2.5-3B backbone (Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct) produces flat R² action-probe profile with ΔR²≈0, confirming exit collapse is specific to VLA action training — extending NLP controls to the third backbone family (Qwen, shared with NORA), closing the stated limitation in discussion.tex:23\\\\n\\\\n## 趋势\\\\n成功 108 / 负向 18 / 失败 7 / 共 145\\\\nn_samples: 5000 →（近 3 次 success）\\\\n\\\\n## 下一步\\\\n（无活跃 idea，请通过 ideas.md 添加）\\\\n\\\\n## 资源状态\\\\nwestd-19183: 无 GPU 信息\\\\n\\\\n## 告警\\\\n- mvp2_action_probe: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- lg_extract_trained_r2: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- lg_extract_untrained_r2: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- lg_extract_trained_r1: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- lg_extract_untrained_r1: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- lg_shuffled_token_control: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- lg_instruction_ablation: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- lg_output_head_analysis: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- threecond_multiseed_r2: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- behavioral_pilot_w4: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- libero_object_full_pipeline: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- tracvla_spatial_pipeline: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- nora_goal_full_pipeline: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- nora_goal_pipeline_v2: 已被终止 — unknown\\\\n- object_pipeline_ridge: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- nora_spatial_pipeline_v1: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- openvla_long_pipeline_v1: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- clustered_bootstrap_reanalysis_v1: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n- clustered_bootstrap_reanalysis_v2: 训练失败 — unknown\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 3815, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4Hb9JbcXyLY73TkTTPeEh","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"574c1836-d740-4c55-8822-1ecbe2691349","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:19:03.833Z"}
