{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TZiAQKFZcPp1o6kEWcCCAQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Method}\n2\t\\label{sec:method}\n3\t\n4\tWe investigate how action-relevant representations are organized across three VLA architectures using complementary probing, attention, and perturbation analyses.\n5\t\n6\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n7\t\\subsection{Architectures and Data}\n8\t\\label{sec:arch_data}\n9\t\n10\tWe study three VLAs that span distinct backbone families (Table~\\ref{tab:arch_overview}).\n11\t\\textbf{OpenVLA}~\\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024} chains a frozen DinoSigLIP vision backbone~\\cite{zhai2023_siglip,oquab2024_dinov2} (DINOv2 ViT-L/14 + SigLIP-SO400M), an MLP projector, and Llama-2-7B~\\cite{touvron2023_llama2} (6.74B parameters, 32 layers, 32 multi-head attention [MHA] heads) for autoregressive 7-D action decoding.\n12\tIt is pretrained on Open X-Embodiment~\\cite{padalkar2023_oxe} (OXE; 970K episodes across multiple robot platforms).\n13\t\\textbf{TracVLA}~\\cite{zheng2024tracevla} pairs a SigLIP encoder with Phi-3-mini~\\cite{abdin2024phi3} (3.8B parameters, 32 layers, 32 MHA heads).\n14\tTracVLA is trained on an OXE subset with spatial trace conditioning.\n15\t\\textbf{NORA}~\\cite{hung2025nora} uses Qwen2.5-VL-3B ($\\sim$3B parameters, 36 layers, grouped-query attention [GQA] with 16 query heads and 2 KV heads) with the native Qwen2.5-VL vision encoder, which processes images at dynamic resolution without a separate projection stage.\n16\t\n17\tEach architecture is evaluated in trained and reference conditions; we define the \\emph{reference checkpoint} as the corresponding pretrained model before LIBERO fine-tuning.\n18\tA critical asymmetry distinguishes the reference baselines: the OpenVLA reference is an OXE-pretrained VLA, already fine-tuned for action prediction on 970K episodes, while the TracVLA and NORA references are pure vision-language models (Phi-3-Vision and Qwen2.5-VL) with no action training.\n19\tOpenVLA trained-vs-reference comparisons therefore measure a VLA-to-VLA transition; TracVLA and NORA comparisons measure VLM-to-VLA transitions.\n20\tFor OpenVLA, two additional conditions support deep-dive analyses (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}): the Llama-2 base language model and a random-weight initialization.\n21\t\n22\tWe evaluate on LIBERO~\\cite{liu2023_libero}, comprising four task suites (Goal, Spatial, Object, Long-Horizon) with 10 tasks and 50 demonstrations each.\n23\tThe three architectures and four suites form a $3 \\times 4$ matrix; 10 of 12 cells are filled (NORA Object and Long-Horizon lack available checkpoints).\n24\t\n25\tArchitecture details appear in Table~\\ref{tab:arch_overview} (Appendix).\n26\t\n27\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n28\t\\subsection{Representation Extraction}\n29\tFrom each architecture, we extract hidden states at every transformer layer from two token positions: (a)~\\textbf{image\\_mean}, the mean activation over image tokens, and (b)~\\textbf{readout}, the hidden state at the final pre-action token, which follows both image and instruction tokens in the causal sequence and can attend to all preceding context.\n30\tL0 denotes the embedding layer output, L1 through L$N$ the hidden states after each transformer layer ($N = 32$ for OpenVLA and TracVLA, $N = 36$ for NORA), with the final layer including the output layer norm.\n31\tIn all three architectures, image tokens precede instruction tokens; the causal mask prevents image tokens from attending to instructions by construction.\n32\t\n33\tWe cross two factors in a $2 \\times 2$ factorial design: \\emph{training status} (trained vs.\\ reference) and \\emph{instruction presence} (with vs.\\ without task description), applied to all 10 available cells in the cross-architecture matrix.\n34\tOpenVLA additionally provides representations from the DinoSigLIP projector output (4{,}096-D), DINOv2 and CLIP external baselines, and the branch-split before fusion; these sources support the OpenVLA deep-dive analyses (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}).\n35\t\n36\t%------------------------------------------------------------------\n37\t\\subsection{Cross-Architecture Analyses}\n38\t\\label{sec:cross_arch_analyses}\n39\t\n40\tThree analyses are applied to every available cell in the $3 \\times 4$ matrix.\n41\t\n42\t\\paragraph{Action probes.}\n43\tTo quantify how much action-relevant structure is accessible at each layer, we train two-layer MLP probes~\\cite{alain2017} to predict 7-DoF action vectors from frozen representations (architecture and training details in Appendix~\\ref{sec:openvla_methods_appendix}).\n44\tWe report probe $R^2$ as a measure of action decodability.\n45\tHigher $R^2$ indicates that actions are more accurately predictable from that representation under the probe; it should not be interpreted as evidence that the model causally uses that layer for action generation.\n46\tWe define $\\Delta R^2$ as the difference between the maximum layer-wise $R^2$ and the exit-layer $R^2$, and use $\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ as a descriptive threshold for substantial peak-to-exit reduction.\n47\tTrain/test splits follow demonstration boundaries (demo-level splitting reduces $R^2$ from $0.7$--$0.9$ to $0.4$--$0.6$ vs.\\ random splitting; Appendix~\\ref{sec:delta_r2_threshold}).\n48\t95\\% CIs are obtained via Monte Carlo cross-validation ($n = 1{,}000$ iterations) with demo-level resampling; the canonical peak layer is fixed from the full-data split to avoid winner's-curse bias.\n49\tLinear probes yield consistent patterns (Appendix~\\ref{sec:linear_probe}).\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{L1 attention head profiling.}\n52\tTo determine whether attention heads preferentially route instruction information to the readout position, each L1 head is classified as instruction-dominant ($>50\\%$ of readout-token attention to instruction tokens) or image-dominant, reported as $k/N$ heads (32 for MHA, 16 for GQA).\n53\tSensitivity analysis at thresholds from $30\\%$ to $70\\%$ confirms ordering robustness (Appendix~\\ref{sec:threshold_sensitivity}).\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{Counterfactual instruction perturbation.}\n56\tTo test whether the model's actions depend on instruction content, the instruction is replaced with four conditions: empty string, wrong task, shuffled tokens, and paraphrased.\n57\tWe measure trained-model sensitivity via Cohen's $d$ on action MSE (OpenVLA, TracVLA) or cosine distance (NORA); reference VLMs use cosine distance on hidden representations.\n58\tThese metrics operate on different scales; cross-metric ratios are invalid (\\S\\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}).\n59\t\n60\tOpenVLA additionally supports cross-instruction probes, representational similarity analysis (RSA)~\\cite{kriegeskorte2008}, and external baselines (DINOv2~\\cite{oquab2024_dinov2}, CLIP~\\cite{radford2021_clip}); these single-architecture methods are detailed in Appendix~\\ref{sec:openvla_methods_appendix}.\n61\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4Hb9JbcXyLY73TkTTPeEh","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"a9f16db4-3108-409c-a0d3-40d4a1290da4","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:19:30.561Z"}
