{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AcRk6M9L3PKL9khRHiMUvv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VemU8iSX51jJ3Eajxtz9Uq","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_15/projects/vla-ssd/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260527T022433/best/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3292,"cache_read_input_tokens":69895,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3292,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TM3u5QfQkhWB8okD6Pp3QB","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"29a88d54-0fe3-4400-8c32-9377eeccf092"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VemU8iSX51jJ3Eajxtz9Uq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{array}\n9\t\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}\n10\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n11\t\\usepackage{caption}\n12\t\\captionsetup{font=small}\n13\t\n14\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{\\emph{i.e.}}\n15\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{\\emph{e.g.}}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\cf}{\\emph{cf.}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\etal}{\\emph{et al.}}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models:\\\\A Cross-Architecture Token-Level Analysis}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{\n22\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n23\t  \\textit{Under Review at CoRL 2026}\n24\t}\n25\t\n26\t\\date{}\n27\t\n28\t\\begin{document}\n29\t\\maketitle\n30\t\n31\t% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) fine-tune large language models on robot demonstration data, yet how training reshapes instruction routing and information flow at the token level remains uncharacterized---and whether such effects generalize across architectures is unknown.\n34\tWe present a systematic experimental framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs, validated across three architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) and four LIBERO task suites.\n35\tWe report three main findings.\n36\tFirst, VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from the mid-layer processing pattern of pretrained LLMs to an entry-exit bookend pattern, achieving immediate instruction routing at layer~1 (99.6\\% classification accuracy vs.\\ 80.2\\% for the pretrained LLM baseline).\n37\tSecond, R$^2$ exit collapse---a sharp drop in action-decodability at exit layers---consistently emerges when VLMs are first fine-tuned into VLAs (6/6 cells across two architectures, $\\Delta = 0.138$--$0.691$) but not during subsequent VLA-to-VLA fine-tuning (0/4 cells, $\\Delta < 0.086$), indicating a one-time representational reorganization.\n38\tThird, under RSA, vision encoder geometry follows an inverted-U profile with peak alignment at mid-layers, invariant to both training state and instruction content ($\\Delta\\rho < 0.017$).\n39\tAn NLP fine-tuning control ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.000$) confirms these effects are specific to action-oriented training.\n40\tThese results characterize a three-tier routing gradient---from VLM base through general VLA to task-specific VLA---suggesting that instruction routing is a staged emergent property of VLA training.\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t% ============================================================\n44\t\\section{Introduction}\n45\t\\label{sec:intro}\n46\t\n47\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) adapt large language models to robotic control by fine-tuning on paired observation-action data, conditioned on natural language instructions~\\citep{brohan2023rt2,kim2024openvla}.\n48\tDespite strong task performance, the internal mechanisms by which these models integrate visual observations with language instructions remain poorly understood.\n49\tPrior behavioral studies have documented visual dominance and instruction underutilization in VLA policies~\\citep{grant2026behavioral,haon2025behavioral}, but these analyses operate at the input--output level and do not decompose the representational roles of different token positions or explain how training reshapes information flow.\n50\t\n51\tWe address this gap with a systematic experimental framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs.\n52\tOur framework combines six complementary analysis methods---Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA), Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA), linear and MLP action probes ($R^2$), cross-instruction classification, attention profiling, and counterfactual perturbation---applied in a factorial design across training conditions (trained, pretrained base, untrained) and instruction configurations.\n53\tWe validate this framework across three VLA architectures built on distinct LLM backbones: OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B), TracVLA (Phi-3-mini), and NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B), tested on four LIBERO task suites (Goal, Spatial, Object, Long), yielding a $3 \\times 4$ evaluation matrix with 10 filled cells.\n54\t\n55\tOur analysis reveals three principal findings:\n56\t\n57\t\\textbf{(1) Immediate dedicated routing (C2).}\n58\tVLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction processing from the mid-layer inverted-U pattern characteristic of pretrained LLMs (peak at layers~9--16) to an entry-exit bookend pattern (peaks at layers~0--1 and~31).\n59\tThree qualitatively different routing strategies emerge: trained VLAs achieve immediate instruction capture at layer~1 (99.6\\%), pretrained LLMs require gradual buildup (80.2\\% at L1, saturating at L5), and random-weight models exhibit accidental routing that decays (100\\% at L1 declining to 82\\% at L31).\n60\t\n61\t\\textbf{(2) Cross-architecture R$^2$ exit collapse.}\n62\tAction-decodability collapses at exit layers when VLMs are first converted to VLAs (6/6 cells, $\\Delta = 0.138$--$0.691$) but not during further VLA-to-VLA fine-tuning (0/4 cells, $\\Delta < 0.086$).\n63\tAn NLP fine-tuning control ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.000$) excludes generic fine-tuning as an explanation.\n64\tThis binary VLM$\\to$VLA vs.\\ VLA$\\to$VLA separation constitutes a mechanistic signature of initial action training.\n65\t\n66\t\\textbf{(3) Vision encoder geometry (observation, under RSA).}\n67\tThe frozen vision encoder provides the dominant source of action-aligned geometric structure (RSA $\\rho = 0.615$), which is invariant to training state and instruction content ($\\Delta\\rho < 0.017$ across a $2 \\times 2$ factorial).\n68\tUnder RSA, DINOv2 achieves the highest alignment ($\\rho = 0.692$), though CKA reverses this ordering, highlighting metric dependence.\n69\t\n70\tThese findings are unified by a three-tier routing gradient: VLM base (no action routing) $\\to$ OXE-pretrained VLA (general routing) $\\to$ task-finetuned VLA (task-specific routing with exit collapse).\n71\t\n72\t\n73\t% ============================================================\n74\t\\section{Related Work}\n75\t\\label{sec:related}\n76\t\n77\t\\textbf{Vision-Language-Action Models.}\n78\tRT-2~\\citep{brohan2023rt2} demonstrated that VLMs can be adapted for robotic control by tokenizing actions as text.\n79\tOpenVLA~\\citep{kim2024openvla} scaled this approach with open-source training on the Open X-Embodiment dataset~\\citep{open_x_embodiment_rt_x_2023}.\n80\tTracVLA and NORA represent recent architectural variants using Phi-3-mini and Qwen2.5-VL backbones, respectively.\n81\t\n82\t\\textbf{Behavioral Analysis of VLAs.}\n83\tGrant~\\etal~\\citep{grant2026behavioral} and Haon~\\etal~\\citep{haon2025behavioral} characterized visual dominance and instruction underutilization through perturbation studies.\n84\tOur work extends this line by moving from behavioral to representational analysis, decomposing token-level information flow.\n85\t\n86\t\\textbf{Mechanistic Interpretability of LLMs.}\n87\tProbing~\\citep{alain2017understanding,hewitt2019structural}, attention analysis~\\citep{clark2019does}, and representational similarity~\\citep{kornblith2019similarity,kriegeskorte2008representational} have been widely applied to language and vision-language models.\n88\tWe adapt these tools to the VLA setting, where the additional action modality creates novel analytical challenges.\n89\t\n90\t\n91\t% ============================================================\n92\t\\section{Experimental Framework}\n93\t\\label{sec:method}\n94\t\n95\t\\subsection{Models and Benchmarks}\n96\t\n97\tWe analyze three VLA architectures spanning distinct LLM backbones and attention configurations:\n98\t\n99\t\\begin{itemize}\n100\t  \\item \\textbf{OpenVLA}: SigLIP + DinoSigLIP vision encoder, Llama-2-7B backbone (32 layers, 32 attention heads). Pretrained on 970K Open X-Embodiment episodes; our ``untrained'' baseline is the OXE-pretrained checkpoint before LIBERO fine-tuning."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TM3u5QfQkhWB8okD6Pp3QB","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"35f284ab-78a5-4e74-8975-e0921ac5e6b4","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:19:35.597Z"}
