{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SvgoU2DfUjP9LNwxrDVz1d","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/discussion.tex:6:Trained $\\Delta R^2$ correlates with published success rates ($\\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}) but is underpowered ($n = 9$; effect size is large, $|\\rho| > 0.5$); tracing collapse onset remains for future work.\ndocs/paper/discussion.tex:9:$\\Delta R^2 > 0.10$ provides a cheap probe-based screening signal that can supplement rollout evaluation, though the observed correlation with published success rates remains non-significant at conventional levels ($\\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$, $n = 9$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}).\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:1119:Correlating trained $\\Delta R^2$ with published LIBERO success rates across all nine available architecture-suite cells yields a positive but non-significant trend (Spearman $\\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$, $n = 9$), marginally above the conventional significance threshold.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:1120:Within-suite comparisons, which control for task difficulty, are uniformly positive: on Goal ($n = 3$ architectures), $\\Delta R^2$ and success rate are perfectly rank-correlated ($\\rho = 1.0$), and every remaining suite ($n = 2$ each) shows the same direction.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:1122:Several caveats temper this interpretation: architecture capacity differences (model size, backbone quality) are not controlled, suite difficulty co-varies with both $\\Delta R^2$ and success rate, multiple suites from the same architecture are not independent observations, and the sample size limits statistical power.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:1126:Closed-loop behavioral validation, including intervention experiments that directly manipulate exit-layer representations, remains a priority for future work.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:1128:\\paragraph{Closed-loop evaluation attempt.}\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:1129:We attempted closed-loop evaluation on LIBERO-Goal using the OpenVLA official checkpoint but achieved SR$=$0/3 across tested configurations, consistent with known reproducibility challenges in VLA deployment (sensitivity to hardware setup, action tokenization, and environment configuration).\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:1140:All analyses are open-loop, consistent with prior representational studies~\\cite{grant2026,haon2025}; closed-loop validation would provide complementary evidence about the behavioral relevance of the identified routing structures.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:1164:Practitioners can rank checkpoints by $\\Delta R^2$ and reserve rollout budgets for the top candidates, substantially reducing the number of expensive closed-loop evaluations needed during model selection.\ndocs/paper/appendix.tex:1177:Representational analysis is an established methodology in the interpretability tradition; behavioral validation provides complementary evidence but is not a prerequisite for mechanistic characterization."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TM3u5QfQkhWB8okD6Pp3QB","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"e3e59db3-acd0-4d1c-a099-fb1206731c40","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:19:44.353Z"}
