{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019jK3mFhqkQGbCz3CTo7QeK","type":"tool_result","content":"798\t\\caption{Number of instruction-dominant heads at L1 under varying attention thresholds. The three-condition ordering is stable across all thresholds.}\n799\t\\label{tab:threshold_sensitivity}\n800\t\\small\n801\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n802\t\\toprule\n803\tThreshold & VLA Trained & Llama-2 base & Rand.\\ Init \\\\\n804\t\\midrule\n805\t30\\% & 32 & 13 & 0 \\\\\n806\t40\\% & 31 & 10 & 0 \\\\\n807\t50\\% & 24 & 9 & 0 \\\\\n808\t60\\% & 9 & 4 & 0 \\\\\n809\t70\\% & 5 & 3 & 0 \\\\\n810\t\\bottomrule\n811\t\\end{tabular}\n812\t\\end{table}\n813\t\n814\t\\subsection{L1 Attention Decomposition by Head Group}\n815\t\\label{sec:attention_decomp}\n816\t\n817\tTable~\\ref{tab:attention_decomp} decomposes L1 attention mass at the readout token into three token categories for instruction-dominant and control head groups.\n818\t\n819\t\\begin{table}[h]\n820\t\\centering\n821\t\\caption{Attention mass allocation at Layer 1, averaged across 5 samples. Instruction-dominant heads (defined as $>50\\%$ attention to instruction tokens) allocate 17.5 percentage points more attention to instruction tokens than control heads, with corresponding reductions in vision ($-8.3$pp) and BOS/sink ($-9.2$pp) attention.}\n822\t\\label{tab:attention_decomp}\n823\t\\small\n824\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n825\t\\toprule\n826\tHead Group & $N$ & Instruction & Vision & BOS/Other \\\\\n827\t\\midrule\n828\tInstruction-dominant & 24 & 62.4\\% & 18.8\\% & 18.8\\% \\\\\n829\tControl & 8 & 44.9\\% & 27.1\\% & 28.0\\% \\\\\n830\t\\midrule\n831\t$\\Delta$ & --- & $+$17.5pp & $-$8.3pp & $-$9.2pp \\\\\n832\t\\bottomrule\n833\t\\end{tabular}\n834\t\\end{table}\n835\t\n836\tHead-level instruction attention entropy reaches $99.5\\%$ of the uniform maximum; attention is broadly distributed rather than concentrated in a few heads.\n837\t\n838\t\\paragraph{Per-token attention normalization.}\n839\tThe $57.7\\%$ aggregate text attention in instruction-dominant heads partly reflects token-count imbalance (22 instruction vs.\\ 256 image tokens).\n840\tPer-token normalization reveals a $38.2\\times$ ratio: each instruction token receives $38.2\\times$ the attention of each image token in trained instruction-dominant heads, compared to only $1.5\\times$ in randomly initialized heads (a $25.5\\times$ increase from training).\n841\tInstruction-dominant heads also show lower attention entropy ($0.465$) than non-dominant heads ($0.569$), indicating more concentrated attention distributions.\n842\t\n843\t\\subsection{Per-Dimension Action MAE Under Head Ablation}\n844\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_dim_mae} extends Table~\\ref{tab:action_shift_perdim} with full per-dimension control-head data and instruction-to-control MAE ratios.\n845\t\n846\t\\begin{table}[h]\n847\t\\centering\n848\t\\caption{Mean absolute error per action dimension under L1 head ablation. Translation dimensions ($dx$/$dy$/$dz$) show 3--5$\\times$ larger instruction-to-control ratio than rotation dimensions (1.9--2.6$\\times$); instruction-dominant heads have disproportionate influence on positional control. Gripper (2.07$\\times$) falls between the two groups.}\n849\t\\label{tab:per_dim_mae}\n850\t\\small\n851\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n852\t\\toprule\n853\tDimension & Instr.\\ 24h MAE & Control 8h MAE & Ratio \\\\\n854\t\\midrule\n855\t$dx$ & 0.00189 & 0.00037 & 5.09$\\times$ \\\\\n856\t$dy$ & 0.00531 & 0.00125 & 4.26$\\times$ \\\\\n857\t$dz$ & 0.00762 & 0.00243 & 3.14$\\times$ \\\\\n858\t$roll$ & 0.02059 & 0.00785 & 2.62$\\times$ \\\\\n859\t$pitch$ & 0.03071 & 0.01387 & 2.21$\\times$ \\\\\n860\t$yaw$ & 0.03388 & 0.01783 & 1.90$\\times$ \\\\\n861\t$gripper$ & 0.29072 & 0.14072 & 2.07$\\times$ \\\\\n862\t\\bottomrule\n863\t\\end{tabular}\n864\t\\end{table}\n865\t\n866\t\\subsection{Per-Task $R^2$ Distribution Under L1 Ablation}\n867\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_task_r2} reports per-task action probe $R^2$ at L1 under baseline and 24-head ablation conditions (MLP probe, 5 demonstrations per task).\n868\t\n869\t\\begin{table}[h]\n870\t\\centering\n871\t\\caption{Per-task action probe $R^2$ at L1 (MLP probe, 5 demos/task). All 10 tasks show positive ablation effect ($\\Delta > 0$): instruction-head ablation uniformly increases action decodability at the entry layer. Mean $\\Delta R^2 = +0.107$; the 50-demo setting (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}) yields a consistent $+0.059$.}\n872\t\\label{tab:per_task_r2}\n873\t\\small\n874\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n875\t\\toprule\n876\tTask & Baseline $R^2$ & Ablated $R^2$ & $\\Delta R^2$ \\\\\n877\t\\midrule\n878\topen middle drawer & 0.197 & 0.221 & $+$0.024 \\\\\n879\topen top drawer + bowl & 0.265 & 0.340 & $+$0.075 \\\\\n880\tpush plate to stove & 0.190 & 0.197 & $+$0.007 \\\\\n881\tput bowl on plate & 0.375 & 0.474 & $+$0.099 \\\\\n882\tput bowl on stove & 0.235 & 0.395 & $+$0.160 \\\\\n883\tput bowl on cabinet & 0.256 & 0.457 & $+$0.201 \\\\\n884\tput cream cheese in bowl & 0.186 & 0.345 & $+$0.159 \\\\\n885\tput wine on rack & 0.189 & 0.424 & $+$0.235 \\\\\n886\tput wine on cabinet & 0.279 & 0.353 & $+$0.074 \\\\\n887\tturn on stove & 0.385 & 0.421 & $+$0.036 \\\\\n888\t\\midrule\n889\tMean $\\pm$ std & $0.256 \\pm 0.070$ & $0.363 \\pm 0.088$ & $+$0.107 \\\\\n890\t\\bottomrule\n891\t\\end{tabular}\n892\t\\end{table}\n893\t\n894\t\\subsection{Task Complexity and Attention Dependence}\n895\t\\label{sec:task_complexity}\n896\t\n897\tWe classify tasks as \\emph{instruction-discriminative} when multiple tasks share the same manipulated object(s), requiring instruction content to disambiguate the target action (e.g., ``put bowl on plate'' vs.\\ ``put bowl on stove''); remaining tasks involve unique object-action pairings recoverable from visual context alone.\n898\tTable~\\ref{tab:task_complexity} reports per-task $|\\Delta R^2|$ under both groupings.\n899\t\n900\t\\begin{table}[h]\n901\t\\centering\n902\t\\caption{Task-level L1 ablation effects ($|\\Delta R^2|$) grouped by instruction dependence. \\emph{Primary} (conservative): cream cheese in bowl classified as visual-sufficient ($2.5\\times$, $p = 0.033$). \\emph{Sensitivity} (shared-object criterion): cream cheese reclassified as instruction-discriminative ($6.5\\times$, $p = 0.008$).}\n903\t\\label{tab:task_complexity}\n904\t\\small\n905\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n906\t\\toprule\n907\tTask & $|\\Delta R^2|$ & Group \\\\\n908\t\\midrule\n909\tput wine on rack & 0.235 & Instr-disc \\\\\n910\tput bowl on cabinet & 0.201 & Instr-disc \\\\\n911\tput bowl on stove & 0.160 & Instr-disc \\\\\n912\tput bowl on plate & 0.099 & Instr-disc \\\\\n913\topen top drawer + bowl & 0.075 & Instr-disc \\\\\n914\tput wine on cabinet & 0.074 & Instr-disc \\\\\n915\t\\midrule\n916\tput cream cheese in bowl$^\\dagger$ & 0.159 & Vis-suff \\\\\n917\tturn on stove & 0.036 & Vis-suff \\\\\n918\topen middle drawer & 0.024 & Vis-suff \\\\\n919\tpush plate to stove & 0.007 & Vis-suff \\\\\n920\t\\midrule\n921\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textbf{Primary}: Instr-disc mean $0.141$; Vis-suff mean $0.057$} \\\\\n922\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{Mann-Whitney $U = 21.0$, $p = 0.033$, ratio $= 2.49\\times$} \\\\\n923\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textbf{Sensitivity}$^\\dagger$: cream cheese as Instr-disc: $p = 0.008$, $6.5\\times$} \\\\\n924\t\\bottomrule\n925\t\\end{tabular}\n926\t\\end{table}\n927\t\n928\t\\paragraph{Leave-one-out sensitivity.}\n929\tAll leave-one-out $p$-values remain $\\leq 0.083$ (marginally significant), with no single task removal reversing the effect direction.\n930\tReclassifying the boundary case (cream cheese in bowl) to the instruction-discriminative group strengthens the result ($p = 0.008$).\n931\t\n932\tBootstrap 95\\% CI on the complex/simple ratio: $[1.03, 11.12]$ (10{,}000 resamples). The wide interval reflects low statistical power from $N=10$ tasks split into groups of 6 and 4; the point estimate ($2.5\\times$) persists under leave-one-out analysis (all $p \\leq 0.083$, marginally significant; \\S\\ref{sec:task_complexity}).\n933\t\n934\t\\subsection{Specificity Control: Matched-Size Head Ablation}\n935\tTo test whether ablation effects are specific to instruction-dominant heads, we ablate four matched-size subsets of 24 heads at L1: instruction-dominant (highest instruction attention fraction), image-dominant (highest image attention fraction), random (5 seeds), and bottom-24 (lowest instruction attention fraction; overlaps 16/24 heads with the instruction-dominant group).\n936\t\n937\t\\begin{table}[h]\n938\t\\centering\n939\t\\caption{Matched-size ablation controls: trajectory disruption ($d$) from ablating 24-head subsets at L1, ranked by instruction attention fraction. Instruction-dominant heads produce the largest effect, but the modest gap ($8\\%$ over random) indicates distributed, attention-graded redistribution rather than a dedicated subset.}\n940\t\\label{tab:specificity_control}\n941\t\\small\n942\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n943\t\\toprule\n944\tCondition (24 heads) & Cohen's $d$ & 95\\% CI \\\\\n945\t\\midrule\n946\tInstruction-dominant & 0.650 & (reference) \\\\\n947\tRandom (5 seeds) & $0.601 \\pm 0.021$ & $[0.568, 0.622]$ \\\\\n948\tImage-dominant & 0.580 & $[0.547, 0.613]$ \\\\\n949\tBottom-24 & 0.565 & --- \\\\\n950\t\\bottomrule\n951\t\\end{tabular}\n952\t\\end{table}\n953\t\n954\tAll conditions yield comparable ground-truth MSE ($0.487$--$0.504$ vs.\\ baseline $0.497$), consistent with direction-selective rather than precision-degrading effects.\n955\t\n956\t\\subsection{Paired Effect Sizes}\n957\tMain-text Cohen's $d$ values use the standard (unpaired) formula for token-level comparisons where observations are independent tokens.\n958\tFor within-subject effects where the same tasks or demonstrations are measured under two conditions, we additionally report paired $d_z = \\bar{\\Delta} / s_\\Delta$, which accounts for within-subject correlation and yields larger effect sizes when correlation is high (Table~\\ref{tab:paired_dz}).\n959\t\n960\t\\begin{table}[h]\n961\t\\centering\n962\t\\caption{Paired effect sizes ($d_z$) for within-subject comparisons. Standard (unpaired) $d$ reported where available. Paired $d_z$ exceeds standard $d$ when within-subject correlation is high.}\n963\t\\label{tab:paired_dz}\n964\t\\small\n965\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n966\t\\toprule\n967\tComparison & Standard $d$ & Paired $d_z$ & $N$ \\\\\n968\t\\midrule\n969\tL1 ablation: 24 dominant vs.\\ baseline & 0.65 & 1.17 & 10 tasks \\\\\n970\tRandom-24 ablation vs.\\ baseline & --- & $1.84 \\pm 0.33$ & 30 subsets \\\\\n971\tDose-response: 32 vs.\\ 4 heads (MSE) & --- & 1.79 & 10 tasks \\\\\n972\tRSA: trained vs.\\ reference (best-vs-best) & --- & 0.55 & 10 tasks \\\\\n973\tCross-arch: OpenVLA vs.\\ TraceVLA peak & --- & 0.48 & 15 seeds \\\\\n974\t\\bottomrule\n975\t\\end{tabular}\n976\t\\end{table}\n977\t\n978\t\\subsection{Cross-Architecture Action Decodability: $2 \\times 2$ Design}\n979\t\\label{sec:cross_arch_r2}\n980\t\n981\tTable~\\ref{tab:2x2_r2} reports per-layer action prediction $R^2$ across two architectures (OpenVLA, TraceVLA) $\\times$ two training conditions (VLA-trained, reference base LLM), providing a $2 \\times 2$ factorial view of how per-layer action decodability differs between trained and reference conditions.\n982\t\n983\t\\paragraph{Convergent reorganization from divergent starting points.}\n984\tReference profiles differ qualitatively: OpenVLA (Llama-2) peaks at L0 ($R^2 = 0.629$) then collapses to near-zero at mid-layers (L20: $0.086$, std $= 0.311$), while Phi-3-V peaks at L6 ($0.578 \\pm 0.018$) with gradual monotonic decline (late-layer std $= 0.068$).\n985\tAfter VLA training, both architectures reach nearly identical mid-layer peaks---OpenVLA at L9 ($R^2 = 0.637 \\pm 0.008$), TraceVLA at L11 ($0.634 \\pm 0.009$)---with $\\Delta = 0.003$ and overlapping CIs.\n986\tDeep-layer variance drops sharply with training: OpenVLA std $0.277 \\to 0.040$; Phi-3-V std $0.068 \\to 0.011$.\n987\tPeak $R^2$ values are comparable before and after training (reference peaks reach $91$--$99\\%$ of trained), but peak locations shift deeper (L0$\\to$L9; L6$\\to$L11) and profile shapes transform from architecture-specific patterns to a shared mid-layer plateau.\n988\t\n989\t\\paragraph{Summary.}\n990\tTable~\\ref{tab:2x2_r2_summary} condenses the key contrasts.\n991\tThe L0 values are identical between trained and reference within each architecture (shared frozen vision encoder + projector), confirming that training effects arise from LLM weight changes.\n992\tThe largest training gains appear at mid-to-deep layers (L15--L25 average: OpenVLA $+0.313$, Phi-3-V $+0.103$), precisely where reference profiles diverge most.\n993\t\n994\t\\begin{table}[h]\n995\t\\centering\n996\t\\caption{$R^2$ profile summary across architectures and training conditions.}\n997\t\\label{tab:2x2_r2_summary}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4Hb9JbcXyLY73TkTTPeEh","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"1d243424-291d-4d4c-b860-c3623e1bf7e9","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:19:52.059Z"}
