{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018ND2P4nwDoDuuXXB9DMLXW","type":"tool_result","content":"998\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n999\t\\toprule\n1000\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{TraceVLA (Phi-3-mini)} \\\\\n1001\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n1002\t & Reference & Trained & Reference & Trained \\\\\n1003\t\\midrule\n1004\tPeak Layer       & L0          & L9          & L6          & L11         \\\\\n1005\tPeak $R^2$       & 0.629       & 0.637       & 0.578       & 0.634       \\\\\n1006\tProfile Shape    & Collapse    & Flat plateau & Ramp $\\to$ decline & Ramp $\\to$ plateau \\\\\n1007\tLate-layer std   & 0.277       & 0.040       & 0.068       & 0.011       \\\\\n1008\t$\\Delta_{\\text{train}}$ (peak) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$+0.008$ (L0$\\to$L9)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$+0.056$ (L6$\\to$L11)} \\\\\n1009\t$\\Delta_{\\text{train}}$ (L15--L25 avg) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$+0.313$} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$+0.103$} \\\\\n1010\t\\bottomrule\n1011\t\\end{tabular}\n1012\t\\end{table}\n1013\t\n1014\t\\begin{table}[h]\n1015\t\\centering\n1016\t\\caption{Per-layer action prediction $R^2$: training effect across architectures (mean $\\pm$ std over 15 seeds). Peak values per condition in \\textbf{bold}.}\n1017\t\\label{tab:2x2_r2}\n1018\t\\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n1019\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n1020\t\\toprule\n1021\tLayer & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{TraceVLA (Phi-3-mini)} \\\\\n1022\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n1023\t & Reference & Trained & Reference & Trained \\\\\n1024\t\\midrule\n1025\tL0  & $\\mathbf{0.629 \\pm 0.016}$ & $0.629 \\pm 0.016$ & $-0.330 \\pm 0.003$ & $-0.330 \\pm 0.002$ \\\\\n1026\tL1  & $0.539 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.625 \\pm 0.017$ & $0.429 \\pm 0.034$ & $0.447 \\pm 0.013$ \\\\\n1027\tL2  & $0.517 \\pm 0.047$ & $0.622 \\pm 0.010$ & $0.458 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.522 \\pm 0.014$ \\\\\n1028\tL3  & $0.448 \\pm 0.216$ & $0.624 \\pm 0.014$ & $0.498 \\pm 0.036$ & $0.579 \\pm 0.011$ \\\\\n1029\tL4  & $0.496 \\pm 0.057$ & $0.628 \\pm 0.014$ & $0.572 \\pm 0.021$ & $0.592 \\pm 0.009$ \\\\\n1030\tL5  & $0.475 \\pm 0.101$ & $0.627 \\pm 0.019$ & $0.573 \\pm 0.020$ & $0.602 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n1031\tL6  & $0.422 \\pm 0.119$ & $0.631 \\pm 0.012$ & $\\mathbf{0.578 \\pm 0.018}$ & $0.609 \\pm 0.012$ \\\\\n1032\tL7  & $0.459 \\pm 0.093$ & $0.632 \\pm 0.012$ & $0.569 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.616 \\pm 0.014$ \\\\\n1033\tL8  & $0.347 \\pm 0.283$ & $0.635 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.560 \\pm 0.025$ & $0.615 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n1034\tL9  & $0.445 \\pm 0.127$ & $\\mathbf{0.637 \\pm 0.008}$ & $0.564 \\pm 0.021$ & $0.617 \\pm 0.013$ \\\\\n1035\tL10 & $0.353 \\pm 0.224$ & $0.624 \\pm 0.014$ & $0.551 \\pm 0.027$ & $0.625 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n1036\tL11 & $0.356 \\pm 0.122$ & $0.623 \\pm 0.016$ & $0.561 \\pm 0.025$ & $\\mathbf{0.634 \\pm 0.009}$ \\\\\n1037\tL12 & $0.409 \\pm 0.203$ & $0.626 \\pm 0.011$ & $0.557 \\pm 0.026$ & $0.632 \\pm 0.005$ \\\\\n1038\tL13 & $0.341 \\pm 0.227$ & $0.624 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.547 \\pm 0.028$ & $0.633 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n1039\tL14 & $0.374 \\pm 0.211$ & $0.623 \\pm 0.015$ & $0.550 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.631 \\pm 0.009$ \\\\\n1040\tL15 & $0.297 \\pm 0.225$ & $0.619 \\pm 0.012$ & $0.550 \\pm 0.022$ & $0.633 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n1041\tL16 & $0.323 \\pm 0.226$ & $0.619 \\pm 0.011$ & $0.534 \\pm 0.018$ & $0.631 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n1042\tL17 & $0.395 \\pm 0.109$ & $0.609 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.519 \\pm 0.024$ & $0.633 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n1043\tL18 & $0.214 \\pm 0.307$ & $0.613 \\pm 0.017$ & $0.502 \\pm 0.047$ & $0.629 \\pm 0.012$ \\\\\n1044\tL19 & $0.241 \\pm 0.258$ & $0.613 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.499 \\pm 0.058$ & $0.627 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n1045\tL20 & $0.086 \\pm 0.311$ & $0.611 \\pm 0.014$ & $0.482 \\pm 0.048$ & $0.628 \\pm 0.011$ \\\\\n1046\tL21 & $0.330 \\pm 0.216$ & $0.589 \\pm 0.023$ & $0.476 \\pm 0.036$ & $0.624 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n1047\tL22 & $0.207 \\pm 0.283$ & $0.586 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.408 \\pm 0.140$ & $0.616 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n1048\tL23 & $0.264 \\pm 0.227$ & $0.578 \\pm 0.028$ & $0.455 \\pm 0.074$ & $0.613 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n1049\tL24 & $0.173 \\pm 0.275$ & $0.577 \\pm 0.013$ & $0.446 \\pm 0.062$ & $0.610 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n1050\tL25 & $0.130 \\pm 0.305$ & $0.553 \\pm 0.053$ & $0.479 \\pm 0.039$ & $0.609 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n1051\tL26 & $0.251 \\pm 0.254$ & $0.535 \\pm 0.046$ & $0.411 \\pm 0.098$ & $0.611 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n1052\tL27 & $0.099 \\pm 0.278$ & $0.552 \\pm 0.027$ & $0.366 \\pm 0.197$ & $0.604 \\pm 0.011$ \\\\\n1053\tL28 & $0.099 \\pm 0.307$ & $0.544 \\pm 0.034$ & $0.358 \\pm 0.218$ & $0.596 \\pm 0.015$ \\\\\n1054\tL29 & $0.088 \\pm 0.275$ & $0.530 \\pm 0.034$ & $0.356 \\pm 0.271$ & $0.585 \\pm 0.010$ \\\\\n1055\tL30 & $0.220 \\pm 0.234$ & $0.511 \\pm 0.029$ & $0.424 \\pm 0.104$ & $0.574 \\pm 0.008$ \\\\\n1056\tL31 & $0.069 \\pm 0.284$ & $0.488 \\pm 0.054$ & $0.332 \\pm 0.262$ & $0.557 \\pm 0.016$ \\\\\n1057\tL32 & $0.584 \\pm 0.011$ & $0.535 \\pm 0.015$ & $0.410 \\pm 0.185$ & $0.550 \\pm 0.007$ \\\\\n1058\t\\bottomrule\n1059\t\\end{tabular}%\n1060\t}\n1061\t\\end{table}\n1062\t\n1063\t\\paragraph{Equivalence testing.}\n1064\tTo formally test whether the cross-architecture peak $R^2$ values are statistically equivalent (rather than merely failing to differ), we apply the Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) procedure~\\cite{lakens2017equivalence} with equivalence bound $\\varepsilon = 0.05$.\n1065\tThe observed difference is $\\Delta = 0.003$ (OpenVLA L9: $0.637 \\pm 0.008$; TraceVLA L11: $0.634 \\pm 0.009$).\n1066\tBoth one-sided tests reject at $p < 0.0001$; the 90\\% confidence interval for the difference is $[-0.002, 0.009]$, falling entirely within $[-0.05, 0.05]$.\n1067\tA conventional two-sample $t$-test yields $p = 0.318$ (not significant), illustrating the distinction between absence of evidence for a difference and evidence of equivalence: the TOST procedure provides the latter.\n1068\t\n1069\t\\subsection{Global Multiple Comparison Correction}\n1070\t\\label{sec:bonferroni_correction}\n1071\t\n1072\tThe paper reports 63 statistical tests spanning permutation tests, $t$-tests, bootstrap comparisons, TOST equivalence, and Spearman correlations.\n1073\tWe apply a Bonferroni correction ($\\alpha_{\\text{adj}} = 0.05 / 63 = 7.94 \\times 10^{-4}$) and the less conservative Holm--Bonferroni step-down procedure; both yield identical results.\n1074\tOf the 45 originally significant tests, 40 survive correction (88.9\\%).\n1075\t\n1076\tAll eight core tests supporting the paper's main claims remain significant:\n1077\tRSA permutation at L12 ($p < 0.0001$), trained vs.\\ reference image geometry at L12 ($p = 3.9 \\times 10^{-5}$), counterfactual wrong and empty instruction ($p < 10^{-100}$), TOST cross-architecture equivalence ($p < 0.0001$), dose-response top-24 ablation ($p < 10^{-100}$), trained vs.\\ reference probe $R^2$ ($p < 0.0001$), and bottom-24 specificity ($p < 10^{-90}$).\n1078\t\n1079\tFive auxiliary tests lose significance under correction:\n1080\t(i)~RSA trained vs.\\ reference preaction at L12 ($p = 0.005 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 0.284$),\n1081\t(ii)~DINOv2 vs.\\ SigLIP RSA ($p = 0.032 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 1.0$),\n1082\t(iii)~fused backbone vs.\\ SigLIP RSA ($p = 0.011 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 0.673$),\n1083\t(iv)~CV exact paired permutation ($p = 0.016 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 0.984$),\n1084\tand (v)~$R^2$ projector vs.\\ VLA Spearman correlation ($p = 0.003 \\to p_{\\text{adj}} = 0.183$).\n1085\tThese concern auxiliary comparisons (encoder ranking, coefficient-of-variation ordering) rather than the core redistribution, probing, or counterfactual findings; no main-text conclusion requires revision.\n1086\t\n1087\t\\subsection{Mechanistic Interpretation}\n1088\tMatched-size ablation ($d$: instruction $0.65$, random $0.60$, image $0.58$; Table~\\ref{tab:specificity_control}) and dose-response (steep through 12 heads, plateau beyond 20) indicate distributed attention redistribution.\n1089\tCounterfactual perturbation produces large effects across all conditions ($d = 1.0$--$1.2$ on Goal; \\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}), with shuffled tokens exceeding paraphrased ($d = 1.096$ vs.\\ $0.996$) despite identical token sets, indicating positional sensitivity.\n1090\tThat ablation raises $R^2$ yet alters trajectories ($d = 0.65$) without increasing ground-truth error ($\\Delta\\text{MSE} \\approx 0$; \\S\\ref{sec:action_trajectory}) reflects an information-computation dissociation~\\cite{hewitt2019control}: instruction-dominant heads control direction rather than precision. Random ablation yields larger $R^2$ gains ($+0.071$ vs.\\ $+0.030$; $z = -3.11$, $0$\\textsuperscript{th} percentile), consistent with distinct $R^2$ signatures.\n1091\t\n1092\t\\subsection{Functional Stages and Predictions}\n1093\tPer-layer profiles suggest three stages: redistribution (L0--L1; probe $99.6\\%$, 24/32 instruction-dominant heads), computation (L8--L13; $R^2$ peak $0.655$--$0.678$), and decay (L14--L31, ${\\sim}56\\%$ of parameters; declining $R^2$ and RSA suggest pruning potential).\n1094\tTask-specific fine-tuning eliminates L31 re-engagement (18 $\\to$ 0 heads; \\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}), consistent with exit-layer attention serving multi-task flexibility ($N{=}2$).\n1095\tTraceVLA~\\cite{zheng2024tracevla} (Phi-3-V) shows consistent entry-exit attention concentration from an opposite starting point (\\S\\ref{sec:openvla_routing}).\n1096\tThe $2 {\\times} 2$ trained-vs-reference comparison reveals a consistent reorganization pattern: reference OpenVLA collapses from L0 ($R^2 = 0.629$) to near-zero at mid-layers (L20: $0.086$, std $= 0.277$), while reference Phi-3-V peaks at L6 ($0.578$) with gradual decline (std $= 0.068$)---qualitatively opposite reference landscapes.\n1097\tYet training drives both to nearly identical mid-layer peaks ($0.637$ vs.\\ $0.634$, $\\Delta = 0.003$) with stabilized deep-layer variance (OpenVLA std $0.277 \\to 0.040$; Phi-3-V $0.068 \\to 0.011$).\n1098\tThis consistent outcome from divergent starting points suggests that mid-layer action specialization is a consistent convergence pattern of VLA training, not an architectural artifact.\n1099\tExit-layer $R^2$ differs ($0.488$ vs.\\ $0.557$), possibly reflecting architectural differences in late-layer processing.\n1100\tRemaining predictions: cross-attention VLAs ($\\pi_0$~\\cite{black2024_pi0}, Octo~\\cite{ghosh2024_octo}) should lack L1 instruction-dominant heads; larger VLAs should yield flatter per-head contributions.\n1101\tFor deployments, L1 instruction probes can flag instruction-discriminative tasks and L1 attention entropy can monitor instruction under-attendance at runtime.\n1102\t\n1103\t\\subsection{Extended Discussion}\n1104\t\\label{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}\n1105\t\n1106\t\\paragraph{Observational three-tier gradient.}\n1107\tThe cross-architecture matrix suggests a three-tier gradient of representational adaptation.\n1108\tTier~1 (VLM bases, no action training): inherited L1 attention (TracVLA 32/32, NORA 13--14/16), negligible exit collapse ($\\Delta R^2 \\leq 0.048$), and minimal counterfactual sensitivity (cosine distance $\\leq 0.185$).\n1109\tTier~2 (general-purpose VLA, OXE-pretrained): strong L1 routing (24--26/32 heads), moderate counterfactual sensitivity ($d \\approx 1.0$), and no systematic exit collapse across suites.\n1110\tTier~3 (task-specific VLAs, fine-tuned on LIBERO): exit collapse at every VLM-to-VLA cell and medium-to-large counterfactual sensitivity ($d = 0.45$--$1.52$).\n1111\tThis gradient is suggestive but limited: Tier~2 contains a single architecture (OpenVLA), and the Tier~2/Tier~3 boundary confounds training stage with architecture.\n1112\tTesting additional general-purpose VLAs (e.g., Octo~\\cite{ghosh2024_octo}, $\\pi_0$~\\cite{black2024_pi0}) at Tier~2 is necessary before the three-tier structure can be treated as a robust empirical pattern.\n1113\t\n1114\t\\paragraph{Exit collapse mechanism.}\n1115\tOne possible explanation for the VLM-to-VLA / VLA-to-VLA asymmetry is that VLM-to-VLA references lack action-relevant exit-layer structure, forcing mid-layer construction without exit preservation (analogous to fine-tuning-induced feature distortion~\\cite{kumar2022finetuning}), while VLA-to-VLA references already maintain this structure.\n1116\tValidating this explanation causally requires controlled training-stage experiments, but the consistency across architectures supports its directional value.\n1117\t\n1118\t\\paragraph{Behavioral relevance.}\n1119\tCorrelating trained $\\Delta R^2$ with published LIBERO success rates across all nine available architecture-suite cells yields a positive but non-significant trend (Spearman $\\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$, $n = 9$), marginally above the conventional significance threshold.\n1120\tWithin-suite comparisons, which control for task difficulty, are uniformly positive: on Goal ($n = 3$ architectures), $\\Delta R^2$ and success rate are perfectly rank-correlated ($\\rho = 1.0$), and every remaining suite ($n = 2$ each) shows the same direction.\n1121\tThis directional evidence is consistent with stronger mid-network action encoding (higher peak $R^2$) followed by more aggressive exit-layer compression being associated with better task performance.\n1122\tSeveral caveats temper this interpretation: architecture capacity differences (model size, backbone quality) are not controlled, suite difficulty co-varies with both $\\Delta R^2$ and success rate, multiple suites from the same architecture are not independent observations, and the sample size limits statistical power.\n1123\tPost-hoc power analysis (Fisher $Z$-transform, two-tailed $\\alpha = 0.05$) yields achieved power $= 0.37$ at $n = 9$; reaching $80\\%$ power would require $n \\geq 21$ architecture-suite cells.\n1124\tThe current analysis is therefore underpowered, and the non-significant $p$-value should not be interpreted as evidence of absence; it reflects insufficient sample size rather than a weak effect.\n1125\tThis correlational evidence does not establish a causal link between exit collapse and task success.\n1126\tClosed-loop behavioral validation, including intervention experiments that directly manipulate exit-layer representations, remains a priority for future work.\n1127\t\n1128\t\\paragraph{Closed-loop evaluation attempt.}\n1129\tWe attempted closed-loop evaluation on LIBERO-Goal using the OpenVLA official checkpoint but achieved SR$=$0/3 across tested configurations, consistent with known reproducibility challenges in VLA deployment (sensitivity to hardware setup, action tokenization, and environment configuration).\n1130\tThis negative result motivates our representational analysis approach, which does not require successful task completion.\n1131\t\n1132\t\\paragraph{Metric constraints.}\n1133\tCounterfactual sensitivity is measured via Cohen's $d$ on action MSE for models that produce actions and via cosine distance on hidden representations for reference VLMs (\\S\\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}).\n1134\tThese metrics differ in scale and distributional properties; ratios between them are not meaningful.\n1135\tThe OpenVLA Long-Horizon inversion (trained $d = 0.56$, reference $d = 1.05$) illustrates how same-metric comparisons can reverse the expected direction, cautioning against overinterpretation of any single cell.\n1136\tA standardized metric framework for cross-condition counterfactual comparison remains an open problem.\n1137\t\n1138\t\\paragraph{Extended limitations.}\n1139\tEach architecture is analyzed at a single trained checkpoint, precluding characterization of how exit collapse or counterfactual sensitivity develop during training.\n1140\tAll analyses are open-loop, consistent with prior representational studies~\\cite{grant2026,haon2025}; closed-loop validation would provide complementary evidence about the behavioral relevance of the identified routing structures.\n1141\tThe three-tier gradient has $n = 1$ at Tier~2, limiting its inferential power; populating this tier with additional general-purpose VLAs is a priority for future work.\n1142\t\n1143\t\\paragraph{Coarse training dynamics.}\n1144\tAlthough intermediate checkpoints are unavailable (\\S\\ref{sec:discussion}), the cross-condition design provides coarse-grained evidence about when exit collapse emerges.\n1145\tFigure~\\ref{fig:training_dynamics} plots $\\Delta R^2$ across four training-pathway categories.\n1146\tNLP fine-tuning ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.000$) and pretrained references ($\\Delta R^2 \\leq 0.097$) cluster below the $0.10$ threshold; VLM-to-VLA fine-tuning produces large collapse ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.455$); subsequent VLA-to-VLA fine-tuning does not intensify it ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.033$--$0.086$).\n1147\tThis constrains the onset: exit collapse emerges during the first action-grounded training stage and does not accumulate with continued task-specific fine-tuning.\n1148\t\n1149\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n1150\t\\centering\n1151\t\\includegraphics[width=0.55\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_training_dynamics.pdf}\n1152\t\\caption{\\textbf{$\\Delta R^2$ across training pathways.} Each point is one architecture-suite cell; markers distinguish backbone families. Exit collapse (above dashed line) is confined to VLM-to-VLA transitions and does not intensify during VLA-to-VLA fine-tuning.}\n1153\t\\label{fig:training_dynamics}\n1154\t\\end{figure}\n1155\t\n1156\t\\subsection{Extended Practical Implications}\n1157\t\\label{sec:extended_practical_implications}\n1158\t\n1159\tThe main text identifies three use cases for the entry-exit decomposition; here we elaborate on each.\n1160\t\n1161\t\\paragraph{Checkpoint selection.}\n1162\t$\\Delta R^2$ provides a probe-based screening signal ($> 0.10$ flags reorganization) requiring only a forward pass and a linear probe.\n1163\tIts wall-clock cost scales with model size rather than environment complexity, unlike rollout evaluation which requires physics simulation and multi-step execution.\n1164\tPractitioners can rank checkpoints by $\\Delta R^2$ and reserve rollout budgets for the top candidates, substantially reducing the number of expensive closed-loop evaluations needed during model selection.\n1165\t\n1166\t\\paragraph{Transfer learning strategy.}\n1167\tThe flat VLA-to-VLA $\\Delta R^2$ profile (\\S\\ref{sec:r2_collapse}) shows that VLA-to-VLA transitions preserve exit structure, justifying staged pipelines (VLM$\\to$VLA$\\to$task) that reuse the first-stage action head without re-learning exit representations.\n1168\tInherited L1 routing can further serve as a frozen prior, reducing tunable parameters during domain transfer.\n1169\tThis suggests that practitioners adapting a general-purpose VLA to a new task suite may benefit from freezing early layers while focusing capacity on mid-to-late layer adaptation.\n1170\t\n1171\t\\paragraph{Mid-layer probe value.}\n1172\tWhere exit collapse is pronounced, mid-layer representations ($R^2$ peak at L9--L11 across architectures) outperform exit-layer features for downstream tasks such as reward modeling, distillation targets, or auxiliary prediction heads.\n1173\tFor knowledge distillation, matching mid-layer features of the teacher rather than exit-layer features may yield better student performance in VLA settings.\n1174\tFor reward modeling, training reward predictors on mid-layer features rather than final-layer outputs may provide more informative training signals.\n1175\t\n1176\t\\subsection{Methodological Context}\n1177\tRepresentational analysis is an established methodology in the interpretability tradition; behavioral validation provides complementary evidence but is not a prerequisite for mechanistic characterization.\n1178\tTable~\\ref{tab:methodological_precedent} situates our approach within this tradition.\n1179\t\n1180\t\\begin{table}[h]\n1181\t\\centering\n1182\t\\caption{\\textbf{Methodological precedent.} Representative interpretability studies and their use of behavioral evaluation. Partial behavioral eval includes correlational or indirect evidence.}\n1183\t\\label{tab:methodological_precedent}\n1184\t\\small\n1185\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llll@{}}\n1186\t\\toprule\n1187\tPaper & Domain & Method & Behav.\\ eval? \\\\\n1188\t\\midrule\n1189\tBelinkov~\\cite{belinkov2022} & NLP & Probing survey & No \\\\\n1190\tOlsson et al.~\\cite{olsson2022} & LM & Induction heads & No \\\\\n1191\tWang et al.~\\cite{wang2023_ioi} & LM & IOI circuit & No \\\\\n1192\tMeng et al.~\\cite{meng2022_rome} & LM & Causal tracing (localization) & No \\\\\n1193\tGrant et al.~\\cite{grant2026} & VLA & Visual dominance & Partial \\\\\n1194\tHaon et al.~\\cite{haon2025} & VLA & Sparse directions & No \\\\\n1195\t\\midrule\n1196\tOurs & VLA & $\\Delta R^2$ + attn.\\ profiling & Partial ($\\rho{=}0.583$) \\\\\n1197\t\\bottomrule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4Hb9JbcXyLY73TkTTPeEh","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"b35942ba-0a5a-4f0a-b628-deec7deba933","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:20:05.998Z"}
