{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015NtzWwaJcxNZnJomdm6dFW","type":"tool_result","content":"1198\t\\end{tabular}\n1199\t\\end{table}\n1200\t\n1201\t\\subsection{NLP Finetuning Controls}\n1202\t\\label{sec:nlp_control_appendix}\n1203\t\n1204\tTable~\\ref{tab:nlp_controls} compares the three NLP finetuning controls used to test whether exit collapse is specific to action-oriented training.\n1205\tAll three chat-finetuned models produce flat action-probe profiles with $\\Delta R^2 = 0.000$, confirming that NLP instruction tuning does not induce the peak-to-exit drop observed in VLM-to-VLA transitions in any of the three backbone families.\n1206\t\n1207\t\\begin{table}[h]\n1208\t\\centering\n1209\t\\caption{\\textbf{NLP finetuning control comparison.} All three models are instruction-tuned for conversational use without action heads or robot demonstration data. None exhibits exit collapse.}\n1210\t\\label{tab:nlp_controls}\n1211\t\\small\n1212\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}\n1213\t\\toprule\n1214\tModel & VLA backbone & $\\Delta R^2$ & Mean $R^2$ & Layers \\\\\n1215\t\\midrule\n1216\tLlama-2-7b-chat & OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B) & 0.000 & $0.155 \\pm 0.001$ & 32 \\\\\n1217\tPhi-3-mini-4k-instruct & TracVLA (Phi-3-mini) & 0.000 & $0.155 \\pm 0.000$ & 32 \\\\\n1218\tQwen2.5-3B-Instruct$^{*}$ & NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B) & 0.000 & $0.155 \\pm 0.000$ & 36 \\\\\n1219\t\\bottomrule\n1220\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\footnotesize $^{*}$Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct shares the Qwen2.5 language backbone with NORA's}\\\\\n1221\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\footnotesize Qwen2.5-VL-3B but lacks the vision encoder and cross-attention modules.}\\\\\n1222\t\\end{tabular}\n1223\t\\end{table}\n1224\t\n1225\tFigure~\\ref{fig:nlp_control_r2} overlays the NLP control profiles (dashed gray) against the corresponding VLA trained and reference curves, illustrating the contrast between action-training-induced collapse and the flat NLP baseline across all three backbone families.\n1226\t\n1227\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n1228\t\\centering\n1229\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_nlp_control_r2.pdf}\n1230\t\\caption{\\textbf{NLP finetuning controls produce flat $R^2$ profiles.} Each panel shows the VLA trained (blue) and reference (orange) per-layer action-probe $R^2$ alongside the NLP control (dashed gray, $R^2 = 0.155$) for the matching backbone family. VLM-to-VLA transitions exhibit peak-to-exit collapse; NLP controls remain flat ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.000$) in all three cases.}\n1231\t\\label{fig:nlp_control_r2}\n1232\t\\end{figure}\n1233\t\n1234\t\\subsection{Claim-Evidence Summary}\n1235\t\\label{sec:claim_evidence_appendix}\n1236\t\n1237\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n1238\t\\centering\n1239\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/fig_claim_evidence.pdf}\n1240\t\\caption{\\textbf{Claim--evidence--scope summary.} Each main finding is paired with its supporting analysis and its main limitation. The figure distinguishes directly observed representational patterns from stronger causal interpretations that require interventional or training-dynamics evidence.}\n1241\t\\end{figure}\n1242\t\n1243\t\\subsection{Cross-Architecture Design Rationale}\n1244\tThe two VLM-to-VLA transitions (TracVLA and NORA) were selected to maximize structural diversity within the constraint of publicly available LIBERO-evaluated checkpoints.\n1245\tTable~\\ref{tab:arch_diversity} summarizes the design-axis differences.\n1246\t\n1247\t\\begin{table}[h]\n1248\t\\centering\n1249\t\\caption{\\textbf{Structural diversity between the two VLM-to-VLA transitions.} The two architectures differ on nearly every major design axis, strengthening the generality of shared findings.}\n1250\t\\label{tab:arch_diversity}\n1251\t\\small\n1252\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\n1253\t\\toprule\n1254\tDesign Axis & TracVLA & NORA \\\\\n1255\t\\midrule\n1256\tLanguage backbone & Phi-3-mini (3.8B) & Qwen2.5-VL (3B) \\\\\n1257\tBackbone family & Microsoft & Alibaba \\\\\n1258\tAttention mechanism & Multi-Head Attention & Grouped-Query Attention \\\\\n1259\tNumber of layers & 32 & 36 \\\\\n1260\tVision encoder & SigLIP (shared w/ base) & Native ViT (dynamic resolution) \\\\\n1261\tVision integration & Projector (late fusion) & Token concatenation (native) \\\\\n1262\tPretraining data & English-centric web & Multilingual web + code \\\\\n1263\tAction head design & Linear projection & MLP \\\\\n1264\tTraining data & OXE + LIBERO & OXE + LIBERO + custom \\\\\n1265\t\\bottomrule\n1266\t\\end{tabular}\n1267\t\\end{table}\n1268\t\n1269\tDespite these differences, both transitions exhibit the same exit-collapse pattern ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.156$--$0.455$), while their respective reference VLMs show only small fluctuations ($\\Delta R^2 \\leq 0.097$).\n1270\tThe probability of observing consistent collapse in two structurally divergent architectures under a null hypothesis of random association is low (permutation test $p = 0.008$; \\S\\ref{sec:delta_r2_threshold}).\n1271\tStructural diversity thus provides evidential weight beyond what the raw $N = 2$ count suggests.\n1272\t\n1273\t\\subsection{Convergent Evidence Summary}\n1274\tThree methodologically distinct approaches triangulate instruction routing in VLAs.\n1275\tTable~\\ref{tab:convergent_evidence} summarizes what each method measures, its key finding, evidence type, and acknowledged limitation.\n1276\t\n1277\t\\begin{table}[h]\n1278\t\\centering\n1279\t\\caption{\\textbf{Convergent evidence across three analytical methods.} Each method addresses a different aspect of instruction routing; no single method is sufficient alone, but their convergence strengthens the overall characterization.}\n1280\t\\label{tab:convergent_evidence}\n1281\t\\scriptsize\n1282\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n1283\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.0cm}p{2.8cm}p{3.2cm}p{1.8cm}p{2.8cm}@{}}\n1284\t\\toprule\n1285\tMethod & What it measures & Key finding & Evidence type & Limitation \\\\\n1286\t\\midrule\n1287\tLayer-wise probing (\\S\\ref{sec:r2_collapse}) & Where action-relevant information is decodable & VLM-to-VLA: peak-to-exit collapse ($\\Delta R^2 = 0.16$--$0.46$); VLA-to-VLA: flat & Correlational (decodability) & Does not establish causal necessity \\\\\n1288\t\\addlinespace\n1289\tCounterfactual perturbation (\\S\\ref{sec:cf_sensitivity}) & Whether instruction content affects model outputs & All trained VLAs: graded divergence ($d = 0.45$--$1.52$) ordered by semantic distance & Behavioral (input--output) & Does not localize which layers are responsible \\\\\n1290\t\\addlinespace\n1291\tAttention head ablation (Appendix~\\ref{sec:action_trajectory}) & Whether identified structures carry functional signals & Dose-response: monotonic divergence ($d = 0.48$--$0.64$) upon progressive head removal & Interventional (causal contribution) & Single architecture (OpenVLA); contribution $\\neq$ necessity \\\\\n1292\t\\bottomrule\n1293\t\\end{tabular}\n1294\t\\end{table}\n1295\t\n1296\tThe three methods form a complementary chain: probing identifies \\emph{where} instruction information concentrates across layers, perturbation confirms \\emph{whether} instruction content functionally affects behavior, and ablation demonstrates \\emph{that} the identified attention structures carry relevant signals.\n1297\tNo method alone supports causal claims about instruction routing mechanisms; their convergence provides stronger evidence than any single approach."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4Hb9JbcXyLY73TkTTPeEh","session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"27647bab-6874-47e7-a767-da9251cc7bf9","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:20:13.586Z"}
