{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TM3u5QfQkhWB8okD6Pp3QB","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper presents a cross-architecture representational analysis of three Vision-Language-Action models (OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA) across four LIBERO task suites. Using layer-wise action probes, attention profiling, and counterfactual instruction perturbations, the authors identify three patterns: (1) R-squared exit collapse is consistently associated with VLM-to-VLA transitions but absent in VLA-to-VLA and NLP-only fine-tuning; (2) all trained VLAs exhibit substantial counterfactual instruction sensitivity; and (3) L1 instruction-dominant attention is largely inherited from pretrained backbones rather than acquired during VLA training. The paper contributes a systematic decomposition of training-associated versus inherited representational structure in VLAs.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths (Methods & Experiments)\n\n1. **Well-designed factorial controls.** The trained/reference contrast across architectures with different reference baseline types (VLA-to-VLA for OpenVLA vs. VLM-to-VLA for TracVLA and NORA) provides a natural experiment that cleanly isolates the contribution of action-oriented training. The NLP finetuning control across all three backbone families (Llama-2-7b-chat, Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct, Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct) with uniform Delta-R-squared of 0.000 is a compelling negative control.\n\n2. **Triangulation across complementary methods.** The paper combines probing (correlational/decodability), counterfactual perturbation (behavioral input-output), and attention head ablation (interventional). These methods address different aspects of instruction routing -- where information is decodable, whether it functionally matters, and whether identified structures carry relevant signals. The convergent evidence table (Appendix Table 19) explicitly maps each method to its evidence type and limitation.\n\n3. **Thorough robustness analysis.** The appendix is remarkably comprehensive: label permutation controls, probe seed sensitivity (5 seeds with std < 0.002), linear vs. MLP probe comparison, demo subsampling, distance metric invariance (cosine vs. Euclidean, r > 0.989), per-DOF RSA decomposition, threshold sensitivity analysis (30-70% for attention dominance, 0.05-0.20 for Delta-R-squared), and TOST equivalence testing for cross-architecture peak convergence.\n\n4. **Honest statistical reporting.** The paper acknowledges underpowered analyses (RSA training effect power = 0.356, Delta-R-squared correlation with success rates p = 0.10 at n = 9), reports Bonferroni correction across all 63 tests (40/45 survive), and explicitly flags which auxiliary comparisons lose significance under correction. The bootstrap CI methodology for Delta-R-squared with demo-level resampling and fixed canonical peak layer (avoiding winner's curse) is methodologically sound.\n\n5. **The dose-response ablation.** Progressive ablation of 4 to 32 L1 instruction-dominant heads producing monotonic trajectory divergence (d = 0.482 to 0.639) with bootstrap CIs provides genuine interventional evidence beyond correlational probing. The specificity control (instruction-dominant vs. random vs. image-dominant subsets of equal size) adds rigor, even though the modest gap (8%) actually undermines the specificity claim -- which the paper commendably acknowledges.\n\n6. **Cross-architecture design with maximal structural diversity.** Table A11 documents that TracVLA and NORA differ on nearly every major design axis (backbone family, attention mechanism, vision encoder, integration method, training data). This diversity strengthens the generality of shared findings beyond what N=2 alone would suggest.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods & Experiments)\n\n1. **(Major) N=2 VLM-to-VLA transitions limits generalizability.** The core exit collapse finding rests on only two independent VLM-to-VLA transitions. While the permutation test yields p = 0.008, this is across 9 cells (many from the same architectures, hence not independent). The paper acknowledges this but the main text claims sometimes read stronger than the evidence warrants (e.g., \"consistently emerges\" from 6/6 cells that span only 2 architectures). The three-tier gradient has n=1 at Tier 2 (only OpenVLA as a general-purpose VLA). Architectures outside the causal-decoder family (flow-matching, diffusion) are untested.\n\n2. **(Major) No closed-loop behavioral validation.** All analyses are open-loop. The paper reports an attempted closed-loop evaluation that achieved SR=0/3, attributed to \"known reproducibility challenges.\" The correlation between Delta-R-squared and published success rates (rho = 0.583, p = 0.10, n = 9) is suggestive but non-significant and underpowered. The ablation finding that instruction-head ablation produces d = 0.65 trajectory divergence with essentially zero change in ground-truth MSE (0.498 to 0.497, p = 0.52) raises a genuine question about functional significance: if removing 24/32 instruction-dominant heads does not increase action error, what behavioral role do these heads actually play? The \"direction rather than precision\" interpretation is post-hoc.\n\n3. **(Moderate) Counterfactual metric incomparability across 5/8 cells.** NORA and reference VLMs use cosine distance on hidden representations while OpenVLA and TracVLA use Cohen's d on action MSE. The paper correctly identifies this as a limitation and avoids cross-metric ratios, but it means the counterfactual finding cannot be uniformly evaluated across the full matrix. The NORA Goal saturation (cosine distance = 1.0) likely reflects numerical issues rather than genuine maximal sensitivity.\n\n4. **(Moderate) Delta-R-squared > 0.10 threshold is descriptive rather than principled.** The paper presents this as a \"natural effect-size boundary\" and shows that any threshold in (0.097, 0.156) yields identical grouping. However, the gap of 0.059 between groups is not large, and the threshold analysis is performed on the same data used to establish the pattern. The 2 of 9 CI overlap (OpenVLA Goal upper bound 0.110 vs. TracVLA Spatial lower bound 0.091) weakens the clean separation claim. There is no theoretical grounding for why 10% explained variance drop should be the meaningful boundary.\n\n5. **(Moderate) The specificity control result actually weakens the \"dedicated routing\" narrative.** Instruction-dominant head ablation (d = 0.65) vs. random-24 head ablation (d = 0.60) yields only an 8% gap. The paper interprets this as \"distributed, attention-graded redistribution,\" which is honest, but it means the identified instruction-dominant heads are not meaningfully more important than arbitrary head selections. The random-subset analysis (Figure A6) shows that dominant-head ablation falls below all 30 random subsets for R-squared increase, further complicating the story. These findings are internally consistent but reduce the practical value of the instruction-dominant head classification.\n\n6. **(Moderate) NORA analyses are incomplete.** Only 2 of 4 LIBERO suites are available for NORA (Object and Long-Horizon checkpoints unavailable). NORA Goal has unreliable bootstrap CIs (exit-layer R-squared approximately -0.006). NORA counterfactual analysis is limited to cosine distance on hidden representations. This means the third architecture contributes substantially less evidence than the other two.\n\n7. **(Minor) NLP control design has a gap.** The NLP controls (chat-finetuned models) lack vision encoders, so they receive text-only input. This means they test whether NLP finetuning of a text-only model produces exit collapse, not whether NLP finetuning of a VLM produces exit collapse. The Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct footnote acknowledges this for NORA but the mismatch applies to all three controls. A more compelling control would be an instruction-tuned VLM (e.g., a VLM finetuned on visual QA but not actions).\n\n8. **(Minor) OpenVLA reference baseline is not a clean \"before/after\" comparison.** The OpenVLA reference is OXE-pretrained on 970K episodes -- already a fully trained VLA. The trained model is then further finetuned on LIBERO. This makes the OpenVLA VLA-to-VLA comparison test something quite different from the TracVLA/NORA VLM-to-VLA comparisons: it tests the marginal effect of task-specific adaptation on top of massive pre-training, not the fundamental VLA transformation.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The ablation produces d = 0.65 trajectory divergence with Delta-MSE approximately 0. Can you provide the success rate impact (even estimated) to assess whether the identified routing structures affect task completion? The \"direction rather than precision\" interpretation needs empirical grounding beyond the MSE-invariance observation.\n\n2. For the NLP controls, have you considered running the same analysis on instruction-tuned VLMs (e.g., LLaVA-chat or InstructBLIP) that receive visual input but have no action training? This would more directly control for multimodal instruction tuning.\n\n3. The permutation test (p = 0.008) treats each architecture-suite cell as independent, but cells from the same architecture share model weights. How does this dependence affect the test's validity? Have you considered a hierarchical permutation that respects the architecture grouping?\n\n4. TracVLA systematically produces higher counterfactual effect sizes (d = 0.97-1.52) than OpenVLA (d = 0.45-1.21). Is this a genuine architectural difference or could it reflect differences in action tokenization, training data scale, or fine-tuning quality?\n\n5. The NORA Goal exit-layer R-squared of approximately -0.006 with unreliable bootstrap CIs -- what is the interpretation here? Negative R-squared from the probe suggests representations at the exit layer are actively anti-correlated with actions, which seems unusual.\n\n6. You report that training intermediate checkpoints are unavailable. Could the exit collapse onset be estimated by training a small VLA from scratch with periodic checkpointing, rather than relying solely on the pre/post comparison?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n1. **Closed-loop behavioral evaluation.** Even if full LIBERO rollouts are challenging, a simplified behavioral test (e.g., comparing action predictions against ground truth on held-out demonstrations with accuracy/success metrics rather than MSE) would strengthen the functional significance claims.\n\n2. **Additional VLA architectures.** Testing on at least one diffusion or flow-matching VLA (e.g., Pi-0, Octo) would test whether exit collapse is specific to autoregressive/tokenized-action architectures. Testing a second general-purpose VLA at Tier 2 is necessary before the three-tier gradient can be taken seriously.\n\n3. **Training dynamics.** Even coarse-grained analysis at 2-3 intermediate checkpoints would reveal whether exit collapse is sudden or gradual, which has implications for understanding the mechanism.\n\n4. **Multimodal NLP control.** An instruction-tuned VLM with visual input (no action training) would be a more appropriate control than text-only chat models.\n\n5. **Effect of model scale.** All three models are in the 3-7B parameter range. Testing whether the findings hold at larger scales (e.g., 13B+ models) or smaller scales would assess scale sensitivity.\n\n6. **Cross-instruction probe on TracVLA and NORA.** The full cross-instruction classification analysis (dense layer profile, LOOCV) is only reported for OpenVLA and partially for TracVLA. Extending this to all three architectures would strengthen the cross-architecture claim.\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Quality: 7/10** -- The experimental methodology is thorough with appropriate controls, robustness checks, and honest statistical reporting. The main weaknesses are N=2 for the core VLM-to-VLA finding, the absence of closed-loop validation, and the gap in the NLP control design.\n- **Clarity: 6/10** -- The main text is well-organized with clean separation between trained-associated and inherited effects. However, the paper occasionally makes claims that read stronger than the evidence supports. The method section is clear. The appendix is comprehensive but dense.\n- **Significance: 5/10** -- The decomposition of training-associated vs. inherited routing is a useful contribution to VLA understanding, but the lack of behavioral validation and the limited architectural scope (N=2 for the key finding, all causal-decoder) constrain the impact. The practical implications (checkpoint screening, transfer learning guidance) remain speculative without validation.\n- **Relevance: 6/10** -- The paper studies VLAs and LIBERO, which are directly relevant to robot learning. However, the purely analytical nature with no closed-loop evaluation, no policy improvement, and failed reproduction attempts weakens the robotics connection. The representational analysis methodology is more aligned with interpretability venues.\n- **Overall: 6/10** -- A methodologically sound analytical contribution with a comprehensive experimental framework. The main findings (exit collapse in VLM-to-VLA, inherited L1 attention) are interesting and well-supported within their scope, but the limited generalizability (N=2 transitions, single benchmark family, no behavioral validation) places the paper marginally above the acceptance threshold for a robotics venue.\n- **Confidence: 4/5** -- I have read the full paper, appendix, and supporting materials carefully. I am familiar with the probing/RSA/CKA methodology and the VLA literature. My confidence is slightly reduced because I cannot independently verify the numerical results.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict\n\nThis paper presents a carefully designed representational analysis of VLA fine-tuning that introduces useful methodological contributions (the factorial trained/reference design, the convergent evidence framework) and identifies an interesting phenomenon (R-squared exit collapse specific to VLM-to-VLA transitions). The statistical reporting is unusually honest for this field, with explicit power analyses, multiple comparison corrections, and acknowledged limitations. However, the paper's central finding rests on N=2 independent VLM-to-VLA transitions, all within the causal-decoder VLA family, evaluated only on LIBERO. The lack of closed-loop behavioral validation is a significant gap for a robotics venue: the discovery that ablating 24/32 \"instruction-dominant\" heads produces near-zero MSE change undermines claims about functional significance that behavioral evidence could have resolved. The NLP controls, while useful, test text-only models rather than multimodal models without action training, leaving an interpretive gap. For CoRL, the paper sits at the boundary: the methodology and findings have genuine value for the VLA community, but the limited scope and absent behavioral grounding make it a borderline submission. Adding one additional VLA architecture family (diffusion/flow-matching) and any form of behavioral validation would substantially strengthen the contribution."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa29a1454b7ab0581 (use SendMessage with to: 'aa29a1454b7ab0581' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 86916\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 174137</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"823833db-5c3b-4878-9224-b5e511fc87dc","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:21:20.679Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce an independent, detailed review of the paper \"Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Token-Level Analysis\" submitted to CoRL 2026.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates papers on six dimensions using a 1-10 scale:\n- **Originality**: Novel problems, methods, or combinations\n- **Quality**: Technical soundness and support for claims\n- **Clarity**: Writing quality and organization\n- **Significance**: Importance and advancement of the field\n- **Relevance**: Connection to robot learning and CoRL audience\n- **Limitations**: Honest acknowledgment of limitations\n\nScale: 10=Top 5% seminal, 9=Top 15% strong accept, 8=Top 50% clear accept, 7=Good accept, 6=Marginally above threshold, 5=Marginally below, 4=Not good enough reject, 3=Clear reject, 2=Strong reject, 1=Trivial/wrong.\nCoRL acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n\n## Your Focus: Methods and Experiments\nYou must deeply evaluate:\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the methods (RSA, CKA, probes, attention profiling, counterfactual perturbation, cross-instruction classification) well-chosen and correctly applied?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are confidence intervals, bootstrap tests, effect sizes, and controls appropriate?\n3. **Cross-architecture validation**: Is the 3×4 matrix (OpenVLA/TracVLA/NORA × 4 LIBERO suites) sufficient? Are the 10 filled cells (not full 12) adequately justified?\n4. **Controls**: NLP finetuning control (ΔR²=0.000), label permutation control, instruction ablation — are these sufficient?\n5. **Threshold choices**: The Δ>0.10 threshold for exit collapse — is this principled or arbitrary?\n6. **Metric issues**: RSA-CKA ordering reversal, counterfactual metric incomparability (5/8 cells) — how well are these handled?\n7. **Reproducibility**: Are methods described well enough to reproduce?\n8. **Missing experiments**: What's notably absent? (e.g., closed-loop evaluation, more architectures)\n\n## Experimental Design Reference\nThe paper reports these experiments:\n- **Vision Encoder Geometry** (Sec 4): RSA source localization, DINOv2/CLIP baselines, branch-split analysis, per-layer RSA curves, CKA verification, 6-method consistency, 2×2 factorial (training × instruction), instruction content ablation\n- **Instruction Routing** (Sec 5): Cross-instruction classification (3 strategies), L1 attention 3-condition gradient, 32-layer dense attention profile, full-layer R² scan, counterfactual action analysis (head ablation), training trajectory (4 conditions)\n- **Cross-Architecture** (Sec 6): R² exit collapse matrix (3×4, 10 cells), NLP finetuning control (3 models), cross-architecture counterfactual sensitivity, three-tier routing gradient, L1 heads training vs inheritance, cross-benchmark consistency, TracVLA mixed heads\n\nThe paper also has an experimental design document listing 20 imagined experiments (plans 001-013, plus cross_arch_r2_collapse, cross_arch_cf, three_tier_gradient, nlp_control, cross_arch_l1_heads, cross_inst_probe, l1_attention_three_cond, dense_attention_profile, cf_trajectory_analysis, training_trajectory, tracvla_mixed_heads). Check whether the paper covers these adequately.\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper presents a systematic framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs. Three main findings:\n1. VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from mid-layer (pretrained LLM pattern) to entry-exit bookend pattern (immediate routing at L1: 99.6% vs 80.2%).\n2. R² exit collapse emerges in VLM→VLA transitions (6/6 cells, Δ=0.138-0.691) but not VLA→VLA (0/4, Δ<0.086). NLP control ΔR²=0.000.\n3. Vision encoder geometry (RSA) follows inverted-U, invariant to training and instruction (Δρ<0.017). DINOv2 highest under RSA but CKA reverses ordering.\n\nThree architectures: OpenVLA (Llama-2-7B), TracVLA (Phi-3-mini), NORA (Qwen2.5-VL-3B). Four LIBERO suites.\n\nKey tables: Table 1 (weight change), Tables 2-11 (vision encoder geometry), Tables 12-17 (instruction routing), Tables 18-25 (cross-architecture).\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in the following structure:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences summarizing the paper.\n\n### Strengths (Methods & Experiments)\nNumbered list of specific strengths.\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods & Experiments)\nNumbered list of specific weaknesses, with severity (Minor/Moderate/Major/Critical).\n\n### Questions for Authors\nNumbered list of clarifying questions.\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\nWhat would strengthen the paper.\n\n### Scores\n- Quality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\n### Verdict\nOne paragraph summary of your assessment.","agentId":"aa29a1454b7ab0581","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper presents a cross-architecture representational analysis of three Vision-Language-Action models (OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA) across four LIBERO task suites. Using layer-wise action probes, attention profiling, and counterfactual instruction perturbations, the authors identify three patterns: (1) R-squared exit collapse is consistently associated with VLM-to-VLA transitions but absent in VLA-to-VLA and NLP-only fine-tuning; (2) all trained VLAs exhibit substantial counterfactual instruction sensitivity; and (3) L1 instruction-dominant attention is largely inherited from pretrained backbones rather than acquired during VLA training. The paper contributes a systematic decomposition of training-associated versus inherited representational structure in VLAs.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths (Methods & Experiments)\n\n1. **Well-designed factorial controls.** The trained/reference contrast across architectures with different reference baseline types (VLA-to-VLA for OpenVLA vs. VLM-to-VLA for TracVLA and NORA) provides a natural experiment that cleanly isolates the contribution of action-oriented training. The NLP finetuning control across all three backbone families (Llama-2-7b-chat, Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct, Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct) with uniform Delta-R-squared of 0.000 is a compelling negative control.\n\n2. **Triangulation across complementary methods.** The paper combines probing (correlational/decodability), counterfactual perturbation (behavioral input-output), and attention head ablation (interventional). These methods address different aspects of instruction routing -- where information is decodable, whether it functionally matters, and whether identified structures carry relevant signals. The convergent evidence table (Appendix Table 19) explicitly maps each method to its evidence type and limitation.\n\n3. **Thorough robustness analysis.** The appendix is remarkably comprehensive: label permutation controls, probe seed sensitivity (5 seeds with std < 0.002), linear vs. MLP probe comparison, demo subsampling, distance metric invariance (cosine vs. Euclidean, r > 0.989), per-DOF RSA decomposition, threshold sensitivity analysis (30-70% for attention dominance, 0.05-0.20 for Delta-R-squared), and TOST equivalence testing for cross-architecture peak convergence.\n\n4. **Honest statistical reporting.** The paper acknowledges underpowered analyses (RSA training effect power = 0.356, Delta-R-squared correlation with success rates p = 0.10 at n = 9), reports Bonferroni correction across all 63 tests (40/45 survive), and explicitly flags which auxiliary comparisons lose significance under correction. The bootstrap CI methodology for Delta-R-squared with demo-level resampling and fixed canonical peak layer (avoiding winner's curse) is methodologically sound.\n\n5. **The dose-response ablation.** Progressive ablation of 4 to 32 L1 instruction-dominant heads producing monotonic trajectory divergence (d = 0.482 to 0.639) with bootstrap CIs provides genuine interventional evidence beyond correlational probing. The specificity control (instruction-dominant vs. random vs. image-dominant subsets of equal size) adds rigor, even though the modest gap (8%) actually undermines the specificity claim -- which the paper commendably acknowledges.\n\n6. **Cross-architecture design with maximal structural diversity.** Table A11 documents that TracVLA and NORA differ on nearly every major design axis (backbone family, attention mechanism, vision encoder, integration method, training data). This diversity strengthens the generality of shared findings beyond what N=2 alone would suggest.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses (Methods & Experiments)\n\n1. **(Major) N=2 VLM-to-VLA transitions limits generalizability.** The core exit collapse finding rests on only two independent VLM-to-VLA transitions. While the permutation test yields p = 0.008, this is across 9 cells (many from the same architectures, hence not independent). The paper acknowledges this but the main text claims sometimes read stronger than the evidence warrants (e.g., \"consistently emerges\" from 6/6 cells that span only 2 architectures). The three-tier gradient has n=1 at Tier 2 (only OpenVLA as a general-purpose VLA). Architectures outside the causal-decoder family (flow-matching, diffusion) are untested.\n\n2. **(Major) No closed-loop behavioral validation.** All analyses are open-loop. The paper reports an attempted closed-loop evaluation that achieved SR=0/3, attributed to \"known reproducibility challenges.\" The correlation between Delta-R-squared and published success rates (rho = 0.583, p = 0.10, n = 9) is suggestive but non-significant and underpowered. The ablation finding that instruction-head ablation produces d = 0.65 trajectory divergence with essentially zero change in ground-truth MSE (0.498 to 0.497, p = 0.52) raises a genuine question about functional significance: if removing 24/32 instruction-dominant heads does not increase action error, what behavioral role do these heads actually play? The \"direction rather than precision\" interpretation is post-hoc.\n\n3. **(Moderate) Counterfactual metric incomparability across 5/8 cells.** NORA and reference VLMs use cosine distance on hidden representations while OpenVLA and TracVLA use Cohen's d on action MSE. The paper correctly identifies this as a limitation and avoids cross-metric ratios, but it means the counterfactual finding cannot be uniformly evaluated across the full matrix. The NORA Goal saturation (cosine distance = 1.0) likely reflects numerical issues rather than genuine maximal sensitivity.\n\n4. **(Moderate) Delta-R-squared > 0.10 threshold is descriptive rather than principled.** The paper presents this as a \"natural effect-size boundary\" and shows that any threshold in (0.097, 0.156) yields identical grouping. However, the gap of 0.059 between groups is not large, and the threshold analysis is performed on the same data used to establish the pattern. The 2 of 9 CI overlap (OpenVLA Goal upper bound 0.110 vs. TracVLA Spatial lower bound 0.091) weakens the clean separation claim. There is no theoretical grounding for why 10% explained variance drop should be the meaningful boundary.\n\n5. **(Moderate) The specificity control result actually weakens the \"dedicated routing\" narrative.** Instruction-dominant head ablation (d = 0.65) vs. random-24 head ablation (d = 0.60) yields only an 8% gap. The paper interprets this as \"distributed, attention-graded redistribution,\" which is honest, but it means the identified instruction-dominant heads are not meaningfully more important than arbitrary head selections. The random-subset analysis (Figure A6) shows that dominant-head ablation falls below all 30 random subsets for R-squared increase, further complicating the story. These findings are internally consistent but reduce the practical value of the instruction-dominant head classification.\n\n6. **(Moderate) NORA analyses are incomplete.** Only 2 of 4 LIBERO suites are available for NORA (Object and Long-Horizon checkpoints unavailable). NORA Goal has unreliable bootstrap CIs (exit-layer R-squared approximately -0.006). NORA counterfactual analysis is limited to cosine distance on hidden representations. This means the third architecture contributes substantially less evidence than the other two.\n\n7. **(Minor) NLP control design has a gap.** The NLP controls (chat-finetuned models) lack vision encoders, so they receive text-only input. This means they test whether NLP finetuning of a text-only model produces exit collapse, not whether NLP finetuning of a VLM produces exit collapse. The Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct footnote acknowledges this for NORA but the mismatch applies to all three controls. A more compelling control would be an instruction-tuned VLM (e.g., a VLM finetuned on visual QA but not actions).\n\n8. **(Minor) OpenVLA reference baseline is not a clean \"before/after\" comparison.** The OpenVLA reference is OXE-pretrained on 970K episodes -- already a fully trained VLA. The trained model is then further finetuned on LIBERO. This makes the OpenVLA VLA-to-VLA comparison test something quite different from the TracVLA/NORA VLM-to-VLA comparisons: it tests the marginal effect of task-specific adaptation on top of massive pre-training, not the fundamental VLA transformation.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The ablation produces d = 0.65 trajectory divergence with Delta-MSE approximately 0. Can you provide the success rate impact (even estimated) to assess whether the identified routing structures affect task completion? The \"direction rather than precision\" interpretation needs empirical grounding beyond the MSE-invariance observation.\n\n2. For the NLP controls, have you considered running the same analysis on instruction-tuned VLMs (e.g., LLaVA-chat or InstructBLIP) that receive visual input but have no action training? This would more directly control for multimodal instruction tuning.\n\n3. The permutation test (p = 0.008) treats each architecture-suite cell as independent, but cells from the same architecture share model weights. How does this dependence affect the test's validity? Have you considered a hierarchical permutation that respects the architecture grouping?\n\n4. TracVLA systematically produces higher counterfactual effect sizes (d = 0.97-1.52) than OpenVLA (d = 0.45-1.21). Is this a genuine architectural difference or could it reflect differences in action tokenization, training data scale, or fine-tuning quality?\n\n5. The NORA Goal exit-layer R-squared of approximately -0.006 with unreliable bootstrap CIs -- what is the interpretation here? Negative R-squared from the probe suggests representations at the exit layer are actively anti-correlated with actions, which seems unusual.\n\n6. You report that training intermediate checkpoints are unavailable. Could the exit collapse onset be estimated by training a small VLA from scratch with periodic checkpointing, rather than relying solely on the pre/post comparison?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n1. **Closed-loop behavioral evaluation.** Even if full LIBERO rollouts are challenging, a simplified behavioral test (e.g., comparing action predictions against ground truth on held-out demonstrations with accuracy/success metrics rather than MSE) would strengthen the functional significance claims.\n\n2. **Additional VLA architectures.** Testing on at least one diffusion or flow-matching VLA (e.g., Pi-0, Octo) would test whether exit collapse is specific to autoregressive/tokenized-action architectures. Testing a second general-purpose VLA at Tier 2 is necessary before the three-tier gradient can be taken seriously.\n\n3. **Training dynamics.** Even coarse-grained analysis at 2-3 intermediate checkpoints would reveal whether exit collapse is sudden or gradual, which has implications for understanding the mechanism.\n\n4. **Multimodal NLP control.** An instruction-tuned VLM with visual input (no action training) would be a more appropriate control than text-only chat models.\n\n5. **Effect of model scale.** All three models are in the 3-7B parameter range. Testing whether the findings hold at larger scales (e.g., 13B+ models) or smaller scales would assess scale sensitivity.\n\n6. **Cross-instruction probe on TracVLA and NORA.** The full cross-instruction classification analysis (dense layer profile, LOOCV) is only reported for OpenVLA and partially for TracVLA. Extending this to all three architectures would strengthen the cross-architecture claim.\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Quality: 7/10** -- The experimental methodology is thorough with appropriate controls, robustness checks, and honest statistical reporting. The main weaknesses are N=2 for the core VLM-to-VLA finding, the absence of closed-loop validation, and the gap in the NLP control design.\n- **Clarity: 6/10** -- The main text is well-organized with clean separation between trained-associated and inherited effects. However, the paper occasionally makes claims that read stronger than the evidence supports. The method section is clear. The appendix is comprehensive but dense.\n- **Significance: 5/10** -- The decomposition of training-associated vs. inherited routing is a useful contribution to VLA understanding, but the lack of behavioral validation and the limited architectural scope (N=2 for the key finding, all causal-decoder) constrain the impact. The practical implications (checkpoint screening, transfer learning guidance) remain speculative without validation.\n- **Relevance: 6/10** -- The paper studies VLAs and LIBERO, which are directly relevant to robot learning. However, the purely analytical nature with no closed-loop evaluation, no policy improvement, and failed reproduction attempts weakens the robotics connection. The representational analysis methodology is more aligned with interpretability venues.\n- **Overall: 6/10** -- A methodologically sound analytical contribution with a comprehensive experimental framework. The main findings (exit collapse in VLM-to-VLA, inherited L1 attention) are interesting and well-supported within their scope, but the limited generalizability (N=2 transitions, single benchmark family, no behavioral validation) places the paper marginally above the acceptance threshold for a robotics venue.\n- **Confidence: 4/5** -- I have read the full paper, appendix, and supporting materials carefully. I am familiar with the probing/RSA/CKA methodology and the VLA literature. My confidence is slightly reduced because I cannot independently verify the numerical results.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict\n\nThis paper presents a carefully designed representational analysis of VLA fine-tuning that introduces useful methodological contributions (the factorial trained/reference design, the convergent evidence framework) and identifies an interesting phenomenon (R-squared exit collapse specific to VLM-to-VLA transitions). The statistical reporting is unusually honest for this field, with explicit power analyses, multiple comparison corrections, and acknowledged limitations. However, the paper's central finding rests on N=2 independent VLM-to-VLA transitions, all within the causal-decoder VLA family, evaluated only on LIBERO. The lack of closed-loop behavioral validation is a significant gap for a robotics venue: the discovery that ablating 24/32 \"instruction-dominant\" heads produces near-zero MSE change undermines claims about functional significance that behavioral evidence could have resolved. The NLP controls, while useful, test text-only models rather than multimodal models without action training, leaving an interpretive gap. For CoRL, the paper sits at the boundary: the methodology and findings have genuine value for the VLA community, but the limited scope and absent behavioral grounding make it a borderline submission. Adding one additional VLA architecture family (diffusion/flow-matching) and any form of behavioral validation would substantially strengthen the contribution."}],"totalDurationMs":174137,"totalTokens":86916,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1504,"cache_read_input_tokens":81855,"output_tokens":3556,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cach