{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y4Hb9JbcXyLY73TkTTPeEh","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review as Reviewer 2 (R2), focused on Novelty and Related Work.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper presents a cross-architecture representational analysis of three decoder-only Vision-Language-Action models (OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA) across four LIBERO task suites, using layer-wise action probes, L1 attention profiling, and counterfactual instruction perturbation. The three main findings are: (1) R-squared exit collapse is specific to VLM-to-VLA transitions and absent in VLA-to-VLA and NLP fine-tuning; (2) trained VLAs exhibit substantial counterfactual sensitivity to instruction content; (3) L1 instruction-dominant attention is inherited from pretrained backbones rather than acquired through VLA training. The paper carefully decomposes training-associated effects from inherited base-model priors.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n1. **The trained-vs-reference decomposition is genuinely novel in the VLA literature.** The literature map I checked confirms that no prior VLA interpretability work (Grant et al. 2026, Swann et al. 2026, Haon et al. 2025, Rizzardo et al. 2026, Lu et al. 2025, Molinari et al. 2025) performs a systematic comparison of trained versus untrained/reference representations within the same architecture. All prior work analyzes a single checkpoint or compares across architectures. This decomposition -- asking what training adds versus what the backbone already provides -- is a legitimate and important new angle.\n\n2. **The cross-architecture validation is a meaningful advance over single-architecture studies.** The $3 \\times 4$ matrix spanning three backbone families (Llama-2, Phi-3, Qwen2.5) with different attention mechanisms (MHA vs. GQA), different vision encoders, and different training pipelines provides genuine evidence of generality. The paper correctly identifies that all prior representational analyses of VLAs target single architectures, and this is the first to test cross-architecture replication.\n\n3. **The exit-collapse finding (VLM-to-VLA vs. VLA-to-VLA asymmetry) is a non-obvious empirical contribution.** It was not predictable from existing work that R-squared would show a clean binary separation between transition types (6/6 VLM-to-VLA cells above 0.10, 0/4 VLA-to-VLA cells below, permutation p=0.008). The NLP controls (all three backbones showing delta-R-squared = 0.000) further strengthen the specificity claim. This is not a trivially expected result from existing LLM interpretability.\n\n4. **Related work coverage is impressively thorough for the VLA interpretability space.** The paper engages substantively with the concurrent explosion of VLA interpretability work (Grant et al. 2026, Swann et al. 2026, Buurmeijer/Rizzardo et al. 2026, Haon et al. 2025, Lu et al. 2025, Molinari et al. 2025, Fang et al. 2025/2026, Mitra et al. 2025), behavioral benchmarks (Hou 2026, Fei 2025), and continual learning (Liu 2026). The concurrent work paragraph is well-constructed, clearly differentiating \"whether\" (behavioral) from \"how much\" (representational) analyses.\n\n5. **The paper engages with core mechanistic interpretability references.** Elhage et al. (2021), Olsson et al. (2022), Wang et al. (2023, IOI circuit), Conmy et al. (2023), and Meng et al. (2022, ROME) are all cited in the related work. The VLM interpretability literature is also engaged (Fan 2026, Neo 2025, Long 2026, Zhang 2026 on instruction anchors). This coverage is stronger than typical robotics papers.\n\n6. **Honest positioning of claims.** The paper consistently uses correlational language (\"associated with,\" \"consistent with\"), acknowledges N=2 for VLM-to-VLA transitions, notes that probes measure decodability not causal reliance, and downgrades earlier stronger claims (e.g., vision encoder geometry is presented as an \"observation\" rather than a core claim). The claim-evidence-scope figure in the appendix is a model of scientific transparency.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n1. **(Moderate) The L1 attention finding is largely expected from existing VLM interpretability work.** Zhang et al. (2026, \"Instruction Anchor\") -- which the paper cites -- already demonstrates that instruction tokens serve as shallow-layer modality arbitration anchors in VLMs. The finding that L1 instruction-dominant attention is inherited from pretrained backbones is consistent with and predicted by this prior work. The paper should more explicitly acknowledge that the \"inherited routing\" finding confirms rather than extends this line of work.\n\n2. **(Moderate) Insufficient engagement with the residual stream and representation transformation literature in LLMs.** The paper focuses on probing and attention analysis but does not engage with the substantial body of work on how representations transform across layers in LLMs -- Geva et al. (2023, \"Dissecting Recall of Factual Associations in Auto-Regressive Language Models\"), Dar et al. (2023, \"Analyzing Transformers in Embedding Space\"), or Belrose et al. (2023, \"Eliciting Latent Predictions from Transformers with the Tuned Lens\"). The exit-collapse phenomenon -- where action-relevant information is decodable at mid-layers but degrades at exit layers -- has parallels to the \"logit lens\" literature showing that intermediate layers often produce better predictions than final layers for certain tasks. This connection is entirely missing.\n\n3. **(Moderate) The counterfactual perturbation methodology is not novel.** Counterfactual instruction perturbation (empty, wrong task, shuffled, paraphrased) is a standard evaluation protocol already used by Fang et al. (2026, LIBERO-CF), Hou et al. (2026, LangGap), and Fei et al. (2025, LIBERO-Plus) at the behavioral level. The paper's contribution is applying it to hidden representations rather than task success, but the methodology itself is borrowed. The paper should be more explicit about what is new (representational measurement) versus what is adapted (perturbation protocol).\n\n4. **(Minor) The \"three-tier routing gradient\" framing overstates the evidence.** Tier 2 contains exactly one architecture (OpenVLA with OXE pretraining). The paper acknowledges this limitation in the appendix but still uses the three-tier framing as a conceptual contribution in the main text. A two-tier distinction (VLM vs. VLA) would be better supported; the three-tier version is speculative given N=1 at Tier 2.\n\n5. **(Minor) Missing related work on probing in NLP beyond the cited papers.** Tenney et al. (2019, \"BERT Rediscovers the Classical NLP Pipeline\") and Jawahar et al. (2019, \"What Does BERT Learn about the Structure of Language?\") are canonical references for layer-wise probing analyses showing that different layers encode different linguistic properties. The observation that action-relevant information peaks at mid-layers has a direct analog in these findings (syntactic information peaking at middle BERT layers). Rogers et al. (2020, \"A Primer in BERTology\") would also strengthen the methodological positioning.\n\n6. **(Minor) Voita et al. (2019, \"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention\") is missing.** This paper demonstrates that only a small subset of attention heads are important and that many can be pruned -- directly relevant to the paper's analysis of instruction-dominant vs. non-dominant heads and the claim about distributed vs. concentrated routing.\n\n7. **(Minor) The RSA-CKA dissociation is well-known and not a novel contribution.** The paper frames the divergence between RSA and CKA orderings as a finding (Section on Metric Sensitivity). However, it is well-established that different similarity metrics can disagree (Ding et al. 2021, \"Grounding Representation Similarity Through Statistical Testing\"; Raghu et al. 2017, \"SVCCA\"). Presenting this as a finding rather than a known methodological caveat slightly oversells the contribution.\n\n8. **(Moderate) The paper does not adequately position itself relative to the \"feature distortion\" literature.** Kumar et al. (2022) is cited once, but the exit-collapse finding has deep connections to the broader literature on how fine-tuning distorts pretrained representations (e.g., Merchant et al. 2020, \"What Happens to BERT Embeddings During Fine-tuning?\"; Mosbach et al. 2020; Zhou et al. 2022). The exit collapse could be understood as a specific instance of fine-tuning-induced representation distortion at output layers, and this connection deserves more than a passing mention.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The NLP controls all show R-squared = 0.155 with zero variance across all layers for all three backbones. Can you confirm this is not an artifact? What action labels are these NLP-only models being probed against, given they have never seen robot data? Is 0.155 simply the R-squared achievable from between-task variance in the LIBERO action space?\n\n2. The exit-collapse pattern is observed in VLM-to-VLA but not VLA-to-VLA transitions. Have you considered the alternative explanation that this simply reflects the OpenVLA reference already having gone through its own exit collapse during OXE pretraining, leaving no further collapse to occur? If so, this would be a statement about training convergence rather than a qualitative distinction between transition types.\n\n3. The paper claims instruction routing is \"inherited\" from pretrained backbones based on L1 attention head counts. But attention head counts at a single threshold (50%) are a coarse measure. Have you examined whether the actual attention distributions (not just binarized counts) differ between trained and reference models?\n\n4. How do you reconcile the finding that L1 instruction attention is \"inherited\" with the OpenVLA three-condition gradient showing 0/32 (random), 9/32 (Llama-2), 24/32 (trained)? The jump from 9 to 24 seems training-induced, not inherited. Is the \"inherited\" claim based primarily on TracVLA (32/32 to 27-30/32) and NORA (13-14/16 to 12-13/16)?\n\n5. The correlation between delta-R-squared and published success rates (rho=0.583, p=0.10, n=9) is presented as suggestive but non-significant. Given that this is the primary link between your representational findings and practical relevance, what would a significant result require -- more architectures, more suites, or a fundamentally different validation approach?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n- **Logit lens / tuned lens literature**: nostalgebraist (2020, \"interpreting GPT: the logit lens\"), Belrose et al. (2023, \"Eliciting Latent Predictions from Transformers with the Tuned Lens\"). The exit-collapse finding is structurally similar to the observation that intermediate representations can be more informative than final-layer representations when decoded through layer-appropriate probes.\n\n- **Geva et al. (2023)**: \"Dissecting Recall of Factual Associations in Auto-Regressive Language Models\" -- directly relevant to understanding how information flows and transforms across layers.\n\n- **Tenney et al. (2019)**: \"BERT Rediscovers the Classical NLP Pipeline\" -- canonical layer-wise probing reference showing different information types peak at different layers.\n\n- **Rogers et al. (2020)**: \"A Primer in BERTology\" -- comprehensive survey of probing studies in NLP, relevant for methodological positioning.\n\n- **Voita et al. (2019)**: \"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned\" -- directly relevant to instruction-dominant vs. non-dominant head analysis.\n\n- **Merchant et al. (2020)**: \"What Happens to BERT Embeddings During Fine-tuning?\" -- relevant to understanding exit-layer representation changes during fine-tuning.\n\n- **Ding et al. (2021)**: \"Grounding Representation Similarity Through Statistical Testing\" -- relevant to the RSA/CKA dissociation discussion.\n\n- **Raghu et al. (2017)**: \"SVCCA: Singular Vector Canonical Correlation Analysis for Deep Learning Dynamics and Interpretability\" -- relevant to representation similarity methodology.\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Originality**: 6/10 -- The trained-vs-reference decomposition and the exit-collapse transition-type specificity are genuinely new. However, individual methods (probing, attention analysis, counterfactual perturbation) are standard, and some findings (inherited L1 routing, RSA-CKA dissociation) confirm rather than extend prior knowledge. The cross-architecture validation adds meaningful scope but the underlying question -- how does fine-tuning reshape representations -- is well-studied in NLP.\n\n- **Clarity**: 8/10 -- The paper is well-organized, uses careful correlational language, and the claim-evidence mapping is exemplary. The distinction between VLM-to-VLA and VLA-to-VLA transitions is clearly drawn throughout.\n\n- **Significance**: 5/10 -- The findings are interesting but primarily observational. The practical implications (checkpoint screening via delta-R-squared, mid-layer features for distillation, uniform pruning) are speculative and not validated. The behavioral correlation is non-significant (p=0.10). The paper provides a useful characterization but does not yet enable concrete design improvements for VLAs.\n\n- **Relevance**: 8/10 -- Directly addresses VLA design and understanding, highly relevant to the CoRL audience. The LIBERO benchmark and the three VLA architectures studied are central to current robot learning research.\n\n- **Overall**: 6/10 -- A solid empirical study with some genuinely novel findings (exit-collapse specificity, cross-architecture validation) but limited in practical impact and with novelty that is incremental relative to the combined LLM interpretability and VLA behavioral analysis literatures. The paper is better positioned as a characterization study than as offering fundamental mechanistic understanding.\n\n- **Confidence**: 4/5 -- I am familiar with both the VLA and mechanistic interpretability literatures and have read the paper thoroughly including the extensive appendix.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict\n\nThis paper makes a credible contribution to VLA understanding through its trained-vs-reference decomposition and the cross-architecture exit-collapse finding. The distinction between VLM-to-VLA and VLA-to-VLA transition effects is genuinely novel and well-supported statistically. However, the paper sits at the boundary of CoRL acceptance. On the positive side, the cross-architecture validation (3 backbones, 4 task suites, 10/12 cells filled) is thorough and the NLP controls are well-designed. On the negative side, the findings are purely observational with no closed-loop behavioral validation, the practical implications remain speculative, and the paper does not sufficiently engage with the broader representation transformation literature from NLP (logit lens, BERTology probing, fine-tuning distortion) that would contextualize the exit-collapse finding. The L1 attention \"inherited routing\" result, while correct, largely confirms what Zhang et al. (2026) already showed about instruction anchoring in VLMs. I lean toward marginal accept contingent on the authors strengthening the positioning relative to NLP representation analysis literature and more honestly acknowledging which findings confirm versus extend prior work."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: adf4b3678d1ef1999 (use SendMessage with to: 'adf4b3678d1ef1999' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 102753\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 181366</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"456ce593-1890-41ff-b013-584e451fd2fb","timestamp":"2026-05-27T03:21:55.792Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus area is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Your Task\nProduce an independent, detailed review of the paper \"Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Token-Level Analysis\" submitted to CoRL 2026.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates papers on six dimensions using a 1-10 scale:\n- **Originality**: Novel problems, methods, or combinations\n- **Quality**: Technical soundness and support for claims\n- **Clarity**: Writing quality and organization\n- **Significance**: Importance and advancement of the field\n- **Relevance**: Connection to robot learning and CoRL audience\n- **Limitations**: Honest acknowledgment of limitations\n\nScale: 10=Top 5% seminal, 9=Top 15% strong accept, 8=Top 50% clear accept, 7=Good accept, 6=Marginally above threshold, 5=Marginally below, 4=Not good enough reject, 3=Clear reject, 2=Strong reject, 1=Trivial/wrong.\nCoRL acceptance rate ~30-35%.\n\n## Your Focus: Novelty and Related Work\nYou must deeply evaluate:\n1. **Novelty of the problem**: Is \"how VLA training reshapes instruction routing at the token level\" a genuinely new and important question?\n2. **Novelty of findings**: Are the three main findings (bookend routing, R² exit collapse, vision encoder invariance) genuinely new insights or largely expected from existing LLM interpretability work?\n3. **Related work coverage**: The paper cites RT-2 (Brohan 2023), OpenVLA (Kim 2024), Grant et al. 2026, Haon et al. 2025, probing (Alain & Bengio 2017, Hewitt & Liang 2019), attention analysis (Clark et al. 2019), RSA (Kornblith 2019, Kriegeskorte 2008), LIBERO (Liu 2024), OXE (2023). Is this sufficient? What's missing?\n4. **Positioning relative to LLM interpretability**: There is a massive body of work on mechanistic interpretability of LLMs (circuits, induction heads, residual stream analysis, etc.). Does the paper adequately position itself?\n5. **Positioning relative to VLA behavioral work**: Grant et al. 2026 and Haon et al. 2025 are cited as behavioral analyses. Is the distinction clearly drawn?\n6. **Novelty of the framework**: Six complementary methods (RSA, CKA, probes, classification, attention, counterfactual) — is this combination novel or standard?\n7. **Incremental vs. fundamental**: Does the paper offer fundamental mechanistic understanding or incremental observations?\n8. **Practical impact**: Does the analysis lead to actionable insights for VLA design? Or is it purely observational?\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper presents a systematic framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only VLAs validated across 3 architectures and 4 task suites. Three main findings:\n\n**Finding 1 (C2): Immediate dedicated routing.** VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from mid-layer inverted-U (pretrained LLM, peak L9-16) to entry-exit bookend (peaks L0-1, L31). Three routing strategies: trained VLA (99.6% at L1), pretrained LLM (80.2% at L1, saturates L5), random init (100% at L1, decays to 82% at L31).\n\n**Finding 2: R² exit collapse.** Action-decodability drops at exit layers during VLM→VLA transition (6/6 cells, Δ=0.138-0.691) but not VLA→VLA (0/4, Δ<0.086). NLP control ΔR²=0.000.\n\n**Finding 3 (C3): Vision encoder geometry.** Frozen encoder provides RSA ρ=0.615 (highest), invariant to training/instruction (Δρ<0.017). DINOv2 tops RSA ranking (0.692) but CKA reverses ordering.\n\nUnified by three-tier routing gradient: VLM base → general VLA → task-specific VLA.\n\n## Key Claims to Evaluate for Novelty\n- \"how training reshapes instruction routing and information flow at the token level remains uncharacterized\" — Is this true?\n- \"whether such effects generalize across architectures is unknown\" — Is cross-architecture validation truly new?\n- The \"three-tier routing gradient\" — Is this a novel conceptual contribution or obvious given the training pipeline?\n- \"instruction routing is a staged emergent property\" — Novel insight or expected?\n- RSA-CKA dissociation as \"metric dependence\" finding — Novel or well-known?\n\n## Related Work You Should Check Against\n- **Mechanistic interpretability of LLMs**: Elhage et al. (2021), Olsson et al. (2022) on induction heads, Conmy et al. (2023) on circuits, Geva et al. (2023) on MLP knowledge neurons. Does the paper engage with this literature?\n- **VLM interpretability**: Any work on how vision-language models process multi-modal information internally?\n- **Probing in NLP**: Beyond Alain & Bengio (2017) and Hewitt & Liang (2019), there's Tenney et al. (2019), Jawahar et al. (2019), etc.\n- **Action prediction from representations**: Any prior work on probing robot policies?\n- **Attention analysis**: Beyond Clark et al. (2019), there's Voita et al. (2019) on attention head roles, Michel et al. (2019) on head pruning, etc.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in the following structure:\n\n### Summary\n2-3 sentences summarizing the paper.\n\n### Strengths (Novelty & Related Work)\nNumbered list of specific strengths.\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty & Related Work)\nNumbered list of specific weaknesses, with severity (Minor/Moderate/Major/Critical).\n\n### Questions for Authors\nNumbered list of clarifying questions.\n\n### Missing Related Work\nSpecific papers or research areas that should be discussed.\n\n### Scores\n- Originality: X/10\n- Clarity: X/10\n- Significance: X/10\n- Relevance: X/10\n- Overall: X/10\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n\n### Verdict\nOne paragraph summary of your assessment.","agentId":"adf4b3678d1ef1999","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nHere is my review as Reviewer 2 (R2), focused on Novelty and Related Work.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper presents a cross-architecture representational analysis of three decoder-only Vision-Language-Action models (OpenVLA, TracVLA, NORA) across four LIBERO task suites, using layer-wise action probes, L1 attention profiling, and counterfactual instruction perturbation. The three main findings are: (1) R-squared exit collapse is specific to VLM-to-VLA transitions and absent in VLA-to-VLA and NLP fine-tuning; (2) trained VLAs exhibit substantial counterfactual sensitivity to instruction content; (3) L1 instruction-dominant attention is inherited from pretrained backbones rather than acquired through VLA training. The paper carefully decomposes training-associated effects from inherited base-model priors.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n1. **The trained-vs-reference decomposition is genuinely novel in the VLA literature.** The literature map I checked confirms that no prior VLA interpretability work (Grant et al. 2026, Swann et al. 2026, Haon et al. 2025, Rizzardo et al. 2026, Lu et al. 2025, Molinari et al. 2025) performs a systematic comparison of trained versus untrained/reference representations within the same architecture. All prior work analyzes a single checkpoint or compares across architectures. This decomposition -- asking what training adds versus what the backbone already provides -- is a legitimate and important new angle.\n\n2. **The cross-architecture validation is a meaningful advance over single-architecture studies.** The $3 \\times 4$ matrix spanning three backbone families (Llama-2, Phi-3, Qwen2.5) with different attention mechanisms (MHA vs. GQA), different vision encoders, and different training pipelines provides genuine evidence of generality. The paper correctly identifies that all prior representational analyses of VLAs target single architectures, and this is the first to test cross-architecture replication.\n\n3. **The exit-collapse finding (VLM-to-VLA vs. VLA-to-VLA asymmetry) is a non-obvious empirical contribution.** It was not predictable from existing work that R-squared would show a clean binary separation between transition types (6/6 VLM-to-VLA cells above 0.10, 0/4 VLA-to-VLA cells below, permutation p=0.008). The NLP controls (all three backbones showing delta-R-squared = 0.000) further strengthen the specificity claim. This is not a trivially expected result from existing LLM interpretability.\n\n4. **Related work coverage is impressively thorough for the VLA interpretability space.** The paper engages substantively with the concurrent explosion of VLA interpretability work (Grant et al. 2026, Swann et al. 2026, Buurmeijer/Rizzardo et al. 2026, Haon et al. 2025, Lu et al. 2025, Molinari et al. 2025, Fang et al. 2025/2026, Mitra et al. 2025), behavioral benchmarks (Hou 2026, Fei 2025), and continual learning (Liu 2026). The concurrent work paragraph is well-constructed, clearly differentiating \"whether\" (behavioral) from \"how much\" (representational) analyses.\n\n5. **The paper engages with core mechanistic interpretability references.** Elhage et al. (2021), Olsson et al. (2022), Wang et al. (2023, IOI circuit), Conmy et al. (2023), and Meng et al. (2022, ROME) are all cited in the related work. The VLM interpretability literature is also engaged (Fan 2026, Neo 2025, Long 2026, Zhang 2026 on instruction anchors). This coverage is stronger than typical robotics papers.\n\n6. **Honest positioning of claims.** The paper consistently uses correlational language (\"associated with,\" \"consistent with\"), acknowledges N=2 for VLM-to-VLA transitions, notes that probes measure decodability not causal reliance, and downgrades earlier stronger claims (e.g., vision encoder geometry is presented as an \"observation\" rather than a core claim). The claim-evidence-scope figure in the appendix is a model of scientific transparency.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses (Novelty & Related Work)\n\n1. **(Moderate) The L1 attention finding is largely expected from existing VLM interpretability work.** Zhang et al. (2026, \"Instruction Anchor\") -- which the paper cites -- already demonstrates that instruction tokens serve as shallow-layer modality arbitration anchors in VLMs. The finding that L1 instruction-dominant attention is inherited from pretrained backbones is consistent with and predicted by this prior work. The paper should more explicitly acknowledge that the \"inherited routing\" finding confirms rather than extends this line of work.\n\n2. **(Moderate) Insufficient engagement with the residual stream and representation transformation literature in LLMs.** The paper focuses on probing and attention analysis but does not engage with the substantial body of work on how representations transform across layers in LLMs -- Geva et al. (2023, \"Dissecting Recall of Factual Associations in Auto-Regressive Language Models\"), Dar et al. (2023, \"Analyzing Transformers in Embedding Space\"), or Belrose et al. (2023, \"Eliciting Latent Predictions from Transformers with the Tuned Lens\"). The exit-collapse phenomenon -- where action-relevant information is decodable at mid-layers but degrades at exit layers -- has parallels to the \"logit lens\" literature showing that intermediate layers often produce better predictions than final layers for certain tasks. This connection is entirely missing.\n\n3. **(Moderate) The counterfactual perturbation methodology is not novel.** Counterfactual instruction perturbation (empty, wrong task, shuffled, paraphrased) is a standard evaluation protocol already used by Fang et al. (2026, LIBERO-CF), Hou et al. (2026, LangGap), and Fei et al. (2025, LIBERO-Plus) at the behavioral level. The paper's contribution is applying it to hidden representations rather than task success, but the methodology itself is borrowed. The paper should be more explicit about what is new (representational measurement) versus what is adapted (perturbation protocol).\n\n4. **(Minor) The \"three-tier routing gradient\" framing overstates the evidence.** Tier 2 contains exactly one architecture (OpenVLA with OXE pretraining). The paper acknowledges this limitation in the appendix but still uses the three-tier framing as a conceptual contribution in the main text. A two-tier distinction (VLM vs. VLA) would be better supported; the three-tier version is speculative given N=1 at Tier 2.\n\n5. **(Minor) Missing related work on probing in NLP beyond the cited papers.** Tenney et al. (2019, \"BERT Rediscovers the Classical NLP Pipeline\") and Jawahar et al. (2019, \"What Does BERT Learn about the Structure of Language?\") are canonical references for layer-wise probing analyses showing that different layers encode different linguistic properties. The observation that action-relevant information peaks at mid-layers has a direct analog in these findings (syntactic information peaking at middle BERT layers). Rogers et al. (2020, \"A Primer in BERTology\") would also strengthen the methodological positioning.\n\n6. **(Minor) Voita et al. (2019, \"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention\") is missing.** This paper demonstrates that only a small subset of attention heads are important and that many can be pruned -- directly relevant to the paper's analysis of instruction-dominant vs. non-dominant heads and the claim about distributed vs. concentrated routing.\n\n7. **(Minor) The RSA-CKA dissociation is well-known and not a novel contribution.** The paper frames the divergence between RSA and CKA orderings as a finding (Section on Metric Sensitivity). However, it is well-established that different similarity metrics can disagree (Ding et al. 2021, \"Grounding Representation Similarity Through Statistical Testing\"; Raghu et al. 2017, \"SVCCA\"). Presenting this as a finding rather than a known methodological caveat slightly oversells the contribution.\n\n8. **(Moderate) The paper does not adequately position itself relative to the \"feature distortion\" literature.** Kumar et al. (2022) is cited once, but the exit-collapse finding has deep connections to the broader literature on how fine-tuning distorts pretrained representations (e.g., Merchant et al. 2020, \"What Happens to BERT Embeddings During Fine-tuning?\"; Mosbach et al. 2020; Zhou et al. 2022). The exit collapse could be understood as a specific instance of fine-tuning-induced representation distortion at output layers, and this connection deserves more than a passing mention.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. The NLP controls all show R-squared = 0.155 with zero variance across all layers for all three backbones. Can you confirm this is not an artifact? What action labels are these NLP-only models being probed against, given they have never seen robot data? Is 0.155 simply the R-squared achievable from between-task variance in the LIBERO action space?\n\n2. The exit-collapse pattern is observed in VLM-to-VLA but not VLA-to-VLA transitions. Have you considered the alternative explanation that this simply reflects the OpenVLA reference already having gone through its own exit collapse during OXE pretraining, leaving no further collapse to occur? If so, this would be a statement about training convergence rather than a qualitative distinction between transition types.\n\n3. The paper claims instruction routing is \"inherited\" from pretrained backbones based on L1 attention head counts. But attention head counts at a single threshold (50%) are a coarse measure. Have you examined whether the actual attention distributions (not just binarized counts) differ between trained and reference models?\n\n4. How do you reconcile the finding that L1 instruction attention is \"inherited\" with the OpenVLA three-condition gradient showing 0/32 (random), 9/32 (Llama-2), 24/32 (trained)? The jump from 9 to 24 seems training-induced, not inherited. Is the \"inherited\" claim based primarily on TracVLA (32/32 to 27-30/32) and NORA (13-14/16 to 12-13/16)?\n\n5. The correlation between delta-R-squared and published success rates (rho=0.583, p=0.10, n=9) is presented as suggestive but non-significant. Given that this is the primary link between your representational findings and practical relevance, what would a significant result require -- more architectures, more suites, or a fundamentally different validation approach?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n- **Logit lens / tuned lens literature**: nostalgebraist (2020, \"interpreting GPT: the logit lens\"), Belrose et al. (2023, \"Eliciting Latent Predictions from Transformers with the Tuned Lens\"). The exit-collapse finding is structurally similar to the observation that intermediate representations can be more informative than final-layer representations when decoded through layer-appropriate probes.\n\n- **Geva et al. (2023)**: \"Dissecting Recall of Factual Associations in Auto-Regressive Language Models\" -- directly relevant to understanding how information flows and transforms across layers.\n\n- **Tenney et al. (2019)**: \"BERT Rediscovers the Classical NLP Pipeline\" -- canonical layer-wise probing reference showing different information types peak at different layers.\n\n- **Rogers et al. (2020)**: \"A Primer in BERTology\" -- comprehensive survey of probing studies in NLP, relevant for methodological positioning.\n\n- **Voita et al. (2019)**: \"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention: Specialized Heads Do the Heavy Lifting, the Rest Can Be Pruned\" -- directly relevant to instruction-dominant vs. non-dominant head analysis.\n\n- **Merchant et al. (2020)**: \"What Happens to BERT Embeddings During Fine-tuning?\" -- relevant to understanding exit-layer representation changes during fine-tuning.\n\n- **Ding et al. (2021)**: \"Grounding Representation Similarity Through Statistical Testing\" -- relevant to the RSA/CKA dissociation discussion.\n\n- **Raghu et al. (2017)**: \"SVCCA: Singular Vector Canonical Correlation Analysis for Deep Learning Dynamics and Interpretability\" -- relevant to representation similarity methodology.\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Originality**: 6/10 -- The trained-vs-reference decomposition and the exit-collapse transition-type specificity are genuinely new. However, individual methods (probing, attention analysis, counterfactual perturbation) are standard, and some findings (inherited L1 routing, RSA-CKA dissociation) confirm rather than extend prior knowledge. The cross-architecture validation adds meaningful scope but the underlying question -- how does fine-tuning reshape representations -- is well-studied in NLP.\n\n- **Clarity**: 8/10 -- The paper is well-organized, uses careful correlational language, and the claim-evidence mapping is exemplary. The distinction between VLM-to-VLA and VLA-to-VLA transitions is clearly drawn throughout.\n\n- **Significance**: 5/10 -- The findings are interesting but primarily observational. The practical implications (checkpoint screening via delta-R-squared, mid-layer features for distillation, uniform pruning) are speculative and not validated. The behavioral correlation is non-significant (p=0.10). The paper provides a useful characterization but does not yet enable concrete design improvements for VLAs.\n\n- **Relevance**: 8/10 -- Directly addresses VLA design and understanding, highly relevant to the CoRL audience. The LIBERO benchmark and the three VLA architectures studied are central to current robot learning research.\n\n- **Overall**: 6/10 -- A solid empirical study with some genuinely novel findings (exit-collapse specificity, cross-architecture validation) but limited in practical impact and with novelty that is incremental relative to the combined LLM interpretability and VLA behavioral analysis literatures. The paper is better positioned as a characterization study than as offering fundamental mechanistic understanding.\n\n- **Confidence**: 4/5 -- I am familiar with both the VLA and mechanistic interpretability literatures and have read the paper thoroughly including the extensive appendix.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict\n\nThis paper makes a credible contribution to VLA understanding through its trained-vs-reference decomposition and the cross-architecture exit-collapse finding. The distinction between VLM-to-VLA and VLA-to-VLA transition effects is genuinely novel and well-supported statistically. However, the paper sits at the boundary of CoRL acceptance. On the positive side, the cross-architecture validation (3 backbones, 4 task suites, 10/12 cells filled) is thorough and the NLP controls are well-designed. On the negative side, the findings are purely observational with no closed-loop behavioral validation, the practical implications remain speculative, and the paper does not sufficiently engage with the broader representation transformation literature from NLP (logit lens, BERTology probing, fine-tuning distortion) that would contextualize the exit-collapse finding. The L1 attention \"inherited routing\" result, while correct, largely confirms what Zhang et al. (2026) already showed about instruction anchoring in VLMs. I lean toward marginal accept contingent on the authors strengthening the positioning relative to NLP representation analysis literature and more honestly acknowledging which findings confirm versus extend prior work."}],"totalDurationMs":181366,"totalTokens":102753,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1135,"cache_read_input_tokens":97867,"output_tokens":3748,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1135},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3748,"cache_read_input_tokens":97867,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1135,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1135,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":15,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01THyuWG7ExY9qd2xYVxtBkg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, I have both R1 and R2 reviews. Now I need to proceed with the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews ✓ (done)\nStep 2: Cross-share — each reviewer sees the other's review and responds\nStep 3: Convergence — identify agreements and disagreements\nStep 4: Synthesize — produce the final consolidated review report\n\nLet me now do Step 2: Cross-share. I'll send each reviewer the other's review and ask them to respond.\n\nActually, looking at the dream-review-full dispatch template more carefully, I should run Steps 2-4 myself as the Director, synthesizing the two reviews. Let me proceed.\n\n\nNow I'm extracting the core findings from both reviews. R1 gave the methods and experiments a 6/10 overall, praising the factorial controls and cross-architecture validation but flagging the small VLM→VLA sample size and lack of closed-loop evaluation as major concerns. The counterfactual metric comparability issue and the arbitrary 0.10 threshold also stood out as moderate weaknesses.\n\nR2 assessed novelty and related work at 6/10 overall, appreciating the trained-versus-reference decomposition and the non-obvious exit-collapse finding, though noting that the L1 result aligns with prior work by Zhang et al. and that the counterfactual approach itself isn't novel. Several gaps in the literature review emerged—missing citations on residual streams, NLP probing, and known dissociations between RSA and CKA metrics.\n\nNow I'm cross-referencing where R1 and R2 converge and diverge. Both reviewers landed on 6/10 overall and flagged the same critical limitations: only two VLM-to-VLA transitions tested, no closed-loop validation, and the three-tier gradient claim resting on a single Tier 2 datapoint. They also align on the paper's methodological rigor and honest framing, though they split on clarity—R1 sees claims outpacing evidence while R2 credits the careful language—and on relevance, where R1 rates it lower than R2 does.\n\nThe key divergence is in their emphasis: R1 focuses more on methodological gaps like the NLP control design, while R2 raises more specific concerns about missing related work. But the bottom line is consistent across both: borderline accept at 6/10, marginally above threshold. I'm now drafting the consolidated report that reflects this consensus.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10714,"cache_read_input_tokens":85630,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10714},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"618e4270-63ac-4b78-b1f7-dc9dd7af056d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01THyuWG7ExY9qd2xYVxtBkg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"两位 Reviewer 独立审稿完成（Step 1）。现在进入 Step 2（交叉分享）和 Step 3（收敛）。\n\n**R1 与 R2 核心评分对比：**\n\n| 维度 | R1 | R2 |\n|------|----|----|\n| Quality | 7 | — |\n| Originality | — | 6 |\n| Clarity | 6 | 8 |\n| Significance | 5 | 5 |\n| Relevance | 6 | 8 |\n| Overall | 6 | 6 |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n\n**共识点：**\n- Overall 6/10（marginally above threshold），方法论扎实但 scope 受限\n- N=2 VLM→VLA 转换是核心 generalizability 瓶颈\n- 缺乏 closed-loop 行为验证是对 robotics venue 的主要短板\n- 发现主要是 observational，缺乏可操作的设计指导\n- 三层级梯度（Tier 2 仅 N=1）被过度包装\n- NLP control 使用纯文本模型而非 instruction-tuned VLM，存在设计缺口\n- 统计报告和方法论的诚实度是亮点\n\n**分歧点：**\n- Clarity（R1=6 vs R2=8）：R1 认为 claims 偶尔超出证据支撑；R2 认为语言审慎、claim-evidence mapping 堪称典范\n- Relevance（R1=6 vs R2=8）：R1 认为更适合 interpretability venue；R2 认为直接服务 VLA 设计、高度契合 CoRL\n\n**收敛判断：** Clarity 取 7（R1 关于 overclaiming 的批评有理但 R2 指出的 claim-evidence 图确实存在）；Relevance 取 7（虽然纯分析性，但 VLA+LIBERO 确实是 CoRL 核心话题）。Overall 维持 6/10 共识。\n\n现在进入 Step 4：撰写综合审稿报告并提交。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10714,"cache_read_input_tokens":85630,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10714},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"57c3561c-7aee-4692-8905-342199a60302"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01THyuWG7ExY9qd2xYVxtBkg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TqUSQEP19KZQrH9RnCD5xz","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models\n\n**Paper:** Instruction Routing in Vision-Language-Action Models: A Cross-Architecture Token-Level Analysis\n**Venue:** CoRL 2026\n**Dream ID:** 20260527T022433\n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThis paper presents a systematic experimental framework for token-level analysis of decoder-only Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs), validated across three architectures (OpenVLA/Llama-2-7B, TracVLA/Phi-3-mini, NORA/Qwen2.5-VL-3B) and four LIBERO task suites. Using six complementary methods (RSA, CKA, action probes, cross-instruction classification, attention profiling, counterfactual perturbation), the paper reports three main findings: (1) VLA fine-tuning reorganizes instruction attention from mid-layer inverted-U to entry-exit bookend pattern with immediate L1 routing (99.6%); (2) R² exit collapse is a one-time signature of VLM→VLA transitions (6/6 cells, Δ=0.138–0.691) absent in VLA→VLA (0/4 cells); (3) vision encoder geometry under RSA is invariant to training and instruction content (Δρ<0.017). These are unified by a three-tier routing gradient from VLM base through general VLA to task-specific VLA.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Reviewer Assignments & Scores\n\n### R1: Methods & Experiments\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Quality | 7/10 |\n| Clarity | 6/10 |\n| Significance | 5/10 |\n| Relevance | 6/10 |\n| Overall | 6/10 |\n| Confidence | 4/5 |\n\n### R2: Novelty & Related Work\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Originality | 6/10 |\n| Clarity | 8/10 |\n| Significance | 5/10 |\n| Relevance | 8/10 |\n| Overall | 6/10 |\n| Confidence | 4/5 |\n\n### Roundtable Consensus Scores\n\n| Dimension | Consensus | Rationale |\n|-----------|-----------|-----------|\n| Originality | 6/10 | Trained-vs-reference decomposition and exit-collapse transition specificity are genuinely new; individual methods are standard |\n| Quality | 7/10 | Thorough methodology with appropriate controls, robustness checks, and unusually honest statistics; limited by N=2 for core finding |\n| Clarity | 7/10 | Well-organized with careful claim-evidence mapping; occasional overclaiming relative to evidence strength |\n| Significance | 5/10 | Observational characterization without behavioral validation or actionable design guidance |\n| Relevance | 7/10 | VLA+LIBERO directly relevant to CoRL; purely analytical nature somewhat attenuates robotics fit |\n| **Overall** | **6/10** | **Marginally above acceptance threshold** |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Consolidated Strengths\n\n### S1. Well-designed factorial controls and cross-architecture validation (R1, R2)\nThe 3×4 matrix spanning three distinct backbone families (Llama-2, Phi-3, Qwen2.5) with different attention mechanisms (MHA vs GQA), vision encoders, and training pipelines provides genuine evidence of generality. The natural experiment created by different reference baselines (VLA→VLA for OpenVLA vs VLM→VLA for TracVLA/NORA) cleanly isolates the contribution of action-oriented training. The NLP finetuning control across all three backbone families (ΔR²=0.000 uniformly) is a compelling negative control.\n\n### S2. Triangulation across complementary methods (R1)\nThe paper combines correlational/decodability (probing), behavioral/input-output (counterfactual perturbation), and interventional (attention head ablation) evidence. The six-method consistency table and convergent evidence mapping across methods is methodologically rigorous and demonstrates framework robustness.\n\n### S3. Unusually honest statistical reporting (R1, R2)\nThe paper explicitly acknowledges underpowered analyses (RSA training effect power=0.356), reports Bonferroni correction across all 63 tests (40/45 survive), flags which comparisons lose significance under correction, uses careful correlational language throughout, and presents a claim-evidence-scope mapping that distinguishes what training creates versus what it inherits. This level of transparency exceeds the norm for the field.\n\n### S4. The exit-collapse finding is non-obvious and well-supported (R2)\nThe clean binary separation between VLM→VLA (6/6 above threshold) and VLA→VLA (0/4 below) was not predictable from prior work. Combined with the NLP control (ΔR²=0.000 across three backbones), this constitutes genuine evidence that exit collapse is specific to the VLM-to-VLA transition rather than a generic fine-tuning artifact.\n\n### S5. The trained-vs-reference decomposition is novel in VLA literature (R2)\nNo prior VLA interpretability work performs systematic comparison of trained versus untrained/reference representations within the same architecture. The distinction between training-induced effects (exit collapse, counterfactual sensitivity) and inherited properties (L1 head counts) is a genuine conceptual contribution.\n\n### S6. Comprehensive engagement with concurrent VLA interpretability work (R2)\nThe related work section engages substantively with the recent explosion of VLA interpretability studies (Grant et al. 2026, Swann et al. 2026, Haon et al. 2025, etc.) and core mechanistic interpretability references (Elhage et al. 2021, Olsson et al. 2022, etc.).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Consolidated Weaknesses\n\n### W1. [Major] N=2 independent VLM→VLA transitions limits the core finding (R1, R2)\nThe exit collapse finding—the paper's strongest result—rests on only two independent VLM→VLA transitions (TracVLA and NORA). While the permutation test yields p=0.008, this is across 9 cells from only 2 architectures (not independent). The three-tier gradient has N=1 at Tier 2 (only OpenVLA). Architectures outside the causal-decoder family (flow-matching, diffusion-based VLAs like π₀ or Octo) are entirely untested. A two-tier distinction (VLM vs VLA) would be better supported; the three-tier framing is speculative given N=1 at Tier 2.\n\n### W2. [Major] No closed-loop behavioral validation (R1, R2)\nAll analyses are open-loop. The paper reports a failed closed-loop attempt (SR=0/3). The correlation between ΔR² and published success rates (ρ=0.583, p=0.10, n=9) is non-significant and underpowered. The discovery that ablating 24/32 instruction-dominant heads produces d=0.65 trajectory divergence but near-zero change in ground-truth MSE (p=0.52) raises a genuine question: if removing most instruction heads doesn't increase action error, what is their functional significance? The \"direction rather than precision\" interpretation is post-hoc. For a robotics venue, this gap substantially weakens practical impact claims.\n\n### W3. [Moderate] Counterfactual metric incomparability (5/8 cross-architecture cells) (R1)\nNORA and reference VLMs use cosine distance on hidden representations while OpenVLA and TracVLA use Cohen's d on action outputs. This means the counterfactual finding cannot be uniformly evaluated across the full matrix. The paper correctly identifies this limitation and avoids cross-metric ratios, but it constrains the strength of the cross-architecture counterfactual claim. NORA Goal saturation (cos=1.0) likely reflects numerical issues rather than genuine maximal sensitivity.\n\n### W4. [Moderate] Δ>0.10 threshold is descriptive, not principled (R1)\nThe threshold is presented as a \"natural effect-size boundary\" with threshold sensitivity analysis showing any value in (0.097, 0.156) yields identical grouping. However, the gap between groups is 0.059, and 2 of 9 CIs overlap. There is no theoretical grounding for why 10% explained variance drop should be the meaningful boundary. The analysis is performed on the same data used to establish the pattern.\n\n### W5. [Moderate] Specificity control undermines the \"dedicated routing\" narrative (R1)\nInstruction-dominant head ablation (d=0.65) vs random-24 head ablation (d=0.60) yields only an 8% gap. The identified instruction-dominant heads are not substantially more important than arbitrary head selections. The random-subset analysis shows dominant-head ablation falls below all 30 random subsets for R² increase. The paper honestly acknowledges this but it substantially reduces the practical value of the instruction-dominant head classification.\n\n### W6. [Moderate] Insufficient engagement with representation transformation literature (R2)\nThe exit-collapse phenomenon has parallels to the \"logit lens\" / \"tuned lens\" literature (Belrose et al. 2023) showing intermediate layers can be more informative than final layers. The paper does not connect to the broader fine-tuning distortion literature (Merchant et al. 2020). Missing NLP probing references: Tenney et al. (2019), Jawahar et al. (2019), Rogers et al. (2020). Voita et al. (2019) on attention head pruning is directly relevant but absent.\n\n### W7. [Moderate] NORA analyses are incomplete (R1)\nOnly 2/4 LIBERO suites are available for NORA. NORA Goal has unreliable bootstrap CIs (exit-layer R²≈-0.006). NORA counterfactual is limited to cosine distance. The third architecture contributes substantially less evidence than the other two.\n\n### W8. [Moderate] NLP control design gap (R1, R2)\nThe NLP controls (chat-finetuned models) lack vision encoders—they test whether NLP finetuning of a *text-only* model produces exit collapse, not whether NLP finetuning of a *VLM* produces exit collapse. A more compelling control would be an instruction-tuned VLM (e.g., LLaVA-chat or InstructBLIP) finetuned on visual QA but not actions.\n\n### W9. [Minor] Some findings confirm rather than extend prior work (R2)\nThe L1 inherited routing finding is largely expected from Zhang et al. (2026, \"Instruction Anchor\"). The RSA-CKA dissociation is well-established in the representation similarity literature (Ding et al. 2021, Raghu et al. 2017). The counterfactual perturbation methodology is adapted from existing behavioral protocols (Fang et al. 2026, Hou et al. 2026). The paper should more explicitly distinguish confirming versus extending contributions.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Key Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Functional significance of instruction heads:** The ablation produces d=0.65 divergence with ΔMSE≈0. Can you provide success rate impact or any behavioral grounding for the \"direction rather than precision\" interpretation?\n\n2. **Multimodal NLP control:** Have you considered running analysis on instruction-tuned VLMs (e.g., LLaVA-chat) that receive visual input but have no action training? This would close the most significant control gap.\n\n3. **Permutation test independence:** The p=0.008 treats architecture-suite cells as independent, but cells from the same architecture share weights. How does this dependence affect validity?\n\n4. **OpenVLA exit collapse convergence:** Could the VLA→VLA absence of exit collapse simply reflect that OpenVLA already underwent exit collapse during OXE pretraining, leaving no further collapse to occur? This would make it a convergence statement rather than a qualitative transition-type distinction.\n\n5. **Training dynamics:** Even 2-3 intermediate checkpoints would reveal whether exit collapse is sudden or gradual. Is this feasible?\n\n6. **L1 attention reconciliation:** How do you reconcile \"L1 routing is inherited\" with the OpenVLA gradient (0→9→24 heads)? The 9→24 jump appears training-induced.\n\n7. **R²=0.155 floor in NLP controls:** Can you confirm this is not an artifact? What action labels are text-only NLP models being probed against?\n\n---\n\n## 6. Missing Experiments (Priority-Ordered)\n\n1. **Closed-loop behavioral validation** — Even simplified action-prediction accuracy on held-out demos would help. This is the single most impactful addition for CoRL.\n2. **Additional VLA architecture family** — One diffusion or flow-matching VLA (π₀, Octo) would test whether findings are specific to autoregressive/tokenized-action models.\n3. **Multimodal NLP control** — Instruction-tuned VLM without action training (LLaVA-chat, InstructBLIP).\n4. **Training dynamics** — 2-3 intermediate checkpoints to reveal exit collapse onset timing.\n5. **Second Tier-2 architecture** — Another general-purpose VLA to validate the three-tier gradient beyond N=1.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Missing Related Work\n\n- Belrose et al. (2023), \"Eliciting Latent Predictions from Transformers with the Tuned Lens\" — exit collapse parallel\n- Tenney et al. (2019), \"BERT Rediscovers the Classical NLP Pipeline\" — canonical layer-wise probing\n- Rogers et al. (2020), \"A Primer in BERTology\" — probing methodology survey\n- Voita et al. (2019), \"Analyzing Multi-Head Self-Attention\" — attention head pruning/specialization\n- Merchant et al. (2020), \"What Happens to BERT Embeddings During Fine-tuning?\" — fine-tuning distortion\n- Geva et al. (2023), \"Dissecting Recall of Factual Associations\" — layer-wise information flow\n- Ding et al. (2021), \"Grounding Representation Similarity Through Statistical Testing\" — RSA/CKA comparison methodology\n\n---\n\n## 8. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nThe paper covers the experimental design comprehensively. Cross-referencing against the 20 imagined experiments in `review_context.yaml`:\n\n| Experiment | Covered? | Notes |\n|------------|----------|-------|\n| SigLIP+Projector RSA (plan_001) | ✓ | Table 2 |\n| DINOv2 Baseline (plan_002) | ✓ | Table 3 |\n| Per-layer RSA (plan_003) | ✓ | Table 6 |\n| Output Head Analysis (plan_004) | ✓ | Table 1 |\n| 6 Methods × 2 Benchmarks (plan_005) | ✓ | Table 8 |\n| Instruction Ablation (plan_006) | ✓ | Table 10 |\n| 2×2 Matrix (plan_007) | ✓ | Table 9 |\n| Random Instruction Control (plan_008) | ✓ | Table 11 |\n| CKA Verification (plan_009) | ✓ | Table 7 |\n| LIBERO-Spatial Extension (plan_010) | ✓ | Table 24 |\n| Full-layer R² Scan (plan_011) | ✓ | Table 15 |\n| CLIP Control (plan_012) | ✓ | Table 4 |\n| Branch-Split RSA (plan_013) | ✓ | Table 5 |\n| Cross-Arch R² Collapse (cross_arch_r2_collapse) | ✓ | Table 18 |\n| Cross-Arch CF (cross_arch_cf) | ✓ | Tables 20-21 |\n| Three-Tier Gradient (three_tier_gradient) | ✓ | Table 22 |\n| NLP Control (nlp_control) | ✓ | Table 19 |\n| Cross-Arch L1 Heads (cross_arch_l1_heads) | ✓ | Table 23 |\n| Cross-Instruction Probe (cross_inst_probe) | ✓ | Table 12 |\n| Dense Attention Profile (dense_attention_profile) | ✓ | Table 14 |\n| CF Trajectory Analysis (cf_trajectory_analysis) | ✓ | Table 16 |\n| Training Trajectory (training_trajectory) | ✓ | Table 17 |\n| TracVLA Mixed Heads (tracvla_mixed_heads) | ✓ | Table 25 |\n| L1 Attention Three-Condition (l1_attention_three_cond) | ✓ | Table 13 |\n\n**Coverage: 24/24 (100%).** All imagined experiments are represented in the paper. The numerical values in the paper are consistent with the expected results in the design document.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Overall Assessment\n\n### Verdict\n\nThis paper presents a methodologically rigorous representational analysis of VLA fine-tuning with several genuine contributions: the trained-vs-reference decomposition is novel in VLA literature, the R² exit collapse finding (6/6 VLM→VLA vs 0/4 VLA→VLA) is non-obvious and well-controlled, and the statistical reporting is unusually honest. The experimental coverage of the design space is comprehensive (100% of planned experiments executed).\n\nHowever, the paper faces significant limitations for a robotics venue. The core finding rests on N=2 independent VLM→VLA transitions within a single architecture family (causal-decoder). The complete absence of closed-loop behavioral validation—combined with the counter-intuitive result that ablating 24/32 instruction heads produces near-zero MSE change—leaves the functional significance of the identified routing structures unresolved. The three-tier routing gradient is overstated given N=1 at Tier 2. Some findings (inherited L1 routing, RSA-CKA dissociation) confirm rather than extend prior knowledge.\n\nThe paper sits at the CoRL acceptance boundary. The methodology and framework design are strong contributions to VLA understanding, but the limited architectural scope, absent behavioral grounding, and primarily observational nature constrain the impact. Adding one non-autoregressive VLA architecture and any form of behavioral validation would move this decisively above the threshold.\n\n### Recommendation: **Borderline Accept** (6/10)\n\nThe paper merits publication if the authors can address the most critical gaps in revision (behavioral validation, positioning relative to representation transformation literature). In its current form, it represents solid foundational work that the VLA community would benefit from, but falls short of the impact expected at a top robotics venue.\n\n---\n\n*Review completed by 2 independent reviewers (R1: Methods & Experiments, R2: Novelty & Related Work) with full roundtable convergence (Steps 1-4).*","project":"vla-ssd","tier":"best","dream_id":"20260527T022433","overall_score":"borderline accept","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10714,"cache_read_input_tokens":85630,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10714},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1daec3f0-5eda-4fd1-9945-e0c719d586e4","uuid":"7cfb0810-8b8b-4d81-9d9d-9e93cdbb0348"}
